(window.__DP=window.__DP||[])[4]="hould time progesterone sampling to the woman's cycle length rather than automatically using calendar day 21.\",\"pr\":\"A 'day-21 progesterone' is only appropriately mid-luteal for a roughly 28-day cycle.\",\"tp\":\"Ask the patient's usual cycle length and expected next period before ordering progesterone so the sample is obtained about 7 days beforehand.\",\"cn\":\"Mid-luteal serum progesterone (day 21 of a 28-day cycle, about 7 days before menses) confirms ovulation.\",\"sl\":\"Evidence of ovulation: serum progesterone in the mid-luteal phase (day 21 of a 28-day cycle) even with regular cycles. Mid-luteal progesterone (MLP) about 7 days before expected menses above about 3 ng/mL indicates ovulatory cycles.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[14]],[\"L33_1\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F378\"},{\"id\":\"F379\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Obstetric antiphospholipid syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which obstetric complication is characteristic of antiphospholipid syndrome?\",\"o\":[\"Fetal macrosomia from hyperinsulinemia\",\"Fetal loss in the second trimester\",\"Isolated neural tube defect\",\"Placenta previa\",\"Polyhydramnios from impaired swallowing\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fetal macrosomia is driven by maternal hyperglycemia and fetal hyperinsulinemia, a growth-promoting pathway rather than placental thrombosis.\",\"Antiphospholipid syndrome can cause fetal death beyond the early first trimester through placental thrombosis, inflammation, and impaired trophoblast function.\",\"A neural tube defect is associated with folate deficiency, selected medications, and genetic factors rather than APS.\",\"Placenta previa reflects low implantation of the placenta over or near the cervix and is not a defining APS complication.\",\"Polyhydramnios from impaired fetal swallowing is related to fetal anomalies and neurologic or gastrointestinal disease rather than APS.\"],\"e\":[\"APS is an autoimmune thrombophilia associated with thrombosis and characteristic pregnancy morbidity.\",\"A second-trimester fetal loss fits the obstetric spectrum of APS, particularly when otherwise unexplained.\",\"Antiphospholipid antibodies promote placental thrombosis, complement activation, and trophoblast dysfunction, reducing placental perfusion and increasing fetal-loss and placental-disease risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Obstetric APS should be considered in recurrent pregnancy loss, unexplained fetal death, or severe placental-mediated complications.\",\"pr\":\"Classification requires a compatible clinical event plus persistent antiphospholipid antibody positivity, not an isolated transient laboratory result.\",\"tp\":\"Document gestational ages and pathology of prior losses precisely and arrange appropriate antibody testing before assigning an APS diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) causes recurrent miscarriage and fetal loss through abnormal placentation.\",\"sl\":\"Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome is found in 15% of recurrent miscarriage, acting through abnormal placentation.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F379\"},{\"id\":\"F380\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":30,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PCOS and endometrial hyperplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with oligomenorrhea and hyperandrogenic features of polycystic ovary syndrome undergoes endometrial biopsy after prolonged irregular bleeding. Which finding is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"Orderly secretory endometrium\",\"Atrophic endometrium\",\"Caseating granulomatous endometritis\",\"Proliferative endometrium with hyperplasia\",\"Decidualized endometrium of pregnancy\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An orderly secretory endometrium requires recent ovulation and progesterone exposure, which chronic oligomenorrheic anovulation makes less likely.\",\"Endometrial atrophy is typical of low-estrogen states such as later menopause rather than estrogenized anovulation.\",\"Caseating granulomatous inflammation suggests genital tuberculosis and does not arise from PCOS endocrine physiology.\",\"Chronic anovulation in PCOS produces prolonged estrogen exposure without regular luteal progesterone, favoring persistent proliferative endometrium and hyperplasia.\",\"Decidualization reflects strong progesterone exposure, usually from pregnancy or progestin therapy, rather than untreated chronic anovulation.\"],\"e\":[\"PCOS commonly causes chronic anovulation, exposing the endometrium to estrogen without predictable progesterone withdrawal.\",\"In a woman with prolonged oligomenorrhea, biopsy may show persistent proliferative change or endometrial hyperplasia.\",\"Failure to form a corpus luteum means inadequate cyclic progesterone, so estrogen-driven glandular proliferation can continue and eventually increase endometrial-neoplasia risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Endometrial protection in PCOS requires attention when oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea produces prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure.\",\"pr\":\"Regular withdrawal bleeding with cyclic progestogen or use of a levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) can protect the endometrium when pregnancy is not desired.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically how often the patient bleeds each year; prolonged amenorrhea warrants a plan for endometrial protection and evaluation of abnormal bleeding.\",\"cn\":\"Chronic anovulation in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) gives unopposed estrogen, so the endometrium is proliferative, often with hyperplasia.\",\"sl\":\"Anovulatory cycles: unopposed oestrogen gives a thick, fragile, unstable endometrium with a long-term risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. If anovulation is longstanding, endometrial biopsy should rule out hyperplasia or carcinoma.\",\"s\":[[\"L30_0\",[14]],[\"L33_1\",[6]],[\"L29_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F380\"},{\"id\":\"F381\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Indications for continuous CTG\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor, when present alone in an otherwise low-risk labor, is least likely to be an indication for continuous cardiotocography?\",\"o\":[\"Previous cesarean birth\",\"Gestational diabetes requiring medication\",\"Vaginal bleeding beyond a normal show\",\"Meconium-stained amniotic fluid requiring ongoing risk assessment\",\"In vitro fertilisation (IVF) conception alone\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A previous cesarean birth warrants continuous CTG because fetal heart-rate abnormalities can be an early sign of uterine-scar compromise.\",\"Medication-treated gestational diabetes is an antenatal maternal risk factor for which continuous CTG is offered in labor.\",\"Vaginal blood loss other than a show raises concern for placental pathology and is an indication for continuous monitoring.\",\"Meconium can indicate fetal compromise and should trigger full risk assessment with discussion of CTG, especially when other risk factors are present.\",\"IVF conception alone is not listed by National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) as an independent antenatal indication for continuous CTG when labor remains otherwise low risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Continuous CTG is reserved for labor with maternal, fetal, or intrapartum factors that raise the probability of fetal compromise.\",\"Previous cesarean birth, medication-treated diabetes, and significant bleeding are recognized indications, whereas IVF conception alone does not automatically require continuous monitoring.\",\"CTG is most useful when the pretest risk of hypoxia, placental bleeding, or uterine-scar complication is increased; indiscriminate use in low-risk labor increases intervention without equivalent benefit.\"],\"tk\":\"Risk-based intrapartum monitoring should match the woman's current maternal, fetal, and labor risk factors rather than conception method alone.\",\"pr\":\"NICE specifically lists pre-existing diabetes and gestational diabetes requiring medication among antenatal indications for continuous CTG.\",\"tp\":\"Reassess monitoring whenever new bleeding, meconium, oxytocin use, fever, hypertension, or fetal concerns develop during labor.\",\"cn\":\"Continuous cardiotocography (CTG) is indicated for medical disorders, vaginal bleeding, meconium and previous caesarean, not conception by in vitro fertilisation (IVF) alone.\",\"sl\":\"Low-risk labours use intermittent auscultation. High-risk pregnancies need continuous electronic fetal monitoring by cardiotocography (CTG): medical disorders, vaginal bleeding, meconium-stained liquor, previous caesarean section (VBAC, vaginal birth after caesarean).\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[59,62]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F381\"},{\"id\":\"F382\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"DeLancey level II support\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which DeLancey level of vaginal support anchors the midvagina laterally to the arcus tendineus fascia pelvis and is most directly associated with paravaginal anterior wall prolapse?\",\"o\":[\"Level II support\",\"Level I apical support\",\"Level III distal support\",\"Pubourethral ligament support alone\",\"Perineal body support alone\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Level II support attaches the middle vagina laterally to the arcus tendineus fascia pelvis; disruption permits anterior vaginal wall descent and cystocele.\",\"Level I support is provided largely by the uterosacral-cardinal ligament complex and suspends the cervix and upper vagina.\",\"Level III support fuses the distal vagina to the perineal membrane, perineal body, and adjacent structures rather than suspending the midvagina.\",\"Pubourethral support contributes to urethral continence but does not define the midvaginal lateral attachment described.\",\"The perineal body is important to distal posterior vaginal support and pelvic-floor integrity, not the level II lateral sidewall attachment.\"],\"e\":[\"Vaginal support is described by DeLancey levels I, II, and III, corresponding broadly to apical, midvaginal, and distal support.\",\"Lateral attachment of the midvagina to the arcus tendineus fascia pelvis is level II.\",\"Detachment of this paravaginal support allows the bladder base and anterior vaginal wall to descend, producing an anterior compartment prolapse.\"],\"tk\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse assessment should identify the failed compartment and support level rather than treating every anterior bulge as an isolated wall defect.\",\"pr\":\"The uterosacral-cardinal complex is level I, so its failure is most closely associated with apical descent rather than a pure paravaginal cystocele.\",\"tp\":\"During pelvic examination, document Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) points and assess apical support because significant apical descent can coexist with anterior prolapse.\",\"cn\":\"DeLancey level II support attaches the midvagina laterally to the arcus tendineus (white line).\",\"sl\":\"Levels of vaginal support: Level I cardinal and uterosacral ligaments; Level II arcus tendineus (white line); Level III perineal membrane and perineal body.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F382\"},{\"id\":\"F383\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Physiologic changes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about normal physiologic adaptation to pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Small Q waves or T-wave inversion may appear in lead III\",\"The elevated diaphragm may shift the cardiac axis slightly leftward\",\"Tidal volume increases while respiratory rate changes little\",\"Urinary tract dilatation may persist for several weeks postpartum\",\"Physiologic hydronephrosis is usually more marked on the left than on the right\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Minor ECG changes in lead III, including T-wave inversion or small Q waves, can occur as normal positional variants in pregnancy.\",\"The enlarging uterus elevates the diaphragm and can produce a modest leftward shift of the cardiac axis.\",\"Pregnancy increases tidal volume and minute ventilation, while respiratory rate changes relatively little in most healthy women.\",\"Pregnancy-related ureteral and collecting-system dilatation can resolve gradually rather than disappearing immediately after delivery.\",\"Pregnancy-related collecting-system dilatation is usually more pronounced on the right, partly because uterine dextrorotation increases right ureteral compression.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy produces coordinated renal, respiratory, and cardiovascular adaptations that can mimic disease.\",\"Physiologic hydronephrosis is typically right-predominant, so left-sided predominance is the false statement.\",\"Progesterone relaxes ureteral smooth muscle, while uterine dextrorotation and mechanical compression accentuate right-sided urinary-tract dilatation.\"],\"tk\":\"Physiologic hydronephrosis of pregnancy is usually right-sided or right-predominant, but symptoms still require assessment for obstruction or infection.\",\"pr\":\"Pregnancy increases minute ventilation mainly by raising tidal volume, producing a mild compensated respiratory alkalosis.\",\"tp\":\"In a pregnant patient with flank pain, check fever, urinalysis, renal function, and ultrasound rather than assuming all hydronephrosis is physiologic.\",\"cn\":\"Physiological hydronephrosis of pregnancy is more marked on the right than the left.\",\"sl\":\"Physiological hydronephrosis: dilatation of calyces, pelvis and ureters, right more than left (dextrorotated uterus; sigmoid protects the left); resolves by 6-12 weeks postpartum. Electrocardiogram (ECG): left axis shift about 15 degrees, T inversion and small Q waves in lead III.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[17,31,39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F383\"},{\"id\":\"F384\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Overactive bladder syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman reports sudden compelling urinary urgency, voiding about 12 times each day, and waking several times at night to urinate. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence\",\"Overflow incontinence\",\"Vesicovaginal fistula\",\"Nocturnal polyuria alone\",\"Overactive bladder\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Stress incontinence causes leakage with cough, exertion, or raised intra-abdominal pressure rather than compelling urgency with frequency and nocturia.\",\"Overflow incontinence is associated with incomplete emptying, chronic retention, and a high post-void residual.\",\"A vesicovaginal fistula produces continuous leakage unrelated to urgency or voluntary voiding.\",\"Nocturnal polyuria increases nighttime urine production but does not explain the marked daytime urgency and frequency described.\",\"Overactive bladder is defined by urgency, usually accompanied by frequency and nocturia, with or without urgency urinary incontinence.\"],\"e\":[\"Overactive bladder is a symptom syndrome centered on urinary urgency and commonly accompanied by frequency and nocturia.\",\"Twelve daytime voids plus nocturnal waking and sudden urgency fit an urgency-predominant storage disorder rather than stress leakage.\",\"Altered afferent signaling and/or involuntary detrusor activation can generate a compelling desire to void at lower bladder volumes.\"],\"tk\":\"Storage urinary symptoms include urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence, whereas voiding symptoms include hesitancy and poor stream.\",\"pr\":\"A 3-day bladder diary can distinguish small frequent voids from polyuria caused by excessive urine production.\",\"tp\":\"Check urinalysis, fluid and caffeine intake, medications, and a bladder diary before escalating uncomplicated overactive-bladder treatment.\",\"cn\":\"Urgency with frequency and nocturia is overactive bladder (OAB).\",\"sl\":\"Overactive bladder (OAB): urgency (sudden compelling desire to pass urine, difficult to defer), with or without urge incontinence, usually with frequency and nocturia.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[4,9]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F384\"},{\"id\":\"F385\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Urodynamic stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Urodynamic testing demonstrates involuntary urine leakage when abdominal pressure rises, without a detrusor contraction. What is the diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Detrusor overactivity incontinence\",\"Urodynamic stress urinary incontinence\",\"Mixed urinary incontinence\",\"Overflow urinary incontinence\",\"Vesicovaginal fistula\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Detrusor overactivity incontinence requires involuntary detrusor contractions during filling that coincide with urgency and leakage.\",\"Urodynamic stress incontinence is leakage with increased abdominal pressure in the absence of a detrusor contraction.\",\"Mixed incontinence combines stress and urgency components; the tracing described isolates pressure-related leakage without detrusor overactivity.\",\"Overflow incontinence results from retention and overdistension with a high residual volume, not leakage specifically provoked by abdominal pressure.\",\"A vesicovaginal fistula produces continuous or near-continuous urinary leakage and is not defined by urodynamic stress provocation.\"],\"e\":[\"Urodynamics separates pressure-provoked urethral leakage from leakage caused by involuntary bladder contraction.\",\"Leakage during increased abdominal pressure with no detrusor contraction defines urodynamic stress urinary incontinence.\",\"When bladder pressure transiently exceeds urethral closure pressure because sphincteric or pelvic-floor support is insufficient, urine escapes despite a stable detrusor.\"],\"tk\":\"Urodynamic stress incontinence is a physiologic observation and should be interpreted alongside the patient's symptom pattern.\",\"pr\":\"Detrusor overactivity is a urodynamic finding, whereas overactive bladder is a symptom syndrome and the two are not synonymous.\",\"tp\":\"Use urodynamics selectively when the result will change management, especially before invasive treatment in complex or mixed presentations.\",\"cn\":\"Urodynamic stress incontinence (USI) is leakage with raised abdominal pressure in the absence of a detrusor contraction.\",\"sl\":\"Urodynamic stress incontinence (USI): involuntary leakage of urine during increased abdominal pressure, in the absence of a detrusor contraction.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F385\"},{\"id\":\"F386\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal CMV serology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which maternal serologic pattern is most compatible with remote cytomegalovirus infection rather than recent primary infection?\",\"o\":[\"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) IgG negative and CMV IgM negative\",\"CMV IgM positive with low-avidity CMV IgG\",\"CMV IgG negative with CMV IgM positive\",\"High-avidity CMV IgG with persistent CMV IgM in the first trimester\",\"CMV IgG positive with CMV IgM negative\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Both antibodies negative means there is no serologic evidence of prior CMV exposure at the time of testing.\",\"Positive IgM with low-avidity IgG raises concern for a recent primary infection.\",\"An isolated CMV IgM result requires confirmation because it may represent very recent infection or a false-positive, not established remote immunity.\",\"High-avidity IgG generally argues against a very recent primary infection, but persistent IgM makes the pattern less straightforward than IgG-positive/IgM-negative remote exposure.\",\"Persistent CMV IgG with absent IgM is the usual pattern of remote previous exposure.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal CMV testing uses IgG, IgM, and IgG avidity when the timing of infection matters.\",\"An IgG-positive, IgM-negative pattern is most compatible with remote previous exposure.\",\"After primary infection, IgG persists and undergoes avidity maturation, while IgM usually wanes; low-avidity IgG together with IgM supports more recent infection.\"],\"tk\":\"CMV serology interpretation should distinguish prior exposure from suspected primary infection because fetal transmission risk differs by timing.\",\"pr\":\"CMV IgM has limited specificity, so IgG avidity testing can help date infection when IgM is positive.\",\"tp\":\"When recent maternal CMV is suspected, involve fetal medicine because counseling may require targeted ultrasound and appropriately timed diagnostic testing.\",\"cn\":\"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) immunoglobulin G (IgG) positive with IgM negative indicates past (remote) infection rather than recent primary infection.\",\"sl\":\"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) immunoglobulin G (IgG) has high sensitivity and specificity as a sign of past or recent infection; CMV IgM suggests recent or ongoing infection.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F386\"},{\"id\":\"F387\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Timing of birth in gestational diabetes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An otherwise uncomplicated pregnancy is affected by well-controlled gestational diabetes. If spontaneous labor has not occurred, by what gestational age should planned birth be offered under National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance?\",\"o\":[\"40+6 weeks\",\"37+6 weeks\",\"38+6 weeks\",\"39+6 weeks\",\"39+0 weeks\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"40+6 weeks is the latest gestational age by which National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) advises birth for uncomplicated gestational diabetes if spontaneous labor has not occurred.\",\"Birth at 37+6 weeks is earlier than routinely required for uncomplicated well-controlled gestational diabetes and is reserved for additional maternal or fetal indications.\",\"Birth at 38+6 weeks may be chosen for individualized complications, but uncomplicated gestational diabetes can continue later under NICE guidance.\",\"39+6 weeks is within term and may be clinically reasonable, but the pregnancy may continue later when maternal and fetal assessment remains reassuring.\",\"Routine planned birth at 39+0 weeks can be appropriate in selected circumstances, but stable uncomplicated disease does not require that exact timing under NICE guidance.\"],\"e\":[\"Timing of birth in gestational diabetes depends on glycemic control, fetal growth, and maternal or fetal complications.\",\"For uncomplicated gestational diabetes, NICE advises birth no later than 40+6 weeks.\",\"Continuing pregnancy indefinitely increases exposure to risks linked to diabetes and advancing gestation, while earlier delivery is individualized when macrosomia, hypertension, poor control, or fetal compromise changes the balance.\"],\"tk\":\"Uncomplicated gestational diabetes should be planned for birth by 40+6 weeks under NICE guidance, with earlier birth considered for complications.\",\"pr\":\"Pre-existing type 1 or type 2 diabetes has a different recommended delivery window from uncomplicated gestational diabetes.\",\"tp\":\"Review glucose control, estimated fetal size, blood pressure, fetal wellbeing, and treatment requirements before finalizing delivery timing.\",\"cn\":\"In uncomplicated gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), offer elective birth by induction or caesarean section no later than 40+6 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Women with gestational diabetes: offer elective birth by induced labour or (if indicated) caesarean section no later than 40 weeks plus 6 days; consider earlier birth if maternal or fetal complications.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F387\"},{\"id\":\"F388\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oncogenic HPV types\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which pair of high-risk human papillomavirus types accounts for the largest proportion of cervical cancers worldwide?\",\"o\":[\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) 6 and 11\",\"HPV 16 and 18\",\"HPV 31 and 33\",\"HPV 42 and 54\",\"HPV 45 and 52\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"HPV 6 and 11 are low-risk types strongly associated with anogenital warts rather than invasive cervical cancer.\",\"HPV 16 and 18 are the dominant oncogenic types and together account for most cervical cancers worldwide.\",\"HPV 31 and 33 are high-risk oncogenic types but contribute a smaller share of cervical cancers than types 16 and 18.\",\"HPV 42 and 54 are not the principal high-risk types responsible for the global cervical cancer burden.\",\"HPV 45 and 52 are oncogenic types seen in cervical neoplasia, with a smaller population-attributable fraction than the dominant pair.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV is the necessary causal pathway for nearly all cervical cancers.\",\"Types 16 and 18 account for the largest proportion of invasive cervical cancers worldwide.\",\"Viral E6 and E7 oncoproteins disable p53 and retinoblastoma tumor-suppressor pathways, allowing persistent infected cells to accumulate genomic abnormalities.\"],\"tk\":\"Cervical cancer prevention combines HPV vaccination with screening because vaccines target the highest-risk oncogenic types but do not eliminate all risk.\",\"pr\":\"HPV 6 and 11 cause most genital warts, whereas HPV 16 and 18 carry major oncogenic potential.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling patients, explain that a positive high-risk HPV test indicates infection risk, not a diagnosis of cancer, and follow the applicable screening algorithm.\",\"cn\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) 16 and 18 cause the largest share of cervical cancers, about 50% on the slide (about 70% worldwide).\",\"sl\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is the primary factor: HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 45; 50% are caused by HPV 16 and 18.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F388\"},{\"id\":\"F389\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"LNG-IUS mechanism of action\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is a principal contraceptive effect of the levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system?\",\"o\":[\"Stimulation of a luteinising hormone (LH) surge and ovulation\",\"Enhanced tubal transport of sperm and oocytes\",\"Endometrial decidualization and glandular atrophy with thickened cervical mucus\",\"Thinning of cervical mucus to facilitate sperm passage\",\"Permanent destruction of the endometrium\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The LNG-IUS does not act by stimulating ovulation; many users continue to ovulate because its dominant effects are local.\",\"Increasing tubal transport would not provide the local sperm-impairing and endometrial effects responsible for contraception.\",\"Local levonorgestrel causes endometrial decidualization and glandular atrophy while thickening cervical mucus, strongly impairing sperm passage and endometrial proliferation.\",\"Levonorgestrel thickens cervical mucus, making sperm penetration more difficult rather than facilitating passage.\",\"The endometrial suppression caused by an LNG-IUS is reversible after removal and is not an ablative destruction of tissue.\"],\"e\":[\"The LNG-IUS releases progestogen primarily within the uterus, producing potent local cervical and endometrial effects.\",\"It causes endometrial decidualization and glandular atrophy and thickens cervical mucus.\",\"Continuous local progestogenic signaling reduces endometrial proliferation and makes cervical mucus hostile to sperm penetration, while ovulation may continue in many users.\"],\"tk\":\"LNG-IUS contraception is predominantly mediated by local progestogenic effects rather than consistent systemic ovulation suppression.\",\"pr\":\"Endometrial suppression explains why the LNG-IUS also substantially reduces heavy menstrual bleeding over time.\",\"tp\":\"Before insertion, exclude pregnancy when indicated, assess sexually transmitted infection (STI) risk, and counsel about irregular bleeding during the first months of use.\",\"cn\":\"The levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) acts mainly on the endometrium (atrophy) and by thickening cervical mucus.\",\"sl\":\"Levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) acts mainly on the endometrium and cervical mucus; copper devices inhibit fertilisation by direct toxicity and cause an anti-implantation inflammatory reaction.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[43]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F389\"},{\"id\":\"F390\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal skull presenting diameters\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which fetal presentation and presenting skull diameter are correctly paired?\",\"o\":[\"Well-flexed vertex — suboccipitobregmatic diameter — approximately 11.5 cm\",\"Brow presentation — mentovertical diameter — approximately 10 cm\",\"Face presentation — submentobregmatic diameter — approximately 9.5 cm\",\"Deflexed vertex — occipitofrontal diameter — approximately 9.5 cm\",\"Face presentation — mentovertical diameter — approximately 13.5 cm\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A well-flexed vertex presents the suboccipitobregmatic diameter, but this measures about 9.5 cm, not 11.5 cm.\",\"Brow presentation exposes the mentovertical diameter, which is approximately 13.5 cm, making 10 cm incorrect.\",\"In a fully extended face presentation, the engaging submentobregmatic diameter measures about 9.5 cm.\",\"A deflexed vertex presents the occipitofrontal diameter at about 11.5 cm, not 9.5 cm.\",\"The mentovertical diameter characterizes brow presentation, not fully extended face presentation.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal head flexion and extension determine the anteroposterior diameter that must traverse the maternal pelvis.\",\"A fully extended face presents the submentobregmatic diameter of about 9.5 cm.\",\"Partial extension into brow exposes the much larger mentovertical diameter, explaining why persistent brow presentation is usually mechanically unfavorable for vaginal birth.\"],\"tk\":\"Presentation mechanics are determined by fetal head attitude because flexion or extension changes the diameter entering the pelvis.\",\"pr\":\"The well-flexed vertex also presents a 9.5-cm suboccipitobregmatic diameter, but through a different pair of landmarks.\",\"tp\":\"During vaginal examination, identify sutures and fontanelles for vertex presentations and facial landmarks carefully before diagnosing face or brow.\",\"cn\":\"In face presentation the fully extended head presents the submentobregmatic diameter, about 9.5 cm.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F390\"},{\"id\":\"F391\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":25,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Antiphospholipid antibody laboratory criteria\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about antiphospholipid-antibody laboratory classification is false?\",\"o\":[\"Persistent antiphospholipid-antibody positivity remains important for classification\",\"Anticardiolipin antibodies are measured by solid-phase immunoassay\",\"Anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies form a separate laboratory domain from lupus anticoagulant\",\"Modern ACR/European Alliance of Associations for Rheumatology (EULAR) classification combines clinical and laboratory domains\",\"Lupus anticoagulant is classified by a moderate-to-high antibody titer\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Persistence of antiphospholipid antibodies, traditionally demonstrated on repeated testing at least 12 weeks apart, reduces misclassification from transient positivity.\",\"Anticardiolipin antibodies are measured by solid-phase immunoassay and are stratified using a quantitative antibody level.\",\"Anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies are solid-phase antibody tests and occupy a laboratory category distinct from lupus-anticoagulant functional testing.\",\"The 2023 ACR/European Alliance of Associations for Rheumatology (EULAR) classification criteria require an entry clinical and laboratory relationship and then weighted clinical and laboratory domain scores.\",\"Lupus anticoagulant is identified with phospholipid-dependent functional coagulation assays; moderate or high titer terminology applies to solid-phase anticardiolipin or anti-β2-glycoprotein I assays.\"],\"e\":[\"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) classification uses evidence of antiphospholipid antibodies together with compatible clinical events.\",\"Lupus anticoagulant is a functional assay phenomenon, so describing it as a moderate-to-high antibody titer is incorrect.\",\"Phospholipid-dependent clotting tests detect lupus-anticoagulant activity, whereas anticardiolipin and anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies are quantified with solid-phase immunoassays.\"],\"tk\":\"APS laboratory interpretation must distinguish functional lupus-anticoagulant testing from quantitative anticardiolipin and anti-β2-glycoprotein I assays.\",\"pr\":\"The 2023 ACR/EULAR criteria are classification criteria for research cohorts, not a substitute for individualized clinical diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"Repeat and interpret antiphospholipid testing with attention to anticoagulant therapy, acute illness, assay method, and the required interval for persistent positivity.\",\"cn\":\"Lupus anticoagulant is a functional clotting assay result, not an antibody titre; anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I are measured by immunoassay.\",\"sl\":\"Antiphospholipid testing listed among selective maternal tests: lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I (anti-β2GP1) antibodies.\",\"s\":[[\"L25_0\",[43]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F391\"},{\"id\":\"F392\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Progression of untreated syphilis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient had a painless genital ulcer that persisted for several weeks and then healed spontaneously without treatment. Which clinical stage of untreated syphilis typically follows next?\",\"o\":[\"Secondary syphilis\",\"Tertiary syphilis\",\"Late latent syphilis\",\"Neurosyphilis\",\"Chancroid\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A resolving painless chancre is primary syphilis; untreated infection usually progresses to secondary syphilis after hematogenous dissemination.\",\"Tertiary syphilis develops years after untreated infection and can involve cardiovascular, neurologic, or gummatous disease rather than following immediately after the chancre.\",\"Late latent syphilis is an asymptomatic phase occurring after earlier infection and is not the usual next symptomatic stage after primary disease.\",\"Neurosyphilis can occur at different stages and is not the obligatory next clinical phase after a primary chancre.\",\"Chancroid is a different sexually transmitted infection caused by Haemophilus ducreyi and classically produces painful genital ulcers.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary syphilis classically causes a painless chancre that can heal even though Treponema pallidum remains in the body.\",\"Untreated infection commonly progresses next to secondary syphilis, with systemic manifestations after dissemination.\",\"Hematogenous spread produces widespread treponemal infection, leading to findings such as diffuse rash, mucous patches, generalized lymphadenopathy, and condyloma lata.\"],\"tk\":\"Spontaneous healing of a syphilitic chancre does not indicate cure; testing and stage-appropriate treatment remain necessary.\",\"pr\":\"Secondary syphilis can include a rash involving the palms and soles, a useful clue in a patient whose genital ulcer has already disappeared.\",\"tp\":\"When syphilis is suspected, test for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) and other sexually transmitted infections (STIs), arrange partner services, and give guideline-concordant penicillin therapy according to stage.\",\"cn\":\"An untreated primary syphilitic chancre heals in 3-8 weeks and may be followed by secondary syphilis.\",\"sl\":\"Primary chancre is single, painless, indurated and resolves over 3-8 weeks. If primary syphilis is untreated, 25% develop secondary syphilis 4-10 weeks after the chancre (rash, condylomata lata, multisystem disease).\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[43,44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F392\"},{\"id\":\"F393\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Varicella infectivity in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the infectious period of primary varicella is correct?\",\"o\":[\"The patient becomes infectious only after the vesicular rash is established\",\"Infectivity ends as soon as the first lesions begin to crust\",\"The patient is infectious only during the febrile prodrome\",\"Visible vesicles must be present before person-to-person transmission can occur\",\"The patient is infectious from about 1–2 days before rash onset until all lesions have crusted\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Varicella shedding begins before the exanthem, so waiting for established vesicles misses part of the contagious period.\",\"A patient remains contagious while uncrusted lesions are present; crusting of only the first lesions does not end transmission risk.\",\"The febrile prodrome may precede the rash, but contagiousness continues through the vesicular phase rather than being confined to prodromal fever.\",\"VZV transmission can occur before visible skin lesions, which is why exposure assessment must include the 1–2 days preceding rash onset.\",\"Primary varicella is contagious from roughly 1–2 days before the rash until all lesions have crusted, reflecting presymptomatic viral shedding and continued shedding from active lesions.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary varicella is highly contagious, and transmission can occur before the characteristic vesicular eruption becomes visible.\",\"The clinically important timing is that a person is infectious approximately 1–2 days before rash onset and remains infectious while active lesions are present.\",\"Respiratory and lesion-associated viral shedding precedes clinical recognition, so susceptible pregnant contacts may have meaningful exposure before the index case is diagnosed.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that varicella infectivity begins before the rash and continues until lesions crust, which determines exposure windows in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Maternal primary varicella can cause severe pneumonia, and fetal risk depends strongly on gestational timing of maternal infection.\",\"tp\":\"When a pregnant patient reports varicella exposure, establish evidence of immunity promptly and separate susceptible patients from active cases during assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Varicella is infectious from about 48 hours before the rash appears until all vesicles have crusted over (typically 5 days).\",\"sl\":\"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) incubation period is 7-21 days; a person is infectious 48 hours before the rash appears and continues to be infectious until the vesicles crust over, typically 5 days.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F393\"},{\"id\":\"F394\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Chlamydia trachomatis — NAAT diagnosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which test is preferred for detecting uncomplicated urogenital Chlamydia trachomatis infection?\",\"o\":[\"Gram stain of endocervical discharge\",\"Nucleic acid amplification test\",\"Saline wet-mount microscopy\",\"Vaginal pH measurement\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) measurement\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Gram staining does not reliably identify Chlamydia trachomatis, an obligate intracellular organism, and lacks the sensitivity needed for routine urogenital diagnosis.\",\"Nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) is the most sensitive recommended routine method and detects chlamydial DNA or RNA in vaginal, cervical, or appropriate urine specimens.\",\"A saline wet mount can identify motile trichomonads or clue cells, but it cannot provide nucleic-acid detection of C. trachomatis.\",\"Vaginal pH measurement helps distinguish vaginitis syndromes; a value above 4.5 may accompany bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis but does not diagnose chlamydial cervicitis.\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) is used selectively in adnexal-mass evaluation and can rise with benign conditions; it has no organism-detection function for chlamydial infection.\"],\"e\":[\"Chlamydia trachomatis is commonly asymptomatic, so a sensitive organism-directed assay is essential when screening or evaluating suspected infection.\",\"For urogenital disease, NAAT is the recommended and most sensitive routine test, using vaginal/cervical swabs or suitable urine specimens.\",\"Amplification of chlamydial DNA or RNA allows detection of low organism burden that microscopy cannot reliably identify.\"],\"tk\":\"Use NAAT as the preferred diagnostic test for urogenital Chlamydia trachomatis infection.\",\"pr\":\"In pregnancy, a documented chlamydial infection requires a test of cure about 4 weeks after treatment because persistent infection can affect both mother and neonate.\",\"tp\":\"After a positive result, arrange partner management and repeat testing after treatment according to sexually transmitted infection (STI) guidance to reduce reinfection and pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) risk.\",\"cn\":\"Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is the test of choice for genital Chlamydia trachomatis infection.\",\"sl\":\"The current standard of care for all cases is nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT): vulvovaginal swabs are the specimen of choice in women, then endocervical swabs, first-catch urine, urethral swabs.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F394\"},{\"id\":\"F395\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pulmonary embolism imaging — maternal breast radiation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman requires thoracic imaging for suspected pulmonary embolism and has a history of breast cancer. Which statement about radiation exposure is most accurate?\",\"o\":[\"V/Q scanning generally gives a higher maternal breast dose than CTPA\",\"Both V/Q scanning and CTPA are contraindicated because fetal radiation exceeds teratogenic thresholds\",\"CTPA consistently gives a tenfold higher fetal dose than V/Q scanning\",\"V/Q scanning generally gives a lower maternal breast dose than CTPA\",\"MR pulmonary angiography is the routine first-line replacement for both tests solely because it uses no ionizing radiation\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Lung scintigraphy generally exposes the maternal breast to less radiation than CTPA; modern computed tomography (CT) dose reduction narrows but does not reverse that usual breast-dose relationship.\",\"Fetal doses from appropriately performed V/Q scanning and CTPA are far below teratogenic thresholds, so clinically indicated imaging should not be withheld on this basis.\",\"Published fetal-dose ranges for CTPA and V/Q scanning overlap; a fixed tenfold fetal-dose excess from CTPA is not a valid general rule.\",\"V/Q scanning usually produces a lower maternal breast dose than CTPA, an exposure consideration when the chest radiograph and local imaging pathway make scintigraphy suitable.\",\"MR pulmonary angiography is not the standard first-line test for suspected PE in pregnancy because diagnostic performance and availability are less established than validated V/Q or CT pathways.\"],\"e\":[\"Suspected pulmonary embolism in pregnancy requires objective diagnosis because untreated PE carries substantial maternal risk.\",\"When maternal breast exposure is the specific concern, V/Q scanning generally delivers a lower breast dose than CTPA, although both tests can be appropriate depending on chest radiography and local expertise.\",\"CTPA directly irradiates thoracic tissues, whereas lung scintigraphy distributes a different low-dose radiation burden; fetal doses from both techniques remain low with modern protocols.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose PE imaging by diagnostic suitability, chest radiograph findings, local expertise, and maternal radiation profile rather than avoiding necessary imaging.\",\"pr\":\"A normal chest radiograph increases the likelihood that lung scintigraphy will yield a diagnostic result, whereas abnormal lung findings may favor CTPA.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay definitive imaging when PE remains clinically suspected; first use compression ultrasonography when symptoms suggest deep vein thrombosis (DVT), because proving DVT can establish venous thromboembolism without chest imaging.\",\"cn\":\"Ventilation/perfusion (V/Q) scanning gives a lower maternal breast radiation dose than computed tomography pulmonary angiography (CTPA).\",\"sl\":\"Investigations for pulmonary embolism (PE): electrocardiogram (ECG), chest radiograph, computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) and ventilation/perfusion (V/Q) scan; suspected PE: chest X-ray (CXR) then V/Q or CTPA. The slides do not compare breast radiation doses.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[14]],[\"L22_0\",[28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F395\"},{\"id\":\"F396\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Normal menstrual cycle frequency\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An otherwise healthy adult woman has regular menstrual cycles every 35 days. How should this cycle frequency be classified?\",\"o\":[\"Within the normal adult range\",\"Oligomenorrhea\",\"Frequent menstrual bleeding\",\"Primary amenorrhea\",\"Secondary amenorrhea\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A 35-day cycle lies within the accepted adult menstrual-cycle range; normal cycles do not need to occur every 28 days.\",\"Oligomenorrhea or infrequent menses implies cycle intervals beyond the normal adult frequency range or too few cycles per year; 35 days can still be physiologic.\",\"Frequent menstrual bleeding refers to abnormally short intervals, typically less than about 24 days in modern terminology, not a 35-day cycle.\",\"Primary amenorrhea concerns failure to establish menarche by expected developmental criteria; ongoing regular cycles exclude that presentation.\",\"Secondary amenorrhea requires cessation of previously established menses for a clinically significant interval; continuing 35-day cycles do not meet that definition.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal menstruation has physiologic variation in cycle length rather than a universal 28-day interval.\",\"A regular 35-day cycle remains within the normal adult range, so the patient should not be labeled with oligomenorrhea from this interval alone.\",\"Most cycle-length variability arises from the follicular phase, while luteal-phase duration is comparatively less variable.\"],\"tk\":\"Classify a regular 35-day menstrual cycle as normal adult cycle frequency in the absence of other abnormalities.\",\"pr\":\"A cycle history should include regularity, duration, and volume of bleeding, because frequency alone does not characterize abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing a reported cycle change, document the actual first day of consecutive menses rather than relying on the patient's estimate of a 'monthly' pattern.\",\"cn\":\"A regular 35-day cycle lies within the normal adult frequency range of 24-38 days.\",\"sl\":\"Normal frequency of menses is 24-38 days (mean 28 plus or minus 7 days); infrequent is over 38 days.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[3,5]],[\"L33_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F396\"},{\"id\":\"F397\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Menopausal HRT — cognition and dementia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 62-year-old woman who is 12 years beyond menopause asks to start systemic hormone replacement therapy solely to improve memory and prevent dementia. What is the most appropriate advice?\",\"o\":[\"Strongly recommend systemic hormone replacement therapy (HRT) because late initiation reliably improves memory\",\"Start estrogen therapy because HRT is established primary prevention for dementia\",\"Do not start HRT solely for cognitive enhancement or dementia prevention\",\"Explain that HRT reverses established age-related cognitive decline after 10 years of menopause\",\"Prescribe HRT primarily as dementia prophylaxis because she is older than 60\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Systemic HRT has not demonstrated reliable cognitive enhancement when initiated late after menopause, so memory complaints alone are not an indication for treatment.\",\"Current menopause guidance states that HRT should not be offered for dementia prevention; estrogen is prescribed for appropriate menopausal indications after individualized risk assessment.\",\"HRT should not be initiated solely to improve cognition or prevent dementia, and this woman is also more than 10 years beyond menopause, when treatment initiation generally has a less favorable risk-benefit profile.\",\"Hormone therapy does not reverse established cognitive aging, and late postmenopausal initiation has not shown the claimed memory benefit.\",\"Age above 60 does not create a dementia-prevention indication; HRT is not a prophylactic cognitive therapy and should be considered only for recognized menopausal indications.\"],\"e\":[\"Menopausal hormone therapy is primarily used for appropriate menopause-related symptoms and selected bone-health indications, not as a cognitive drug.\",\"This woman seeks treatment solely for memory and dementia prevention at age 62, 12 years after menopause, so starting systemic HRT for that purpose is not recommended.\",\"Estrogen effects on vascular, thrombotic, and neural pathways depend on timing and patient risk, and clinical trials do not support late initiation as a strategy to restore cognition.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not prescribe systemic HRT solely to prevent dementia or improve memory.\",\"pr\":\"New cognitive symptoms after menopause still warrant assessment for depression, sleep disorders, thyroid disease, medication effects, and vascular risk factors.\",\"tp\":\"If HRT is being considered for genuine vasomotor or other menopausal symptoms, document uterine status, venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk, cardiovascular risk, breast history, and time since menopause before choosing a regimen.\",\"cn\":\"Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) should not be started solely to prevent dementia or improve memory.\",\"sl\":\"Dementia is listed as unclear whether directly associated with falling estrogen, and dementia and cognition are listed among the uncertainties concerning hormone replacement therapy (HRT).\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[18,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F397\"},{\"id\":\"F398\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Twin pregnancy — maternal cardiovascular adaptation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which normal maternal physiologic adaptation is typically exaggerated in a twin pregnancy compared with a singleton pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Increase in cardiac output\",\"Decrease in plasma volume\",\"Decrease in glomerular filtration rate\",\"Reduction in uterine blood flow\",\"Decrease in maternal oxygen consumption\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Twin gestation produces a greater increase in cardiac output because the larger fetoplacental mass increases preload and uteroplacental perfusion demand.\",\"Maternal plasma volume expands rather than decreases in pregnancy and the expansion is generally greater with multifetal gestation.\",\"Pregnancy causes an increase in renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR), not a reduction, as systemic and renal vascular resistance fall.\",\"Uterine blood flow rises substantially to perfuse the fetoplacental unit and is not physiologically reduced in twin pregnancy.\",\"Maternal oxygen consumption increases with pregnancy and is further influenced by the greater metabolic demands of multifetal gestation.\"],\"e\":[\"Multifetal gestation amplifies several normal maternal adaptations because fetoplacental metabolic demand is greater than in singleton pregnancy.\",\"Among the listed changes, the normal rise in maternal cardiac output is exaggerated in twins, alongside greater blood-volume expansion.\",\"A larger placental and fetal mass increases preload, uteroplacental perfusion requirements, and metabolic demand, driving the enhanced cardiovascular response.\"],\"tk\":\"Expect greater cardiovascular adaptation, including a larger rise in cardiac output, in twin pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Because twin pregnancy increases circulating-volume demands, iron-deficiency anemia and hypertensive disorders require particularly attentive antenatal surveillance.\",\"tp\":\"Do not attribute marked dyspnea, resting tachycardia, or orthopnea automatically to twin physiology; assess for anemia, pulmonary edema, thromboembolism, and cardiac disease when symptoms are disproportionate.\",\"cn\":\"Twin pregnancy causes a greater rise in plasma volume and cardiac output than singleton pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal risks in twins: greater rise in plasma volume and cardiac output than singletons; more dilutional anaemia; higher risk of decompensation with maternal heart disease.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F398\"},{\"id\":\"F399\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"RhD alloimmunization — sensitizing events\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which event is not considered a potentially sensitizing event for rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization?\",\"o\":[\"Chorionic villus sampling\",\"Amniocentesis\",\"Pregnancy termination after 12 weeks\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound\",\"External cephalic version\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Chorionic villus sampling disrupts placental tissue and can produce fetomaternal hemorrhage, making it a potentially sensitizing event.\",\"Amniocentesis is an invasive intrauterine procedure that can introduce fetal RhD-positive erythrocytes into maternal blood.\",\"A pregnancy termination after 12 weeks can cause fetomaternal blood mixing and is treated as a potentially sensitizing event in an unsensitized RhD-negative patient.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasonography does not breach the fetal or placental circulation and is not considered an RhD-sensitizing event.\",\"External cephalic version can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage and is recognized as a potentially sensitizing event in an unsensitized RhD-negative patient.\"],\"e\":[\"Rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization requires exposure of an RhD-negative mother to RhD-positive fetal erythrocytes, allowing formation of maternal anti-D antibodies.\",\"Invasive procedures and events that can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage are potentially sensitizing, whereas transvaginal ultrasound is noninvasive to the fetal circulation.\",\"Once maternal IgG anti-D forms, it can cross the placenta in a later RhD-positive pregnancy and cause fetal red-cell hemolysis.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish fetomaternal-hemorrhage events from noninvasive imaging when deciding whether an event is RhD sensitizing.\",\"pr\":\"Anti-D prophylaxis prevents new sensitization but does not treat a patient who has already formed immune anti-D antibodies.\",\"tp\":\"Record maternal RhD type early and review anti-D need after bleeding, invasive fetal procedures, pregnancy loss, abdominal trauma, or external cephalic version according to current local guidance.\",\"cn\":\"Potentially sensitizing events needing anti-D include invasive procedures, termination, external cephalic version and trauma; transvaginal ultrasound is not one.\",\"sl\":\"Rhesus immunoglobulin (RhIG) is given for blunt abdominal trauma, early pregnancy complications and obstetric procedures, routinely at 28 and 34 weeks (or once at 28 to 30 weeks), and after delivery if the baby is Rh positive.\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F399\"},{\"id\":\"F400\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Migraine with aura — contraceptive eligibility\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which contraceptive method is appropriate for a woman with migraine with aura?\",\"o\":[\"Combined hormonal transdermal patch\",\"Combined hormonal vaginal ring\",\"Progestogen-only pill\",\"Low-dose ethinyl-estradiol combined oral contraceptive\",\"Standard combined oral contraceptive pill\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A combined transdermal patch contains estrogen, so it carries the same unacceptable-risk classification as other combined hormonal contraception in migraine with aura.\",\"A combined vaginal ring contains estrogen and is avoided in migraine with aura because the concern is estrogen exposure rather than the route of administration.\",\"A progestogen-only pill is acceptable in migraine with aura and avoids the estrogen-associated ischemic stroke risk that restricts combined hormonal methods.\",\"Reducing the ethinyl-estradiol dose does not remove the combined-hormonal contraindication in migraine with aura under contraceptive eligibility criteria.\",\"A combined oral contraceptive contains estrogen and is classified as an unacceptable health risk in a patient with migraine with aura.\"],\"e\":[\"Migraine with aura changes contraceptive eligibility because estrogen-containing combined hormonal methods increase ischemic stroke risk in this population.\",\"The suitable listed method is a progestogen-only pill, while the pill, patch, and ring formulations containing estrogen should be avoided.\",\"Estrogen alters coagulation and vascular risk, adding to the baseline cerebrovascular risk associated with aura; progestogen-only methods do not carry the same restriction.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose progestogen-only or suitable non-estrogen contraception for migraine with aura and avoid combined hormonal contraception.\",\"pr\":\"Ask specifically about focal neurologic aura symptoms; a patient may report 'migraine' without recognizing visual, sensory, or speech aura as relevant.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing estrogen-containing contraception, document aura history, smoking, blood pressure, and vascular risk, and reassess new or changing headaches.\",\"cn\":\"Migraine with aura is UK Medical Eligibility Criteria (UKMEC) category 4 for combined hormonal contraception; progestogen-only methods are suitable.\",\"sl\":\"Migraine: risk of ischaemic stroke increases with aura, UK Medical Eligibility Criteria (UKMEC) 4; focal migraine with aura is an absolute contraindication to combined oral contraceptives (COC). Progestogen-only methods are indicated where oestrogen is contraindicated, e.g. migraine with aura.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[20,23,38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F400\"},{\"id\":\"F401\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Unopposed estrogen — endometrial hyperplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman with an intact uterus has been taking unopposed systemic estrogen and develops vaginal bleeding. Which endometrial effect of this regimen most directly explains the bleeding?\",\"o\":[\"Atrophic vaginitis\",\"Endometrial polyp unrelated to hormone exposure\",\"Cervical ectropion\",\"Endometrial atrophy from profound hypoestrogenism\",\"Endometrial hyperplasia\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Atrophic vaginitis can cause spotting in a hypoestrogenic vagina, but systemic estrogen exposure in this patient instead drives endometrial proliferation.\",\"An endometrial polyp can cause focal bleeding, but unopposed estrogen preferentially produces diffuse proliferative stimulation rather than explaining a hormone-independent focal lesion.\",\"Cervical ectropion arises from the cervix and is associated with estrogenized reproductive states; the relevant pathology here is endometrial proliferation in an intact uterus.\",\"Endometrial atrophy is linked to estrogen deficiency, whereas this patient's unopposed systemic estrogen stimulates glands and stroma.\",\"In a patient with an intact uterus, unopposed systemic estrogen stimulates endometrial proliferation and can cause hyperplasia, producing abnormal bleeding and increasing neoplastic risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Systemic estrogen given to a patient with a uterus requires attention to endometrial protection, because persistent estrogenic stimulation drives proliferation.\",\"The added source clarification makes the mechanism specific: unopposed systemic estrogen can produce endometrial hyperplasia and bleeding.\",\"Without adequate progestogenic opposition, endometrial glands continue proliferating, increasing the risk of hyperplasia and, depending on type and duration, endometrial carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Protect an intact uterus from unopposed systemic estrogen with an appropriate progestogen regimen unless a specialist indication dictates otherwise.\",\"pr\":\"Any postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation even when HRT is a plausible explanation, because malignancy cannot be excluded from symptoms alone.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify the exact HRT preparation, adherence, uterine status, and bleeding pattern; follow the current unscheduled-bleeding pathway rather than simply stopping or changing hormones empirically.\",\"cn\":\"Unopposed systemic oestrogen in a woman with a uterus causes endometrial hyperplasia, presenting with bleeding; progestogen is protective.\",\"sl\":\"Risks of hormone replacement therapy (HRT): increased risk of endometrial cancer with unopposed oestrogen. Unopposed estrogen relationship is well documented; addition of progestin appears to be protective.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[31]],[\"L42_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F401\"},{\"id\":\"F402\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal growth restriction — neonatal outcome determinants\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which listed factor has the strongest direct influence on neonatal survival and major morbidity in fetal growth restriction?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal age alone\",\"Gestational age at delivery\",\"Fetal sex alone\",\"Maternal parity alone\",\"Amniotic fluid index alone\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal age modifies obstetric risk but does not directly determine the infant's degree of prematurity at birth, a major driver of FGR outcome.\",\"Gestational age at delivery strongly determines neonatal survival and morbidity because earlier birth adds respiratory, neurologic, and gastrointestinal complications of prematurity to placental insufficiency.\",\"Fetal sex can modestly modify population-level outcomes, but it does not outweigh gestational maturity and placental function in an individual FGR pregnancy.\",\"Maternal parity influences some obstetric risks but has no direct effect comparable with neonatal organ maturity at delivery.\",\"The amniotic fluid index contributes to surveillance, but outcome depends on gestational age, Doppler status, growth trajectory, and fetal condition rather than amniotic fluid index (AFI) alone.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal growth restriction reflects failure to achieve biologic growth potential, often because placental function cannot meet fetal demands.\",\"Among the listed factors, gestational age at delivery has the strongest direct effect on neonatal outcome, because earlier birth adds the major morbidity of prematurity.\",\"Clinical timing balances two competing hazards: ongoing placental insufficiency and hypoxia versus iatrogenic prematurity, using Dopplers and surveillance to decide when delivery becomes safer than remaining in utero.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize gestational age at delivery as a major determinant of neonatal outcome in fetal growth restriction.\",\"pr\":\"Umbilical-artery Doppler deterioration, especially absent or reversed end-diastolic flow, identifies higher placental resistance and changes surveillance and delivery planning.\",\"tp\":\"Do not time delivery from estimated fetal weight alone; integrate growth trend, umbilical-artery Doppler, amniotic fluid, fetal testing, and gestational age.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational age at delivery is the strongest determinant of neonatal survival and major morbidity in fetal growth restriction (FGR).\",\"sl\":\"Management balances stillbirth risk in utero against prematurity after birth; there is no in-utero treatment, so management is surveillance plus well-timed birth.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F402\"},{\"id\":\"F403\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"GnRH agonist add-back therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Why is add-back hormone therapy used during prolonged gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist treatment for estrogen-dependent gynecologic disease?\",\"o\":[\"To stimulate ovulation during pituitary suppression\",\"To enlarge estrogen-dependent lesions before surgery\",\"To reduce bone mineral loss and vasomotor symptoms\",\"To increase menstrual blood loss as a marker of treatment response\",\"To restore full physiologic estrogen levels and reverse pituitary downregulation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"After sustained therapy, GnRH agonists downregulate pituitary gonadotropin secretion; add-back therapy is not intended to restore ovulation.\",\"GnRH agonists are used to suppress estrogen-dependent disease such as endometriosis, so promoting lesion growth would oppose the therapeutic objective.\",\"Low-dose add-back therapy reduces bone mineral loss and vasomotor symptoms caused by treatment-induced hypoestrogenism while preserving useful disease suppression.\",\"GnRH agonist therapy generally reduces or suppresses menstruation; provoking additional menstrual blood loss is not a monitoring strategy.\",\"Add-back regimens use limited hormone replacement, not full physiologic estrogen restoration, because excessive estrogen could undermine suppression of estrogen-dependent pathology.\"],\"e\":[\"Sustained GnRH agonist exposure causes pituitary receptor downregulation, lowering luteinising hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and producing a marked hypoestrogenic state.\",\"Add-back therapy is used to limit bone mineral density loss and vasomotor symptoms during prolonged treatment.\",\"A carefully selected low hormone dose reduces estrogen-deficiency toxicity without restoring enough ovarian stimulation to negate the therapeutic suppression of endometriosis or related disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Use add-back therapy to protect bone and improve tolerability during prolonged GnRH agonist treatment.\",\"pr\":\"Without add-back protection, prolonged hypoestrogenism can cause hot flushes, vaginal dryness, and accelerated bone loss, limiting treatment duration.\",\"tp\":\"Before prolonged GnRH agonist therapy, review baseline bone-risk factors, contraception needs, expected bleeding changes, and add-back adherence.\",\"cn\":\"Add-back hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists reduces bone loss and hypo-oestrogenic vasomotor symptoms.\",\"sl\":\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists plus or minus add-back hormone replacement therapy (HRT). Disadvantages: expensive, limited duration, marked hypo-oestrogenic side effects and osteoporosis.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[27,31]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F403\"},{\"id\":\"F404\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Cause\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage — causes and timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about secondary postpartum hemorrhage is false?\",\"o\":[\"Retained products of conception are a recognized cause\",\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage occurs from more than 24 hours to 12 weeks after birth\",\"Endometritis can cause delayed bleeding with uterine tenderness or fever\",\"Initial assessment includes maternal hemodynamic status and the severity of ongoing bleeding\",\"Subinvolution of the placental implantation site is not a recognized cause\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Retained placental tissue can prevent normal involution and cause persistent or recurrent secondary postpartum bleeding.\",\"Current Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) patient guidance defines secondary PPH as abnormal or heavy vaginal bleeding between 24 hours and 12 weeks after birth.\",\"Endometritis is a recognized cause and may add fever, uterine tenderness, or offensive lochia to delayed postpartum bleeding.\",\"Any significant delayed hemorrhage requires prompt assessment of hemodynamic stability and ongoing blood loss before etiologic investigation is completed.\",\"This statement is false because subinvolution of the placental implantation site is a recognized cause of secondary postpartum hemorrhage, through failure of normal closure and involution of placental-bed vessels.\"],\"e\":[\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage is abnormal or heavy bleeding occurring after the first 24 hours and up to 12 weeks postpartum in current RCOG material.\",\"The false statement denies placental-site subinvolution: retained tissue, infection, and placental-site subinvolution are recognized causes.\",\"With subinvolution, placental-bed vessels fail to regress and seal normally, permitting recurrent or delayed uterine bleeding after delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate secondary PPH for retained tissue, endometritis, and placental-site subinvolution while simultaneously assessing maternal stability.\",\"pr\":\"Fever, uterine tenderness, and offensive lochia raise concern for postpartum endometritis, whereas brisk bleeding can occur even without infection.\",\"tp\":\"In a patient with delayed heavy bleeding, obtain intravenous (IV) access and assess vital signs, blood loss, hemoglobin, uterine findings, and need for imaging or escalation before assuming the cause is benign.\",\"cn\":\"Secondary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), from 24 hours to 12 weeks, is caused by endometritis, retained tissue or subinvolution of the placental site.\",\"sl\":\"Secondary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) is abnormal or excessive bleeding from the birth canal between 24 hours and 12 weeks postnatally. Causes: endometritis, retained placental tissue, subinvolution of placental site, pseudo-aneurysms and arteriovenous malformations (rare).\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[24]],[\"L26_0\",[33]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F404\"},{\"id\":\"F405\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Intrauterine transfusion for fetal anemia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement regarding intrauterine fetal red-cell transfusion is false?\",\"o\":[\"Donor packed red cells are selected to be compatible with maternal serum\",\"It can be performed before 12 weeks of gestation\",\"The procedure is performed under continuous ultrasound guidance\",\"A concentrated red-cell product with a hematocrit around 70–80% limits transfusion volume\",\"Donor red cells should lack the antigen targeted by the clinically significant maternal antibody\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"For alloimmune fetal anemia, donor cells are cross-match compatible with maternal serum so circulating maternal antibodies do not rapidly destroy transfused erythrocytes.\",\"This is false: therapeutic fetal intravascular transfusion is not performed before 12 weeks, when safe fetal vascular access and meaningful intravascular treatment are not feasible.\",\"Intravascular fetal transfusion is an ultrasound-guided procedure, commonly accessing the umbilical vein to place the needle and monitor the fetus.\",\"A high-hematocrit packed-cell product corrects anemia with less infused volume, reducing the risk of fetal circulatory overload.\",\"When maternal alloantibodies cause anemia, transfused cells are chosen negative for the corresponding target antigen, preventing antibody-mediated destruction of the donor erythrocytes.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrauterine transfusion treats severe fetal anemia, most often when alloimmunization has produced clinically important red-cell destruction.\",\"The false recalled statement is the claim that transfusion can be done before 12 weeks; the procedure requires later-gestation ultrasound-guided access to the fetal circulation.\",\"Concentrated, compatible, antigen-negative donor erythrocytes raise fetal oxygen-carrying capacity while minimizing the volume infused into a compromised circulation.\"],\"tk\":\"Use ultrasound-guided intrauterine transfusion with appropriately selected concentrated red cells for severe fetal anemia when specialist criteria are met.\",\"pr\":\"An elevated middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity is a noninvasive marker of fetal anemia and helps identify fetuses needing further assessment.\",\"tp\":\"Coordinate fetal medicine and transfusion services early because donor blood requires maternal cross-matching, relevant antigen negativity, irradiation, and specialized preparation.\",\"cn\":\"Intrauterine transfusion is done under ultrasound guidance with antigen-negative, maternally compatible, high-haematocrit red cells, and not before 12 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Intrauterine transfusion is intravascular (umbilical, intrahepatic, intracardiac) or intraperitoneal; intraperitoneal is used in non-hydropic fetuses and at gestational ages earlier than 22 weeks, being absorbed over 7 to 10 days.\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F405\"},{\"id\":\"F406\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":25,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Term intrauterine fetal death — mode of delivery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A hemodynamically stable woman at 39 weeks has confirmed intrauterine fetal death. There is no maternal indication for operative delivery. What is the most appropriate delivery plan?\",\"o\":[\"Cesarean delivery solely because the fetus has died\",\"Immediate hysterectomy\",\"Expectant management for several weeks regardless of maternal preference\",\"Induction of labor with a plan for vaginal birth\",\"External cephalic version followed by elective cesarean delivery\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cesarean delivery adds hemorrhage, infection, thromboembolism, and future scar-related risk without fetal benefit when fetal death alone is the indication.\",\"Hysterectomy is reserved for exceptional life-threatening maternal pathology and would cause permanent loss of fertility plus major operative morbidity in this stable patient.\",\"Short expectant management may be discussed in selected cases, but prolonged mandatory waiting at term offers no fetal benefit and may increase emotional and maternal burden.\",\"For a stable term patient without a maternal operative indication, induction of labor and planned vaginal birth are generally appropriate, minimizing avoidable surgical morbidity.\",\"External cephalic version has no fetal-rescue purpose after confirmed death, and fetal demise itself does not justify subsequent elective cesarean delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"After confirmed term intrauterine fetal death, management centers on maternal safety, respectful counseling, and planned delivery, because fetal rescue is no longer possible.\",\"In a stable 39-week patient with no operative maternal indication, induction with planned vaginal birth is preferred over cesarean solely for fetal death.\",\"Avoiding unnecessary uterine surgery reduces hemorrhage, infection, thromboembolism, and future scar-related pregnancy risks while allowing controlled delivery and postmortem evaluation if desired.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage stable term IUFD with planned vaginal delivery, usually by induction, unless a separate maternal indication requires cesarean birth.\",\"pr\":\"Bereavement care should include options for memory-making, placental/fetal investigation, genetic testing when appropriate, and follow-up to review results.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm fetal death sonographically, assess maternal status, discuss analgesia and induction options, and provide clear continuity of bereavement support throughout labor and postpartum care.\",\"cn\":\"In a well woman with intrauterine fetal death (IUFD), vaginal birth after induction is recommended; caesarean is reserved for specific maternal indications.\",\"sl\":\"If the mother is well, discuss induction now or going home and returning (mifepristone then misoprostol 24-48 h later); over 90% labour spontaneously within 3 weeks. Vaginal birth is recommended for most; consider caesarean for placenta praevia, transverse lie, previous classical caesarean section, maternal instability or maternal request.\",\"s\":[[\"L25_0\",[31,34,39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F406\"},{\"id\":\"F407\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Urinary incontinence — one-hour pad test\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After a standardized 1-hour pad test for suspected urinary incontinence, which pad-weight increase most closely matches a commonly used positive cutoff?\",\"o\":[\"More than 2 g\",\"More than 0.5 g\",\"More than 5 g\",\"More than 10 g\",\"More than 20 g\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A threshold around 2 g of pad-weight gain is used in some standardized 1-hour pad-test protocols to identify objective urine leakage; exact cutoffs vary across protocols.\",\"A 0.5-g change is close to the range where handling, sweat, or measurement variability can contribute substantially and is below common standardized positivity thresholds.\",\"A 5-g gain indicates definite leakage but is above the approximate positive threshold used in this 1-hour testing convention and may already reflect greater severity.\",\"A 10-g gain represents substantial leakage in a 1-hour test and is not the minimum threshold used to establish a positive result.\",\"A 20-g gain reflects much larger urine loss and is used in severity discussions rather than as the low-end positivity cutoff for the standardized 1-hour test.\"],\"e\":[\"A pad test provides an objective gravimetric estimate of urinary leakage by comparing pad weight before and after a standardized activity period.\",\"A gain around 1.4–2 g is used as a positive cutoff in established 1-hour protocols, making more than 2 g the closest listed threshold while recognizing protocol variation.\",\"Because approximately 1 mL of urine adds about 1 g of pad mass, consistent bladder volume, activity, timing, and weighing technique are essential for meaningful interpretation.\"],\"tk\":\"Interpret a 1-hour pad test with the threshold specified by the standardized protocol, commonly around 1.4–2 g for positivity.\",\"pr\":\"Published pad-test cutoffs are not universal; thresholds for positivity differ from thresholds used to grade mild, moderate, and severe leakage.\",\"tp\":\"Use the same calibrated scale, test duration, bladder-volume protocol, and activity sequence when comparing pad weights over time.\",\"cn\":\"In the 1-hour standardized pad test, more than 2 g weight gain is positive and more than 10 g is severe.\",\"sl\":\"1-hour standardized test: pre-weigh pad, drink 500 mL, rest 15 min, moderate exercise 30 min, 15 min provocative exercises; positive test >2 g increase; severe incontinence >10 g increase.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[29]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F407\"},{\"id\":\"F408\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"ACE-inhibitor fetotoxicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication should be avoided in pregnancy because it can cause fetal renal injury and oligohydramnios?\",\"o\":[\"Low-dose aspirin\",\"Methyldopa\",\"Vitamin C\",\"Paracetamol (acetaminophen)\",\"Enalapril\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Low-dose aspirin inhibits platelet cyclo-oxygenase and is used for pre-eclampsia prevention in selected high-risk pregnancies; it is not associated with the ACE-inhibitor fetopathy pattern.\",\"Methyldopa is a centrally acting alpha-2 agonist with longstanding pregnancy use for hypertension; it lacks the fetal renin-angiotensin blockade of ACE inhibitors.\",\"Vitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin involved in collagen synthesis and antioxidant pathways; usual nutritional doses are not linked to oligohydramnios from fetal renal failure.\",\"Paracetamol provides analgesic and antipyretic effects and is commonly used in pregnancy at recommended doses; it does not cause fetal renal dysgenesis.\",\"Enalapril is an ACE inhibitor; fetal renin-angiotensin blockade can cause renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, and neonatal renal failure, especially with later-pregnancy exposure.\"],\"e\":[\"ACE inhibitors such as enalapril inhibit the fetal renin-angiotensin system and can produce oligohydramnios and fetal renal impairment.\",\"Among these agents, enalapril has a characteristic fetal renal fetopathy in which impaired urine production reduces amniotic fluid.\",\"Because fetal renal perfusion depends on angiotensin II, blocking this pathway can impair renal development and function; pregnancy hypertension is treated with alternatives that do not block the fetal renin-angiotensin system.\"],\"tk\":\"ACE inhibitors are avoided in pregnancy because fetal renin-angiotensin blockade can cause renal injury and oligohydramnios.\",\"pr\":\"Exposure later in gestation is particularly associated with oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia, and neonatal renal failure.\",\"tp\":\"When a pregnant patient is taking an ACE inhibitor, stop it promptly and select a pregnancy-compatible antihypertensive after reviewing blood pressure and comorbidities.\",\"cn\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors such as enalapril are contraindicated in pregnancy: oligohydramnios, fetal renal failure, growth restriction and abnormal ossification.\",\"sl\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy (oligohydramnios, intrauterine growth restriction [IUGR], renal failure, abnormal bone ossification) but can be used in breastfeeding. Stop teratogenic agents (ACEIs, ARBs [angiotensin receptor blockers], statins, thiazides) and switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[37]],[\"L01_0\",[10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F408\"},{\"id\":\"F409\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Risk of Malignancy Index calculation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has bilateral multilocular ovarian masses and a cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) level of 300 U/mL. Using risk of malignancy index (RMI) I (U × M × CA-125), what is her Risk of Malignancy Index?\",\"o\":[\"2700\",\"Cannot be determined from the information provided\",\"900\",\"300\",\"1800\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"With at least two suspicious ultrasound features, U = 3; postmenopausal status gives M = 3, so 3 × 3 × 300 = 2700.\",\"The stem provides the three required RMI I components: ultrasound morphology, menopausal status, and CA-125.\",\"A value of 900 would omit one of the two threefold multipliers used for postmenopausal status and a high ultrasound score.\",\"Using the CA-125 concentration alone ignores the ultrasound score and menopausal score that define the RMI I calculation.\",\"An RMI of 1800 would require a different multiplication factor; the standard RMI I scoring for two or more ultrasound features uses U = 3.\"],\"e\":[\"RMI I multiplies an ultrasound score (U), menopausal score (M), and CA-125 concentration.\",\"Bilaterality plus multilocularity gives at least two suspicious ultrasound features, so U = 3; postmenopausal status gives M = 3, and 3 × 3 × 300 = 2700.\",\"A high RMI reflects the combined effect of suspicious morphology, menopause, and tumor-marker elevation and is used to triage patients toward gynecologic oncology assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"RMI I = ultrasound score × menopausal score × CA-125; this patient’s score is 2700.\",\"pr\":\"In the traditional RMI I system, two or more suspicious ultrasound features score U = 3 rather than simply counting each feature.\",\"tp\":\"When an adnexal mass has a high RMI, arrange specialist gynecologic-oncology evaluation rather than routine benign-cyst follow-up.\",\"cn\":\"Risk of Malignancy Index (RMI) = U x M x cancer antigen 125 (CA-125); 2+ ultrasound features give U=3 and postmenopause M=3.\",\"sl\":\"RMI = U x M x CA-125. Complex cyst features: ascites, bilateral lesions, septations, solid components or papillary projections, metastases. Slide question: CA-125 300 with bilateral septated cysts and ascites gives RMI 2700.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[14,8,29]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F409\"},{\"id\":\"F410\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"FIGO stage IIB cervical cancer\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with cervical carcinoma has parametrial invasion, but the tumor has not reached the pelvic wall. Which International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage best describes this disease?\",\"o\":[\"FIGO stage IB\",\"FIGO stage IA\",\"FIGO stage IV\",\"FIGO stage III\",\"FIGO stage IIB\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Stage IB is invasive carcinoma confined to the cervix beyond stage IA criteria.\",\"Stage IA is microscopic invasive disease confined to the cervix and does not include parametrial extension.\",\"Stage IV disease invades the bladder or rectal mucosa or extends beyond the true pelvis/distantly.\",\"Stage III includes extension to the pelvic wall, lower third of the vagina, hydronephrosis, or qualifying nodal disease depending on the substage.\",\"Stage IIB cervical cancer extends into the parametrium without reaching the pelvic wall.\"],\"e\":[\"Cervical-cancer staging is based on the anatomic extent of disease rather than histologic grade alone.\",\"Parametrial invasion without pelvic-wall involvement corresponds to FIGO stage IIB.\",\"Once tumor reaches the pelvic wall, lower vagina, causes hydronephrosis, or has qualifying nodal spread, staging moves into stage III categories; adjacent-organ mucosal invasion or distant spread is stage IV.\"],\"tk\":\"Parametrial invasion without pelvic-wall involvement defines FIGO stage IIB cervical cancer.\",\"pr\":\"Stage IIA involves the upper vagina without parametrial invasion, whereas stage IIB specifically includes the parametrium.\",\"tp\":\"Describe the exact sites of local extension on examination and imaging because treatment planning depends on stage-defining anatomy.\",\"cn\":\"International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage IIB cervical cancer means parametrial involvement without reaching the pelvic wall.\",\"sl\":\"IIA: involvement of the upper two-thirds of the vagina without parametrial invasion. IIB: with parametrial involvement.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F410\"},{\"id\":\"F411\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Aspirin after prior eclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman had eclampsia in her previous pregnancy and is now pregnant again. Which preventive medication should be offered from 12 weeks' gestation if there is no contraindication?\",\"o\":[\"Prophylactic phenytoin\",\"Low-dose aspirin\",\"Routine magnesium sulfate throughout pregnancy\",\"Therapeutic-dose low-molecular-weight heparin\",\"Daily furosemide\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Phenytoin is an antiepileptic drug, but prior eclampsia does not justify chronic anticonvulsant prophylaxis between pregnancies.\",\"A previous hypertensive disorder of pregnancy is a high-risk factor for pre-eclampsia; low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks reduces recurrence risk.\",\"Magnesium sulfate is used for seizure prophylaxis or treatment in severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, not as continuous antenatal prevention from early pregnancy.\",\"Therapeutic anticoagulation is reserved for conditions such as acute venous thromboembolism (VTE) or selected thrombophilias and is not standard prevention after isolated prior eclampsia.\",\"Loop diuretics reduce intravascular volume and are not used routinely to prevent pre-eclampsia in an otherwise stable pregnant patient.\"],\"e\":[\"A history of eclampsia represents previous hypertensive disease in pregnancy, a major recurrence risk for pre-eclampsia.\",\"In a subsequent pregnancy, guideline-based prevention includes low-dose aspirin beginning at 12 weeks when no contraindication is present.\",\"Aspirin reduces platelet thromboxane activity and improves the prostacyclin-thromboxane balance implicated in abnormal placentation; it prevents disease rather than treating an acute eclamptic seizure.\"],\"tk\":\"Previous eclampsia is a high-risk indication for antenatal low-dose aspirin prophylaxis from 12 weeks.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) advises 75–150 mg aspirin daily from 12 weeks until birth for women at high risk of pre-eclampsia.\",\"tp\":\"At booking, document the exact prior hypertensive diagnosis and start aspirin on time rather than waiting for hypertension to recur.\",\"cn\":\"Previous pre-eclampsia or eclampsia is a high-risk factor; give low-dose aspirin 75-150 mg daily from 12 weeks (before 16 weeks) until delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Low-dose aspirin 75-150 mg starting before 16 weeks until delivery for one or more high-risk factors or two or more moderate risk factors; pre-eclampsia/eclampsia in a previous pregnancy is a high-risk factor.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[33,34,12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F411\"},{\"id\":\"F412\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placental abruption presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is least characteristic of placental abruption?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal bleeding\",\"Painless bright-red bleeding with a soft, nontender uterus\",\"Fetal heart-rate abnormalities\",\"Severe abdominal pain\",\"A hard, board-like uterus\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Abruption can produce external hemorrhage through the cervix, although a substantial component of blood loss may remain concealed.\",\"Painless bleeding with a soft uterus is a classic placenta previa pattern; abruption more often produces uterine tenderness or hypertonus.\",\"Placental separation can impair fetal oxygen delivery, producing nonreassuring fetal heart-rate patterns or fetal death in severe cases.\",\"Placental separation can cause retroplacental bleeding that stretches and irritates the uterus, producing severe abdominal or back pain.\",\"Severe abruption can produce uterine hypertonus and tenderness, giving a firm or board-like abdomen.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental abruption classically causes painful bleeding, uterine tenderness, and hypertonus, although bleeding may be concealed.\",\"Severe abdominal pain, a board-like uterus, vaginal bleeding, and fetal heart-rate abnormalities all fit abruption; painless bleeding with a soft uterus is the contrasting pattern.\",\"Premature placental separation reduces the functioning exchange surface and may trigger uterine irritability, maternal hemorrhage, and acute fetal hypoxia.\"],\"tk\":\"Placental abruption is usually painful and associated with uterine tenderness or hypertonus; painless soft-uterus bleeding suggests another cause.\",\"pr\":\"The amount of visible bleeding can underestimate severity because a substantial retroplacental hematoma may remain concealed.\",\"tp\":\"In suspected abruption, assess maternal hemodynamics and fetal status immediately; do not rely on ultrasound to exclude the diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Painless bright-red bleeding with a soft non-tender uterus suggests placenta praevia, not placental abruption.\",\"sl\":\"A tense or woody uterus indicates significant abruption; a soft, non-tender uterus suggests a lower genital tract cause or placenta or vasa praevia. Abruption presents with vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, contractions and fetal distress; placenta praevia bleeding is painless and bright red.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[13,32,40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F412\"},{\"id\":\"F413\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Endometrial cancer risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT a recognized risk factor for endometrial carcinoma?\",\"o\":[\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"Obesity\",\"Unopposed estrogen therapy\",\"Cigarette smoking\",\"Nulliparity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Chronic anovulation in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) produces prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure and increases endometrial hyperplasia/cancer risk.\",\"Adipose-tissue aromatization increases peripheral estrogen exposure, making obesity a major endometrial-cancer risk factor.\",\"Estrogen without adequate progestogenic opposition promotes endometrial proliferation and hyperplasia, increasing carcinoma risk.\",\"Cigarette smoking is not a standard risk factor for endometrial carcinoma and has been associated epidemiologically with lower estrogen-dependent endometrial cancer risk, despite its many serious harms.\",\"Nulliparity is associated with more lifetime ovulatory estrogen exposure and is a recognized epidemiologic risk factor.\"],\"e\":[\"Most classic endometrioid endometrial-cancer risks increase cumulative unopposed estrogen exposure.\",\"Obesity, PCOS, unopposed estrogen therapy, and nulliparity all increase risk; cigarette smoking is not used as an endometrial-cancer risk factor.\",\"Chronic estrogenic stimulation without cyclic progesterone drives endometrial proliferation, which can progress through hyperplasia to carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Endometrial-cancer risk rises with prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure; smoking is not a standard risk factor.\",\"pr\":\"Tamoxifen also increases endometrial pathology risk because it has partial estrogen-agonist activity in the endometrium.\",\"tp\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding warrants prompt evaluation regardless of whether the patient has obvious estrogen-related risk factors.\",\"cn\":\"Smoking decreases, rather than increases, the risk of endometrial cancer; obesity, nulliparity, late menopause and anovulation increase it.\",\"sl\":\"Smoking decreases the risk of developing endometrial cancer. Obesity, nulliparity and late menopause are risk factors; the relationship with unopposed estrogen is well documented.\",\"s\":[[\"L42_0\",[13,7,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F036\"},{\"id\":\"F414\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ovarian tumor associations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which ovarian-tumor association is mismatched?\",\"o\":[\"Thecoma — Meigs syndrome\",\"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor — testosterone secretion\",\"Granulosa cell tumor — estrogen secretion\",\"Dysgerminoma — lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) elevation\",\"Granulosa cell tumor — inhibin B\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":4,\"ox\":[\"Correct (this is the mismatched pair). Classic Meigs syndrome is ovarian fibroma with ascites and right pleural effusion; thecoma is not the canonical tumor for this syndrome.\",\"Wrong (correct pair). Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors secrete testosterone and can cause virilization and rapidly progressive hirsutism.\",\"Wrong (correct pair). Granulosa cell tumors secrete estrogen and can cause precocious puberty, abnormal uterine bleeding or endometrial hyperplasia.\",\"Wrong (correct pair). Dysgerminoma is associated with raised lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), which is used as its tumor marker.\",\"Wrong (correct pair). Granulosa cell tumors secrete inhibin B, which is used as a marker for diagnosis and follow-up.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovarian tumors often have characteristic hormonal or marker associations that help narrow the differential.\",\"Granulosa tumors are linked to estrogen and inhibin B, Sertoli-Leydig tumors to androgens, and dysgerminoma to LDH; classic Meigs syndrome instead accompanies an ovarian fibroma.\",\"Meigs syndrome results from a benign fibrous ovarian tumor with ascites and pleural effusion, and the effusions resolve after tumor removal.\"],\"tk\":\"Classic Meigs syndrome is associated with ovarian fibroma, not thecoma.\",\"pr\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding in a patient with a granulosa cell tumor should prompt assessment for estrogen-stimulated endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating an ovarian mass, match age, imaging morphology, endocrine manifestations, and tumor markers rather than relying on a single marker.\",\"cn\":\"Classic Meigs syndrome (ascites, pleural effusion) accompanies ovarian fibroma, not thecoma; granulosa cell tumours secrete oestrogen and inhibin.\",\"sl\":\"Sex cord-stromal tumours: granulosa cell tumour is low grade, indolent with late recurrence and secretes inhibin; Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour; thecoma/fibroma group usually benign. The slides do not name Meigs syndrome.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"c\",\"n\":\"The QBank marked E, but granulosa cell tumour does secrete inhibin B, so E is a correct pair. The mismatched pair is A: Meigs syndrome (ascites, pleural effusion, benign ovarian tumour) goes with fibroma, not thecoma, as the QBank's own take-home says (ovarian cancer slides, slide 6).\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F414\"},{\"id\":\"F415\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"DHT and external genital development\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which hormone is primarily responsible for masculinization of the external genitalia in a 46,XY fetus?\",\"o\":[\"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT)\",\"Anti-Müllerian hormone\",\"Luteinizing hormone\",\"Follicle-stimulating hormone\",\"Testosterone\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Correct. Dihydrotestosterone (DHT), formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, drives formation of the penis, scrotum and prostate in a 46,XY fetus.\",\"Wrong. Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) from Sertoli cells causes regression of the Müllerian ducts but does not masculinize the external genitalia.\",\"Wrong. Luteinizing hormone (LH) stimulates Leydig cells to make testosterone, acting upstream rather than directly on the external genitalia.\",\"Wrong. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) acts mainly on Sertoli cells and is not the androgen that masculinizes the external genitalia.\",\"Wrong. Testosterone mainly develops the Wolffian duct structures (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles); external genital masculinization needs its conversion to dihydrotestosterone (DHT).\"],\"e\":[\"Masculinization of the external genitalia requires conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) by 5-alpha-reductase.\",\"A 46,XY fetus can produce testosterone yet have undervirilized external genitalia if DHT formation or androgen action is impaired.\",\"Testosterone mainly supports Wolffian-duct derivatives, whereas DHT has greater androgen-receptor potency in tissues forming the penis, scrotum, and prostate.\"],\"tk\":\"DHT, generated by 5-alpha-reductase, is the principal androgen for external male genital development.\",\"pr\":\"5-alpha-reductase deficiency can produce 46,XY undervirilization at birth with virilization becoming more apparent at puberty.\",\"tp\":\"In a difference of sex development (DSD) evaluation, separate Müllerian-duct status, testosterone production, DHT conversion, and androgen-receptor action.\",\"cn\":\"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT), made from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, masculinizes the external genitalia of the 46,XY fetus.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal testes produce androgens that virilize the indifferent external genitalia. Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is more potent than testosterone and is formed by 5-alpha-reductase.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[16]],[\"L31_0\",[10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F415\"},{\"id\":\"F416\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Trichomoniasis and strawberry cervix\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A \\\"strawberry cervix\\\" on speculum examination is classically associated with which infection?\",\"o\":[\"Trichomoniasis\",\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"Genital herpes simplex infection\",\"Bacterial vaginosis\",\"Chlamydial cervicitis\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Trichomonas vaginalis can cause punctate cervical hemorrhages producing the classic strawberry-cervix appearance.\",\"Candida commonly causes intense pruritus, vulvar erythema, and thick curdy discharge with budding yeast or pseudohyphae.\",\"Herpes simplex virus (HSV) typically causes painful vesicles or ulcers and can produce an ulcerative cervicitis rather than diffuse petechial cervical changes.\",\"Bacterial vaginosis causes thin homogeneous discharge, elevated vaginal pH, and clue cells rather than punctate cervical hemorrhages.\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis can cause mucopurulent endocervical discharge and cervical friability, often with minimal symptoms.\"],\"e\":[\"Trichomoniasis is caused by the flagellated protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis and can produce diffuse punctate cervical hemorrhages.\",\"The descriptive term strawberry cervix refers to these petechial changes and strongly suggests trichomoniasis in the appropriate discharge syndrome.\",\"Infection raises vaginal pH and provokes mucosal inflammation; nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is more sensitive than wet-mount microscopy for diagnosis.\"],\"tk\":\"A strawberry cervix is a classic examination finding of Trichomonas vaginalis infection.\",\"pr\":\"Trichomoniasis is sexually transmitted, so management includes treating sexual partners to reduce reinfection.\",\"tp\":\"Use a sensitive NAAT when available and test for coexisting sexually transmitted infections (STIs) according to local sexual-health guidance.\",\"cn\":\"Strawberry cervix is the classic sign of Trichomonas vaginalis infection.\",\"sl\":\"Trichomoniasis causes vulvitis, vaginitis and cervicitis; classic: strawberry cervix. Diagnosis by wet smear from the posterior fornix showing motile flagellated organisms.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F416\"},{\"id\":\"F417\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Swyer syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A phenotypic female with a 46,XY karyotype has a uterus and cervix but fails to develop normal secondary sexual characteristics. Which diagnosis is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome\",\"5-alpha-reductase deficiency\",\"Swyer syndrome (46,XY pure gonadal dysgenesis)\",\"Turner syndrome\",\"Klinefelter syndrome\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) has functioning testes that secrete anti-Müllerian hormone, so the uterus and upper vagina are absent despite a female external phenotype.\",\"5-alpha-reductase deficiency occurs in 46,XY individuals with testes and AMH production, so Müllerian structures are absent.\",\"Swyer syndrome features 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis, absent testicular AMH production, persistence of Müllerian structures, and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism.\",\"Turner syndrome is classically 45,X with streak ovaries and a uterus; the karyotype in the stem is 46,XY.\",\"Klinefelter syndrome is usually 47,XXY with a male phenotype, small testes, and primary testicular failure.\"],\"e\":[\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY pure gonadal dysgenesis with nonfunctioning streak gonads.\",\"Because there are no functioning Sertoli cells to produce anti-Müllerian hormone, the uterus and cervix persist despite the XY karyotype.\",\"Lack of gonadal sex steroids leads to hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and poor pubertal development; streak gonads also carry a gonadoblastoma risk.\"],\"tk\":\"A 46,XY individual with a uterus and gonadal failure suggests Swyer syndrome.\",\"pr\":\"The presence or absence of a uterus is a powerful discriminator between Swyer syndrome and complete androgen insensitivity syndrome.\",\"tp\":\"Patients with Swyer syndrome require specialist difference of sex development (DSD) care, including discussion of prophylactic removal of dysgenetic gonads because of tumor risk.\",\"cn\":\"A 46,XY phenotypic female with a uterus and absent puberty has Swyer syndrome (pure gonadal dysgenesis).\",\"sl\":\"Swyer syndrome: 46,XY, no testosterone or anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH), female external genitalia, uterus and fallopian tubes present, absence of pubertal development.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[17]],[\"L29_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F290\"},{\"id\":\"F418\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Idiopathic hirsutism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 27-year-old woman with normal body mass index (BMI), regular ovulatory cycles, normal serum androgen levels, and no virilization has gradually progressive hirsutism. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia\",\"Cushing syndrome\",\"Idiopathic (familial) hirsutism\",\"Androgen-secreting ovarian tumor\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) often causes elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone and may present with menstrual irregularity or biochemical adrenal androgen excess.\",\"Cushing syndrome usually has systemic hypercortisolism features such as proximal weakness, violaceous striae, easy bruising, or hypertension.\",\"Idiopathic hirsutism occurs with normal ovulatory function and normal circulating androgens, often reflecting increased pilosebaceous androgen sensitivity.\",\"An androgen-producing tumor usually causes rapidly progressive hirsutism, marked androgen elevation, or virilization rather than a slow isolated pattern.\",\"PCOS commonly causes hirsutism but usually accompanies ovulatory dysfunction and/or biochemical hyperandrogenism in addition to clinical androgen excess.\"],\"e\":[\"Hirsutism with normal cycles, normal androgen concentrations, and no virilization can represent idiopathic hirsutism.\",\"The absence of rapid progression, menstrual dysfunction, or biochemical androgen excess makes a tumor or classic hyperandrogenic disorder less likely.\",\"Hair follicles can show increased local androgen sensitivity or 5-alpha-reductase activity even when circulating androgen levels are within the reference range.\"],\"tk\":\"Idiopathic hirsutism is considered when hirsutism occurs with normal ovulation and normal serum androgens.\",\"pr\":\"Rapid onset, virilization, or markedly elevated testosterone should trigger evaluation for an androgen-secreting tumor.\",\"tp\":\"Document a modified Ferriman-Gallwey score and assess menstrual pattern before ordering targeted androgen studies.\",\"cn\":\"Hirsutism with regular ovulatory cycles, normal androgens and no virilization is idiopathic (familial) hirsutism.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of hyperandrogenism: polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) 75%, idiopathic hirsutism 15%; investigations include testosterone, free androgen index (FAI), dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and 17-hydroxyprogesterone.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[14,19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F103\"},{\"id\":\"F419\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PID etiologies and IUD timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which option is least likely to represent a typical infectious cause or current risk context for pelvic inflammatory disease?\",\"o\":[\"Bacterial vaginosis\",\"Chlamydia\",\"Gonorrhea\",\"Intrauterine device (IUD) last year\",\"Trichomoniasis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"BV-associated anaerobic flora are linked with upper-genital-tract polymicrobial infection and can coexist with PID.\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis is a major sexually transmitted cause of PID and may produce subtle or minimally symptomatic disease.\",\"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a classic cervicitis-associated PID pathogen and can ascend into the upper genital tract.\",\"PID risk related to IUD placement is concentrated in the first few weeks after insertion; an uncomplicated insertion one year earlier is not a continuing infectious cause.\",\"Trichomonas vaginalis is associated with genital-tract inflammation and can coexist with organisms implicated in PID.\"],\"e\":[\"PID is usually an ascending polymicrobial upper-genital-tract infection, with chlamydia and gonorrhea prominent causes and anaerobic vaginal flora also implicated.\",\"The source's one-year-old IUD history is the outlier because insertion-related PID risk is concentrated shortly after device placement, not maintained indefinitely.\",\"Cervical barrier disruption during insertion can transiently facilitate bacterial ascent, whereas long-term IUD presence without active infection does not create persistent PID risk.\"],\"tk\":\"IUD-related PID risk is concentrated soon after insertion rather than persisting for a year.\",\"pr\":\"Do not remove an IUD automatically when treating uncomplicated PID; management depends on response to antibiotics and patient preference.\",\"tp\":\"Test for gonorrhea and chlamydia and begin empiric PID treatment promptly when the clinical criteria are met.\",\"cn\":\"Intrauterine device (IUD)-related pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) risk rises only in the first 3 weeks after insertion.\",\"sl\":\"Intrauterine contraceptive device (IUCD) risks: pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) with a 6-fold increase in the first 3 weeks; overall risk is low unless sexually transmitted infection (STI) exposure.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F419\"},{\"id\":\"F420\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Combined oral contraceptives and cancer risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Current or recent combined oral contraceptive use is associated with a small increase in the risk of which cancer, while reducing the risks of endometrial and ovarian cancer?\",\"o\":[\"Endometrial stromal sarcoma\",\"Breast cancer\",\"Colorectal cancer\",\"Endometrial cancer\",\"Ovarian cancer\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometrial stromal sarcoma is rare and is not the characteristic hormonal-contraceptive cancer association tested in routine counseling.\",\"Current or recent combined hormonal contraceptive use is associated with a small transient increase in risk that diminishes after discontinuation.\",\"Combined oral contraceptive use has not been established as producing the same small increase in breast-cancer risk and may be associated with reduced colorectal-cancer risk in observational data.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives reduce endometrial-cancer risk through progestin opposition of estrogen-driven proliferation.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives reduce ovarian-cancer risk partly through suppression of repeated ovulation.\"],\"e\":[\"Combined oral contraceptives alter site-specific cancer risks rather than shifting them all in the same direction.\",\"Current or recent use is associated with a small increase in breast-cancer risk, whereas endometrial and ovarian cancer risks fall.\",\"Suppression of ovulation and progestin-mediated endometrial protection explain important benefits, while breast tissue remains hormonally responsive to estrogen-progestin exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"COCPs slightly increase breast-cancer risk during current or recent use while reducing endometrial and ovarian cancer risk.\",\"pr\":\"The absolute breast-cancer increase in reproductive-age users is small because baseline incidence is low in this age group.\",\"tp\":\"Counsel using absolute as well as relative risk and consider personal history of breast cancer when choosing contraception.\",\"cn\":\"Combined oral contraceptives (COC) slightly increase breast cancer risk while reducing endometrial and ovarian cancer risk by about 50%.\",\"sl\":\"Breast cancer: small increase from 10/1000 non-pill users to 11/1000 pill users, back to background 10 years after stopping; cervical slight increase. Endometrial and ovarian cancer decrease by 50%, colorectal by 20%.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[19,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F420\"},{\"id\":\"F421\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Voiding lower urinary tract symptoms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a voiding lower urinary tract symptom?\",\"o\":[\"Nocturia\",\"Urgency urinary incontinence\",\"Increased daytime frequency\",\"Poor urinary stream\",\"Urgency\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Nocturia is a storage symptom defined by waking from sleep to pass urine.\",\"Urgency incontinence is a storage-phase symptom in which leakage accompanies or immediately follows urgency.\",\"Frequency is a storage symptom involving abnormally frequent daytime voiding.\",\"A poor or weak stream is a voiding symptom reflecting impaired flow during bladder emptying.\",\"Urgency is a storage symptom characterized by a sudden compelling desire to void that is difficult to defer.\"],\"e\":[\"Lower urinary tract symptoms are divided into storage, voiding, and post-micturition groups.\",\"A poor urinary stream occurs during emptying and is therefore a voiding symptom, whereas urgency, nocturia, frequency, and urgency incontinence occur during storage.\",\"Flow depends on detrusor contraction and outlet resistance; reduced flow may result from bladder-outlet obstruction or impaired detrusor contractility.\"],\"tk\":\"Poor urinary stream is a voiding symptom; urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence are storage symptoms.\",\"pr\":\"Intermittency, hesitancy, straining, and terminal dribbling also belong to the voiding-symptom group.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify whether a urinary complaint occurs during filling, emptying, or immediately after voiding before selecting investigations.\",\"cn\":\"Slow (poor) urinary stream is a voiding symptom; nocturia, frequency, urgency and urge incontinence are storage symptoms.\",\"sl\":\"Voiding symptoms: slow stream (perception of reduced urine flow), intermittent stream, hesitancy, straining to void.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F421\"},{\"id\":\"F422\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Storage lower urinary tract symptoms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a storage lower urinary tract symptom?\",\"o\":[\"Intermittent stream\",\"Post-micturition dribble\",\"Urgency\",\"Straining to void\",\"Hesitancy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intermittency is a voiding-phase symptom in which urinary flow stops and starts.\",\"Post-micturition dribble is a post-micturition symptom, occurring after the main urinary stream has ended.\",\"Urgency is a storage symptom defined by a sudden compelling desire to void that is difficult to defer.\",\"Straining is a voiding symptom that reflects the need to increase abdominal pressure to assist bladder emptying.\",\"Hesitancy is a voiding symptom involving difficulty initiating urine flow.\"],\"e\":[\"Storage symptoms arise during the bladder-filling phase and include urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence.\",\"The selected symptom is urgency; the distractors belong to voiding or post-micturition phases.\",\"Overactive bladder is defined by urgency, usually with increased frequency and nocturia, with or without urgency urinary incontinence.\"],\"tk\":\"Urgency is a storage symptom and is the defining complaint of overactive bladder.\",\"pr\":\"Overactive bladder is a symptom syndrome, so detrusor overactivity on urodynamics is not required for the clinical diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"Ask patients for a bladder diary when frequency and urgency severity are unclear; it provides objective voiding-pattern data.\",\"cn\":\"Urgency is a storage symptom; hesitancy, intermittency and straining are voiding, and dribble is post-micturition.\",\"sl\":\"Storage symptoms include urgency (sudden compelling desire to pass urine, difficult to defer). Voiding: slow stream, intermittency, hesitancy, straining. Post-micturition: incomplete emptying, post-micturition dribble.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[4,7,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F422\"},{\"id\":\"F423\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Lactational amenorrhea contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman is 8 weeks postpartum after cesarean birth, is amenorrheic, and is fully breastfeeding her infant day and night. Which counseling statement about contraception is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Exclusive breastfeeding alone is reliable contraception for the first 12 months postpartum\",\"She should avoid all hormonal contraception because she had a cesarean delivery\",\"No contraception is needed until the first postpartum menstrual period\",\"She may use the lactational amenorrhea method while all three LAM criteria remain satisfied\",\"Combined estrogen-progestin pills are the preferred method immediately after cesarean birth\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Breastfeeding alone is insufficient once 6 months postpartum, menses resumes, or feeding becomes less than fully/nearly fully breastfeeding.\",\"Cesarean delivery does not prohibit all hormonal methods; method choice depends on postpartum timing, breastfeeding, and venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk.\",\"Ovulation can occur before the first postpartum menses, so waiting for menstruation can lead to unintended pregnancy.\",\"LAM is effective only when the patient is amenorrheic, fully or nearly fully breastfeeding, and less than 6 months postpartum.\",\"Early postpartum estrogen-containing contraception is limited by venous thromboembolism risk and breastfeeding considerations.\"],\"e\":[\"The lactational amenorrhea method has three simultaneous requirements: amenorrhea, fully or nearly fully breastfeeding, and <6 months postpartum.\",\"At 8 weeks postpartum, the woman in the stem currently satisfies all three criteria, so LAM can provide temporary contraception while they continue to be met.\",\"Frequent suckling suppresses hypothalamic gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility and delays ovulation, but this suppression weakens as feeding intervals lengthen or complementary feeding begins.\"],\"tk\":\"LAM requires amenorrhea, full or near-full breastfeeding, and less than 6 months postpartum.\",\"pr\":\"LAM should be replaced or supplemented when any one of the three criteria is no longer met.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss a transition plan to a longer-term method before 6 months postpartum so contraception is continuous when LAM eligibility ends.\",\"cn\":\"The lactational amenorrhoea method (LAM) is over 98% effective only if fully breastfeeding, amenorrhoeic and under 6 months postpartum.\",\"sl\":\"Lactational amenorrhoea method (LAM) is more than 98% effective if fully breastfeeding, less than 6 months postpartum and amenorrhoeic; no medical conditions restrict LAM.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[10]],[\"L35_1\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F264\"},{\"id\":\"F424\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Female genital tract embryology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about normal female internal genital tract development is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Absence of anti-Müllerian hormone permits Müllerian structures to persist\",\"The Müllerian ducts form the fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina\",\"The Wolffian ducts largely regress in a typical female fetus\",\"The lower vagina develops mainly from the urogenital sinus\",\"Ovarian hormones are required for Müllerian duct development into the uterus and fallopian tubes\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"AMH normally causes Müllerian duct regression in a 46,XY fetus with functioning Sertoli cells.\",\"Paramesonephric ducts undergo fusion and canalization to form the tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina.\",\"Without sustained fetal testicular testosterone, the mesonephric ducts regress rather than differentiating into male internal ducts.\",\"Sinovaginal bulbs from the urogenital sinus form the vaginal plate and lower vaginal epithelium.\",\"Müllerian duct development occurs largely by default in the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone, not because fetal ovarian hormones drive the process.\"],\"e\":[\"Female internal genital development proceeds through Müllerian duct persistence and differentiation rather than requiring fetal ovarian hormone secretion.\",\"In the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone, the Müllerian ducts persist and form the tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina; the Wolffian ducts largely regress without sustained testosterone.\",\"This embryology explains why gonadal dysgenesis can coexist with a uterus when functioning Sertoli cells and AMH are absent.\"],\"tk\":\"Müllerian development occurs because AMH is absent, not because fetal ovarian hormones are required.\",\"pr\":\"The lower vagina has a different embryologic origin from the upper vagina, arising mainly from the urogenital sinus.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating congenital genital anomalies, identify which structure failed to form before inferring the embryologic defect.\",\"cn\":\"Mullerian ducts form the tubes, uterus and upper vagina because anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) is absent, independent of ovarian hormones.\",\"sl\":\"Lack of anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) causes Wolffian regression and allows Mullerian ducts to form the fallopian tubes, uterus and upper vagina; Mullerian development occurs separately from gonadal development.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[16]],[\"L29_0\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F424\"},{\"id\":\"F425\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oral treatment of bacterial vaginosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has a thin, fishy-smelling vaginal discharge consistent with bacterial vaginosis. Which is a recommended oral first-line regimen?\",\"o\":[\"Azithromycin 1 g orally once\",\"Metronidazole 500 mg orally twice daily for 7 days\",\"Doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days\",\"Amoxicillin 500 mg orally three times daily for 7 days\",\"Fluconazole 150 mg orally once\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Single-dose azithromycin has roles in selected sexually transmitted infections, but it is not a recommended BV regimen.\",\"Oral metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days is a CDC-recommended regimen for symptomatic bacterial vaginosis.\",\"Doxycycline is used in regimens for chlamydial infection and pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), not as standard monotherapy for bacterial vaginosis.\",\"Amoxicillin does not provide the targeted anaerobic coverage used in established bacterial-vaginosis regimens.\",\"Fluconazole treats vulvovaginal candidiasis, which typically causes pruritus and thick curdy discharge rather than an amine odor.\"],\"e\":[\"Bacterial vaginosis reflects replacement of lactobacilli by a polymicrobial anaerobic vaginal flora and often causes thin discharge with a fishy odor.\",\"A recommended oral regimen is metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days.\",\"Metronidazole is active against anaerobic organisms; topical metronidazole and clindamycin are also guideline-supported regimens, which is why the stem specifically asks for an oral regimen.\"],\"tk\":\"Symptomatic bacterial vaginosis can be treated with metronidazole 500 mg orally twice daily for 7 days.\",\"pr\":\"Clue cells and a positive amine odor are classic Amsel criteria for bacterial vaginosis.\",\"tp\":\"When choosing BV therapy, distinguish oral from topical regimens so multiple recommended routes do not create an ambiguous answer.\",\"cn\":\"Symptomatic bacterial vaginosis (BV) is treated with metronidazole 500 mg orally twice daily for 5-7 days.\",\"sl\":\"Metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 5-7 days, or metronidazole vaginal gel once daily for 5 days, or clindamycin 2% vaginal cream once daily for 7 days; do not treat the male partner.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[3,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F051\"},{\"id\":\"F426\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum endometritis treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about postpartum endometritis management is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Persistent fever despite appropriate antibiotics should prompt reassessment for complications\",\"Fever, uterine tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia are typical findings\",\"Postpartum endometritis is usually polymicrobial\",\"Ampicillin plus gentamicin is the standard first-line regimen for all postpartum endometritis\",\"Cesarean birth is an important risk factor\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong (true statement). Fever persisting despite appropriate antibiotics should prompt a search for retained products, pelvic abscess, resistant organisms or septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.\",\"Wrong (true statement). Postpartum fever with uterine tenderness and foul-smelling lochia is the typical clinical picture of endometritis.\",\"Wrong (true statement). Postpartum endometritis is usually a polymicrobial ascending infection with mixed aerobic and anaerobic genital tract organisms.\",\"Correct (this is the incorrect statement). The classic first-line regimen is intravenous clindamycin plus gentamicin to cover anaerobes and gram-negative organisms; ampicillin plus gentamicin is not the standard regimen for all cases.\",\"Wrong (true statement). Cesarean birth, especially after labor or ruptured membranes, is the single most important risk factor for postpartum endometritis.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum endometritis is usually a polymicrobial ascending infection, particularly after cesarean delivery.\",\"A classic empiric intravenous regimen is clindamycin plus gentamicin; calling ampicillin plus gentamicin the universal standard first-line regimen is inaccurate.\",\"Broad coverage is needed because the postpartum uterine cavity can contain aerobic and anaerobic organisms, and persistent fever warrants evaluation for deep pelvic complications.\"],\"tk\":\"Clindamycin plus gentamicin is a classic empiric regimen for postpartum endometritis because broad polymicrobial coverage is required.\",\"pr\":\"Failure to improve after 48–72 hours of adequate antibiotics should raise concern for septic pelvic thrombophlebitis or pelvic abscess.\",\"tp\":\"Review delivery route, duration of membrane rupture, retained tissue risk, and current vital signs when postpartum fever persists.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum endometritis is polymicrobial and is treated with clindamycin plus gentamicin, not ampicillin plus gentamicin.\",\"sl\":\"Assessment of vaginal microbiology should be performed and appropriate antimicrobial therapy (combination of clindamycin and gentamicin) initiated when endometritis is suspected.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[25]],[\"L26_0\",[34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F426\"},{\"id\":\"F427\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Bishop score components\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is NOT a component of the Bishop score used to assess cervical favorability before induction of labor?\",\"o\":[\"Cervical consistency\",\"Fetal station\",\"Fetal presentation\",\"Cervical effacement\",\"Cervical dilation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A softer cervix indicates greater cervical ripeness and contributes to a more favorable Bishop score.\",\"Station assesses descent of the presenting part relative to the ischial spines and is included in the score.\",\"The Bishop score does not include fetal presentation; it scores dilatation, effacement, station, consistency, and cervical position.\",\"Effacement or cervical length reflects cervical thinning and is part of Bishop scoring.\",\"Cervical dilation is a core Bishop component and contributes more points as the cervix becomes more dilated.\"],\"e\":[\"The Bishop score estimates cervical favorability for induction using five examination findings.\",\"It includes dilation, effacement, station, cervical consistency, and cervical position; fetal presentation is clinically important but is not a scored Bishop component.\",\"A higher score reflects cervical ripening and predicts a greater likelihood of successful induction with amniotomy and oxytocin when otherwise appropriate.\"],\"tk\":\"The Bishop score uses dilation, effacement, station, consistency, and position; fetal presentation is not one of its five components.\",\"pr\":\"A very unfavorable cervix often benefits from cervical-ripening methods before oxytocin-based induction.\",\"tp\":\"Record each Bishop component separately rather than writing only a total score so serial examinations remain interpretable.\",\"cn\":\"The Bishop score includes cervical dilatation, effacement, consistency, position and fetal station, but not fetal presentation.\",\"sl\":\"The Bishop score is based on the dilation, effacement (or length), position and consistency of the cervix and the station of the head with respect to the ischial spines.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F358\"},{\"id\":\"F428\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Urinary versus menstrual symptoms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which complaint is NOT a urinary symptom?\",\"o\":[\"Polyuria\",\"Menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding)\",\"Nocturia\",\"Urinary urgency\",\"Dysuria\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Polyuria means an abnormally large 24-hour urine volume and is a urinary-volume complaint.\",\"Menorrhagia describes excessive menstrual blood loss and belongs to gynecologic bleeding rather than lower urinary tract symptomatology.\",\"Nocturia is waking from sleep to void urine and is classified as a storage lower urinary tract symptom.\",\"Urgency is a sudden compelling desire to void that is difficult to defer.\",\"Dysuria is pain or burning with urination, commonly associated with lower urinary tract inflammation or infection.\"],\"e\":[\"Urinary complaints describe abnormalities of urine volume, storage, voiding, or micturition pain.\",\"Menorrhagia refers to heavy menstrual bleeding and is therefore not a urinary symptom.\",\"Distinguishing vaginal bleeding from hematuria or urinary symptoms begins with careful history about the timing and route of bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Menorrhagia is a menstrual bleeding disorder, whereas polyuria, nocturia, dysuria, and urgency are urinary complaints.\",\"pr\":\"Patients may misidentify the source of blood, so direct questioning about whether blood appears with voiding, on pads, or cyclically can prevent misclassification.\",\"tp\":\"If the source of bleeding is uncertain, examine the genital tract and obtain urinalysis rather than assuming hematuria or vaginal bleeding.\",\"cn\":\"Menorrhagia is a menstrual symptom; nocturia, urgency, polyuria and dysuria are urinary symptoms.\",\"sl\":\"Lower urinary tract symptoms are classified as storage (incontinence, urgency, frequency, nocturia, nocturnal enuresis), voiding (slow stream, intermittency, hesitancy, straining) and post-micturition symptoms.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[4,5,7,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F428\"},{\"id\":\"F429\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":1,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Indications for salpingectomy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which option lists recognized clinical settings in which salpingectomy may be required because of tubal disease?\",\"o\":[\"Tubal ectopic pregnancy, severely damaged hydrosalpinx, and destructive tubal endometriosis\",\"Endometrial polyp, cervical polyp, and adenomyosis\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea, mittelschmerz, and premenstrual syndrome\",\"Uncomplicated bacterial vaginosis, cervical ectropion, and vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"Stress urinary incontinence, overactive bladder, and cystocele\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Each listed condition can produce irreversible tubal disease for which salpingectomy may be appropriate depending on stability, fertility goals, and operative findings.\",\"These disorders involve the uterus or cervix, not destructive fallopian-tube pathology requiring salpingectomy.\",\"These functional or cyclical conditions do not create structural tubal destruction that would justify salpingectomy.\",\"These are lower-genital-tract conditions and do not ordinarily require removal of a fallopian tube.\",\"These are pelvic-floor or lower urinary tract disorders and are anatomically unrelated to the fallopian tube.\"],\"e\":[\"Salpingectomy removes a diseased fallopian tube when conservative treatment is unsuitable or unsafe.\",\"Tubal ectopic pregnancy, severe hydrosalpinx, and destructive tubal disease from endometriosis can each provide a tubal indication for surgery.\",\"Decision-making balances acute safety with future fertility because loss of a tube changes natural conception potential and may alter assisted-reproduction planning.\"],\"tk\":\"Salpingectomy may be required for tubal ectopic pregnancy or severely damaged hydrosalpinx/endometriotic tubes.\",\"pr\":\"Before IVF, selected patients with a significant hydrosalpinx may benefit from salpingectomy or proximal tubal occlusion because hydrosalpingeal fluid can impair implantation.\",\"tp\":\"Document contralateral tubal status and fertility goals before salpingectomy whenever the clinical situation allows informed planning.\",\"cn\":\"Salpingectomy is indicated for tubal disease such as tubal ectopic pregnancy, severe hydrosalpinx and destructive tubal endometriosis.\",\"sl\":\"Surgery for hydrosalpinges before in-vitro fertilisation (IVF): laparoscopic salpingectomy or disconnection of both tubes improves IVF/intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) success rates (pregnancy rate increased by 50%).\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F429\"},{\"id\":\"F430\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Adenomyosis symptoms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about adenomyosis is true?\",\"o\":[\"Adenomyosis is a malignant uterine disorder\",\"Clue cells are diagnostic of adenomyosis\",\"Pelvic pain and dysmenorrhea may be cyclical\",\"Adenomyosis occurs only after menopause\",\"Antibiotics are definitive first-line therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Adenomyosis is a benign condition characterized by endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium.\",\"Clue cells indicate bacterial vaginosis on vaginal microscopy and have no diagnostic role in adenomyosis.\",\"Adenomyosis often causes progressive dysmenorrhea, heavy bleeding, and cyclical pelvic pain related to ectopic endometrial glands within myometrium.\",\"Adenomyosis is most often symptomatic during the reproductive years and is estrogen responsive.\",\"Adenomyosis is not an infection; treatment may include non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), hormonal suppression, or surgery depending on symptoms and fertility goals.\"],\"e\":[\"Adenomyosis is ectopic endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, producing a diffusely enlarged tender uterus in many patients.\",\"Symptoms commonly include heavy menstrual bleeding and cyclical pelvic pain or dysmenorrhea.\",\"Repeated intramyometrial bleeding and inflammation contribute to uterine hypertrophy and pain; treatment therefore targets bleeding, inflammation, or hormonal stimulation rather than infection.\"],\"tk\":\"Adenomyosis commonly causes cyclical pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, and heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound may show a globular uterus, myometrial cysts, and heterogeneous myometrium.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about fertility goals before escalating treatment because hysterectomy is definitive but eliminates future pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Adenomyosis is benign and presents with heavy menstrual bleeding and progressive, cyclical dysmenorrhoea in multiparous women in late reproductive life.\",\"sl\":\"Adenomyosis occurs most often in multiparous women at the end of reproductive life; presenting complaints are heavy menstrual bleeding, progressive dysmenorrhoea and deep dyspareunia, with a symmetrically enlarged uterus that may be tender.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[48]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F430\"},{\"id\":\"F431\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Third-degree perineal tear\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about obstetric perineal trauma is correct?\",\"o\":[\"A third-degree perineal tear involves the anal sphincter complex\",\"The second stage of labor ends with placental delivery\",\"A fourth-degree tear spares the rectal mucosa\",\"Station +5 is located at the maternal ischial spines\",\"A third-degree tear is confined to vaginal mucosa and perineal skin\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Third-degree tears extend through the perineal muscles into the external and/or internal anal sphincter but do not necessarily disrupt rectal mucosa.\",\"The second stage ends with birth of the baby; placental delivery occurs during the third stage.\",\"Fourth-degree tears extend through the sphincter complex and involve the anorectal mucosa.\",\"The ischial spines define station 0; positive stations are below the spines toward the introitus.\",\"Injury limited to skin and mucosa is a first-degree tear, not a third-degree obstetric anal sphincter injury.\"],\"e\":[\"Perineal tears are graded according to the deepest injured anatomic structure.\",\"A third-degree tear involves the anal sphincter complex, whereas a fourth-degree tear additionally reaches the anorectal mucosa.\",\"Accurate classification matters because obstetric anal sphincter injury requires meticulous repair and follow-up to reduce fecal incontinence and pelvic-floor morbidity.\"],\"tk\":\"Third-degree perineal trauma involves the anal sphincter; fourth-degree trauma also involves anorectal mucosa.\",\"pr\":\"A careful rectal examination after repair helps confirm that rectal mucosa has not been inadvertently sutured.\",\"tp\":\"When a severe tear is suspected, ensure repair is performed by a clinician trained in obstetric anal sphincter injury techniques.\",\"cn\":\"A third-degree perineal tear involves the anal sphincter complex; a fourth-degree tear also involves the anorectal mucosa.\",\"sl\":\"First degree: perineal skin and/or vaginal mucosa; second degree: perineal muscles but not anal sphincters; third degree: anal sphincter complex (3a less than 50% of external anal sphincter, 3b more than 50%, 3c both external and internal sphincters); fourth degree: involving anorectal mucosa.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[42]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F431\"},{\"id\":\"F432\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Synthetic oxytocin\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is a synthetic oxytocin preparation used for induction, augmentation, and prevention or treatment of uterine atony?\",\"o\":[\"Carboprost\",\"Misoprostol\",\"Terbutaline\",\"Syntocinon (oxytocin)\",\"Methylergometrine\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analogue used for uterine atony and is avoided in patients with significant asthma.\",\"Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analogue used for cervical ripening, induction, and uterotonic purposes in selected settings.\",\"Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist that relaxes uterine smooth muscle and can be used for acute tocolysis.\",\"Syntocinon activates the uterine Gq-coupled oxytocin receptor, increasing intracellular calcium and myometrial contractions.\",\"Methylergometrine is an ergot alkaloid uterotonic and is avoided in significant hypertension because of vasoconstrictive effects.\"],\"e\":[\"Oxytocin is a posterior-pituitary peptide that stimulates rhythmic myometrial contraction.\",\"Syntocinon is a synthetic oxytocin preparation used for labor induction or augmentation and as a uterotonic after birth.\",\"Binding to Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors increases intracellular calcium in myometrial cells, producing uterine contraction.\"],\"tk\":\"Syntocinon is synthetic oxytocin and is used as both a labor-stimulating drug and uterotonic.\",\"pr\":\"Excessive oxytocin can cause uterine tachysystole with fetal heart-rate abnormalities, requiring dose reduction or discontinuation.\",\"tp\":\"Use an infusion protocol that allows rapid titration and reassess contraction frequency and fetal status after each dose change.\",\"cn\":\"Syntocinon is synthetic oxytocin, used for induction and augmentation of labour and as a uterotonic.\",\"sl\":\"Oxytocin (Syntocinon) is a synthetic nonapeptide identical with oxytocin; with amniotomy it is the preferred induction method when the Bishop score is 7 or more.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F432\"},{\"id\":\"F433\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Shoulder dystocia maneuvers\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which action should NOT be used to resolve shoulder dystocia?\",\"o\":[\"Rubin maneuver\",\"Excessive downward traction on the fetal head\",\"McRoberts maneuver\",\"Suprapubic pressure\",\"Wood screw maneuver\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Rubin maneuver applies internal pressure to the shoulder to achieve adduction and rotation into an oblique diameter.\",\"Excessive traction increases the risk of brachial plexus injury and does not relieve the bony impaction.\",\"McRoberts hyperflexes the maternal hips, flattening the lumbosacral angle and is a first-line shoulder dystocia maneuver.\",\"Suprapubic pressure can adduct and rotate the anterior shoulder to reduce the bisacromial diameter.\",\"The Wood screw maneuver rotates the fetal shoulders by applying pressure to the posterior aspect of a shoulder.\"],\"e\":[\"Shoulder dystocia is managed with positional and rotational maneuvers that alter maternal pelvic geometry or rotate the fetal shoulders.\",\"Excessive traction on the head should be avoided because it increases brachial plexus injury risk without correcting the impacted shoulder.\",\"McRoberts positioning, suprapubic pressure, and internal rotational maneuvers aim to release the anterior shoulder while minimizing fetal nerve stretch.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not use excessive traction for shoulder dystocia; use structured positional and rotational maneuvers instead.\",\"pr\":\"Fundal pressure is also avoided because it can worsen shoulder impaction and increase maternal or fetal injury.\",\"tp\":\"Call for help immediately, announce the diagnosis, stop routine traction, and document the sequence and timing of each maneuver.\",\"cn\":\"Shoulder dystocia is managed with McRoberts, suprapubic pressure and internal rotational manoeuvres, avoiding excessive downward traction on the head.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F433\"},{\"id\":\"F434\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":1,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy testing in reproductive-age bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A reproductive-age woman presents with unexplained vaginal bleeding. Which test should be obtained early in the diagnostic evaluation?\",\"o\":[\"Pregnancy test (urine or serum human chorionic gonadotrophin [hCG])\",\"Cervical cytology\",\"Pelvic ultrasound before confirming pregnancy status\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\",\"Complete blood count alone\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pregnancy testing is essential because ectopic pregnancy and early pregnancy loss can present with bleeding and require time-sensitive management.\",\"Cervical cytology is a screening test for cervical precancer, not the first diagnostic test for acute unexplained vaginal bleeding.\",\"Ultrasound is important when pregnancy is suspected, but interpreting findings is strengthened by pregnancy status and quantitative hCG when indicated.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) is a nonspecific tumor-associated marker and is not a first-line test for acute reproductive-age bleeding.\",\"A CBC estimates anemia and blood loss but cannot determine whether pregnancy-related bleeding is present.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy-related causes must be considered early in any reproductive-age patient with unexplained vaginal bleeding.\",\"A pregnancy test rapidly separates pregnancy-associated differentials such as ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage from many nonpregnancy causes.\",\"Once pregnancy status is known, symptoms, quantitative hCG when appropriate, and transvaginal ultrasound can be integrated to determine location and viability.\"],\"tk\":\"Pregnancy testing is an early essential investigation in reproductive-age patients with unexplained vaginal bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"A negative urine pregnancy test can be falsely reassuring very early in gestation; use serum hCG when clinical suspicion remains high.\",\"tp\":\"Assess hemodynamic stability before pursuing etiologic testing when bleeding is heavy or accompanied by syncope or severe pain.\",\"cn\":\"Pregnancy must be excluded early (urine or serum human chorionic gonadotrophin, hCG) in any reproductive-age woman with abnormal bleeding.\",\"sl\":\"Rule out pregnancy before applying the polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified (PALM-COEIN) classification of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB). Investigations: pregnancy test, complete blood count (CBC), ferritin, thyroid function tests (TFT), +/- coagulation screen, transvaginal ultrasound.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[46,59]],[\"L33_1\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F434\"},{\"id\":\"F435\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Laparoscopic confirmation of endometriosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has chronic cyclical pelvic pain and suspected endometriosis despite nondiagnostic noninvasive evaluation. Which procedure permits direct visualization of pelvic endometriotic lesions and biopsy when needed?\",\"o\":[\"Diagnostic laparoscopy\",\"Plain pelvic radiography\",\"Hysterosalpingography\",\"Computed tomography (CT) of the pelvis\",\"Cervical cytology\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Laparoscopy permits direct pelvic visualization and targeted biopsy of suspicious endometriotic lesions when operative diagnosis is needed.\",\"Plain radiography does not visualize typical endometriotic implants or adhesions.\",\"Hysterosalpingography (HSG) evaluates tubal patency and uterine cavity contour, not peritoneal endometriotic implants.\",\"Computed tomography (CT) has limited sensitivity for superficial endometriosis and is not the preferred modality for direct diagnosis.\",\"Cervical cytology detects cervical epithelial abnormalities and does not evaluate pelvic endometriosis.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometriosis can often be managed from a clinical and imaging diagnosis, but surgery remains useful when direct confirmation or treatment is required.\",\"Laparoscopy permits direct visualization of pelvic implants and biopsy when pathology is necessary.\",\"Endometriosis often involves peritoneal surfaces and ovaries, so direct visualization can identify implants, endometriomas, and adhesions that may be occult on less sensitive tests.\"],\"tk\":\"Laparoscopy allows direct visualization and biopsy of endometriosis when operative confirmation is required.\",\"pr\":\"A normal ultrasound does not exclude superficial peritoneal endometriosis.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay empiric symptom treatment solely to obtain surgical confirmation when the history is typical and there is no urgent surgical indication.\",\"cn\":\"Laparoscopy is the gold standard diagnostic test for endometriosis, allowing direct visualisation and biopsy.\",\"sl\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound (TVS) detects endometrioma, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) assesses extraperitoneal lesions, cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) has specificity 85-90% and sensitivity 20-50%; laparoscopy is the gold standard diagnostic test for endometriosis.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F435\"},{\"id\":\"F436\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Uterine atony risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition increases the risk of uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage?\",\"o\":[\"Short uncomplicated first stage of labor\",\"Oligohydramnios\",\"Small-for-gestational-age fetus\",\"Spontaneous placental separation with a firm uterus\",\"Polyhydramnios\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A brief uncomplicated labor lacks the prolonged myometrial fatigue associated with atony after prolonged or augmented labor.\",\"Reduced amniotic-fluid volume does not create the same uterine overdistension that predisposes to atony.\",\"A small fetus is less likely to overdistend the uterus than macrosomia or multifetal gestation.\",\"A firm contracted uterus after placental delivery indicates effective myometrial compression of uterine vessels, which protects against atony.\",\"Polyhydramnios causes uterine overdistension, which can impair effective myometrial contraction after placental delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of primary postpartum hemorrhage and reflects inadequate myometrial contraction after birth.\",\"Polyhydramnios predisposes by causing uterine overdistension, similar to multifetal gestation and fetal macrosomia.\",\"Normal postpartum contraction compresses the spiral vessels at the placental bed; when the uterus remains relaxed, these vessels continue to bleed because the physiologic living ligature mechanism fails.\"],\"tk\":\"Uterine overdistension from polyhydramnios is a classic risk factor for uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"pr\":\"Other important atony risks include prolonged labor, chorioamnionitis, magnesium exposure, and multiple gestation.\",\"tp\":\"When PPH occurs, palpate uterine tone immediately because a boggy uterus directs rapid treatment toward uterine massage and uterotonics.\",\"cn\":\"Polyhydramnios overdistends the uterus and is a risk factor for uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F436\"},{\"id\":\"F437\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"ICP versus HELLP laboratory features\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which laboratory abnormality can occur in both intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome?\",\"o\":[\"Elevated serum bile acids\",\"Hypertension with proteinuria\",\"Elevated aspartate aminotransferase (AST)\",\"Severe thrombocytopenia\",\"Marked lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) elevation from hemolysis\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Raised bile acids are central to intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and are not a defining laboratory feature of HELLP.\",\"Hypertension and proteinuria support a preeclampsia-spectrum disorder; intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) often occurs without hypertension.\",\"Both ICP and HELLP can produce transaminase elevation, so AST alone does not distinguish the two disorders.\",\"Thrombocytopenia is a defining component of HELLP syndrome and is not a characteristic feature of uncomplicated ICP.\",\"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) elevation from microangiopathic hemolysis supports HELLP rather than isolated intrahepatic cholestasis.\"],\"e\":[\"ICP and HELLP can both cause abnormal hepatic transaminases, but their surrounding laboratory patterns differ.\",\"AST may be elevated in both, whereas high bile acids favor ICP and thrombocytopenia with hemolysis favors HELLP.\",\"ICP reflects impaired hepatobiliary bile transport, while HELLP involves endothelial injury and microangiopathic hemolysis within the pre-eclampsia spectrum.\"],\"tk\":\"Transaminases can rise in both ICP and HELLP; bile acids and platelet/hemolysis findings help distinguish them.\",\"pr\":\"In ICP, fetal risk correlates more closely with serum bile-acid concentration than with the degree of transaminase elevation.\",\"tp\":\"A pregnant patient with pruritus plus hypertension, thrombocytopenia, or hemolysis needs evaluation beyond isolated cholestasis.\",\"cn\":\"Raised aspartate aminotransferase (AST) occurs in both intrahepatic cholestasis and HELLP (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) syndrome.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis: raised alanine aminotransferase (ALT) or aspartate aminotransferase (AST), gamma GT and bile acids. HELLP is haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets; severe pre-eclampsia labs include AST/ALT more than double the upper limit of normal.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[17]],[\"L01_0\",[38,56]],[\"L22_0\",[46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F437\"},{\"id\":\"F438\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic inlet conjugate diameters\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet is the narrowest and represents the shortest distance available for passage of the fetal head?\",\"o\":[\"External conjugate\",\"Anatomical (true) conjugate\",\"Diagonal conjugate\",\"Transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet\",\"Obstetric conjugate\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The external conjugate is measured between external landmarks and is substantially longer than internal pelvic inlet measurements.\",\"The anatomical conjugate extends from the sacral promontory to the superior border of the pubic symphysis and is slightly longer than the functional AP inlet diameter.\",\"The diagonal conjugate extends to the inferior border of the pubic symphysis and can be estimated by vaginal examination.\",\"The transverse inlet diameter spans the widest lateral distance across the pelvic brim and is not an anteroposterior conjugate.\",\"The obstetric conjugate is the shortest anteroposterior inlet diameter, extending from the sacral promontory to the nearest point on the posterior pubic symphysis.\"],\"e\":[\"The clinically important inlet AP diameter is the obstetric conjugate, which defines the narrowest fixed AP space through which the presenting part must pass.\",\"It is shorter than the diagonal and anatomical conjugates; the diagonal conjugate is the one accessible on vaginal examination.\",\"Because the obstetric conjugate cannot be measured directly in routine examination, clinicians estimate it from the diagonal conjugate to assess pelvic inlet adequacy.\"],\"tk\":\"The obstetric conjugate is the narrowest anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet.\",\"pr\":\"The obstetric conjugate is commonly estimated as roughly 1.5–2 cm less than the diagonal conjugate.\",\"tp\":\"Use pelvic measurements only as part of the overall labor assessment; clinical progress is more informative than isolated pelvimetry values.\",\"cn\":\"The obstetric conjugate is the shortest anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet through which the fetal head must pass.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F134\"},{\"id\":\"F439\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Routine booking investigations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In an otherwise low-risk pregnancy, which test is NOT routinely performed at the initial booking visit and is instead offered later to women at risk of gestational diabetes?\",\"o\":[\"Full blood count\",\"Urinalysis for protein assessment\",\"75-g oral glucose tolerance test\",\"ABO/rhesus D (RhD) blood group and red-cell antibody screen\",\"Blood pressure measurement\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A booking full blood count screens for anemia and hematologic abnormalities early in pregnancy.\",\"Urine assessment at booking helps identify proteinuria or urinary abnormalities that may alter antenatal evaluation.\",\"For women with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) risk factors, the oral glucose tolerance test is generally performed at 24–28 weeks, rather than as a routine test for every patient at booking.\",\"Blood group, rhesus D (RhD) status, and antibody screening are standard early antenatal blood tests.\",\"Blood pressure is recorded at the booking visit and subsequent routine antenatal visits to screen for chronic and gestational hypertension.\"],\"e\":[\"Booking care establishes baseline maternal health and pregnancy risk for the remainder of antenatal care.\",\"Routine early assessment includes blood pressure, urine testing, blood group/antibody testing, and a full blood count; a diagnostic OGTT is generally scheduled at 24–28 weeks for patients with gestational-diabetes risk factors.\",\"Gestational insulin resistance rises as placental hormones increase, so later testing improves detection of pregnancy-induced dysglycemia.\"],\"tk\":\"A routine OGTT is not a universal booking test; risk-based testing is usually performed at 24–28 weeks.\",\"pr\":\"A history of previous gestational diabetes can justify earlier glucose testing than the standard 24–28-week window.\",\"tp\":\"At booking, document GDM risk factors so patients who need later OGTT are scheduled before the testing window is missed.\",\"cn\":\"The 75-g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) is not a booking test; it is offered at 24-28 weeks for risk factors.\",\"sl\":\"Booking investigations: complete blood count (CBC), blood group, urine analysis and culture, hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), rubella IgG and screening for sexually transmitted diseases. The 75-g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) is offered at 24 to 28 weeks to women with any risk factor.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[10]],[\"L10_0\",[28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F112\"},{\"id\":\"F440\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Timing of fetal anomaly screening\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about routine fetal structural anomaly screening is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Ultrasound is central to structural-anomaly screening\",\"Screening can identify markers or anomalies associated with chromosomal disorders\",\"Abnormal screening may lead to diagnostic testing such as amniocentesis\",\"Open neural-tube and abdominal-wall defects may be detected on screening\",\"The detailed fetal anomaly ultrasound is routinely performed at 15 weeks\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The mid-trimester scan evaluates fetal anatomy and placental location using standardized ultrasound views.\",\"Ultrasound findings can alter estimated risk for aneuploidy and may prompt diagnostic testing.\",\"Amniocentesis can obtain fetal cells for karyotyping, chromosomal microarray, or targeted genetic testing after appropriate counseling.\",\"Structural ultrasound can identify spina bifida and ventral-wall defects when views are adequate.\",\"Routine structural anomaly ultrasound is performed later; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends 18+0 to 20+6 weeks for the fetal anomaly scan.\"],\"e\":[\"Structural fetal screening is timed to optimize anatomic visualization in the mid-trimester.\",\"A routine detailed anomaly scan at 15 weeks is too early for the standard mid-trimester program; NICE recommends 18+0 to 20+6 weeks.\",\"Screening estimates risk and detects structural abnormalities, whereas invasive tests such as amniocentesis provide diagnostic genetic information when indicated.\"],\"tk\":\"Routine detailed fetal anomaly ultrasound is performed in the mid-trimester, not at 15 weeks.\",\"pr\":\"A normal anomaly scan reduces the likelihood of many major structural abnormalities but cannot exclude every congenital condition.\",\"tp\":\"If views are incomplete because of fetal position or technical limitations, arrange repeat imaging according to the local fetal-screening pathway.\",\"cn\":\"The routine detailed fetal anomaly scan is performed in the mid-trimester (about 18 to 21 weeks), not at 15 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"In diabetes, an ultrasound scan at 18 to 21 weeks to detect fetal structural anomalies, including the fetal heart, should be offered; in twins the detailed anomaly scan is at about 20 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[14]],[\"L23_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F081\"},{\"id\":\"F441\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":44,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Uterine fibroid presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 38-year-old woman has heavy menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure, and intermittent hypogastric pain. Examination shows a firm, irregularly enlarged uterus. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Adenomyosis\",\"Uterine leiomyoma (fibroid)\",\"Pelvic inflammatory disease\",\"Endometriosis\",\"Simple ovarian cyst\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Adenomyosis can cause heavy painful periods but classically gives a diffusely enlarged, globular, tender uterus rather than an irregular nodular contour.\",\"Fibroids commonly cause heavy menstrual bleeding, pressure symptoms, and an irregularly enlarged firm uterus in reproductive-age patients.\",\"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) more often causes cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness with infectious risk factors, discharge, or fever.\",\"Endometriosis often causes cyclical pelvic pain, deep dyspareunia, dyschezia, and infertility rather than a firm irregular uterine enlargement.\",\"An ovarian cyst produces an adnexal mass rather than the characteristic irregularly enlarged uterine contour described here.\"],\"e\":[\"Leiomyomas are benign uterine smooth-muscle tumors that can produce bleeding or bulk symptoms depending on size and location.\",\"Heavy menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure, and a firm irregularly enlarged uterus strongly favor fibroids.\",\"Submucosal lesions distort the endometrial cavity and promote bleeding, while larger intramural or subserosal tumors create mass-effect symptoms.\"],\"tk\":\"Heavy bleeding plus a firm irregularly enlarged uterus is a classic clinical pattern of uterine leiomyomas.\",\"pr\":\"Submucosal fibroids are disproportionately associated with heavy menstrual bleeding even when their total size is modest.\",\"tp\":\"Use pelvic ultrasound to define fibroid number, size, and location before choosing medical, hysteroscopic, or surgical management.\",\"cn\":\"Heavy menstrual bleeding with pressure symptoms and a firm, irregularly enlarged uterus suggests uterine fibroids (leiomyoma).\",\"sl\":\"Bimanual examination: enlarged uterus suggests fibroids or adenomyosis. Adenomyosis gives a symmetrically enlarged uterus that may be tender. Lecture 44 has no slides (study from Hacker and Moore chapter 19).\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[55]],[\"L38_0\",[48]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F441\"},{\"id\":\"F442\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension and pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which pre-existing condition carries such a high maternal risk in pregnancy that pre-pregnancy counseling should strongly discourage pregnancy and involve a specialist pulmonary-hypertension pregnancy team?\",\"o\":[\"Mild intermittent asthma\",\"Type 1 diabetes with good preconception control and no end-organ disease\",\"Well-controlled chronic hypertension\",\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension\",\"Treated hypothyroidism with euthyroid thyroid function\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Well-controlled mild asthma usually has a favorable pregnancy course; maintaining maternal oxygenation is more important than withholding indicated inhaled therapy.\",\"Pregnancy in type 1 diabetes is high risk but can often proceed with intensive glycemic and multidisciplinary care.\",\"Chronic hypertension increases risks such as superimposed pre-eclampsia and fetal growth restriction, but pregnancy is not categorically discouraged when disease is appropriately managed.\",\"Pregnancy in pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) carries very high risk of right-heart failure, hemodynamic decompensation, and maternal death, especially around delivery and early postpartum.\",\"Adequately treated hypothyroidism is compatible with pregnancy when thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is monitored and levothyroxine adjusted as needed.\"],\"e\":[\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a very-high-risk cardiovascular condition in pregnancy.\",\"The marked increase in plasma volume and cardiac output can precipitate right-ventricular failure and maternal decompensation, so pregnancy requires specialist counseling and may be strongly discouraged.\",\"PAH fixes pulmonary vascular resistance at an abnormally high level; the right ventricle may fail when pregnancy and delivery impose major hemodynamic stress.\"],\"tk\":\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension carries very high maternal risk in pregnancy and requires specialist pre-pregnancy counseling.\",\"pr\":\"The early postpartum period remains dangerous because abrupt volume shifts can worsen right-ventricular loading conditions.\",\"tp\":\"Refer patients with PAH who are considering pregnancy to a multidisciplinary pregnancy-heart team before conception whenever possible.\",\"cn\":\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension carries very high maternal mortality; pregnancy is discouraged and termination is offered.\",\"sl\":\"Indications for termination of pregnancy: primary pulmonary hypertension, Eisenmenger syndrome, coarctation of aorta, Marfan syndrome with dilated aortic root.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F442\"},{\"id\":\"F443\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":1,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"RhD sensitizing events\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In an unsensitized RhD-negative pregnant patient, which procedure is not considered a fetomaternal-hemorrhage sensitizing event?\",\"o\":[\"Amniocentesis\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound\",\"Chorionic villus sampling\",\"External cephalic version\",\"Significant abdominal trauma\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Amniocentesis traverses the membranes with a needle and can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage, so RhD-negative unsensitized patients may require anti-D prophylaxis.\",\"A routine transvaginal ultrasound is noninvasive and does not breach placental or fetal vessels; it is not expected to cause clinically important fetomaternal hemorrhage.\",\"Chorionic villus sampling directly samples placental tissue and can expose the maternal circulation to RhD-positive fetal erythrocytes.\",\"External cephalic version can produce occult fetomaternal hemorrhage through placental shear and is a recognized indication for anti-D in eligible patients.\",\"Blunt abdominal trauma may disrupt the placental interface and permit fetal red cells to enter maternal blood.\"],\"e\":[\"Rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization occurs when fetal RhD-positive erythrocytes enter the circulation of an RhD-negative patient and stimulate anti-D antibodies.\",\"Invasive procedures such as amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling (CVS), external cephalic version, and significant abdominal trauma can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage; routine transvaginal ultrasound does not disrupt the placental interface.\",\"Prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin clears susceptible fetal RhD-positive cells before a durable maternal immune response develops.\"],\"tk\":\"Anti-D prophylaxis is considered after clinically important sensitizing events in an unsensitized RhD-negative pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"A transvaginal scan is safe in early pregnancy and does not increase miscarriage risk simply because the probe is placed vaginally.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm maternal RhD status and sensitization status before deciding whether anti-D is indicated after an invasive procedure or bleeding event.\",\"cn\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound is not a sensitizing event; amniocentesis, chorionic villus sampling, external cephalic version and abdominal trauma are.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F399\"},{\"id\":\"F444\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Anti-D prophylaxis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization and anti-D prophylaxis is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Anti-D reverses established maternal anti-D alloimmunization\",\"Routine anti-D is given to RhD-positive patients\",\"Anti-D treats fetal anemia caused by hemolytic disease\",\"Anti-D prevents all causes of hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn\",\"Anti-D is offered after sensitizing events to unsensitized RhD-negative patients\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Once immune anti-D antibodies are already present, prophylactic anti-D cannot erase maternal immunologic memory; the pregnancy instead requires fetal-anemia surveillance.\",\"An RhD-positive patient already expresses the D antigen and does not require prophylaxis against anti-D alloimmunization.\",\"Established fetal anemia from red-cell alloimmunization is assessed with middle cerebral artery Doppler and may require intrauterine transfusion, rather than prophylactic anti-D.\",\"Anti-D specifically targets exposure to the rhesus D (RhD) antigen; hemolytic disease can also result from non-D red-cell antibodies such as anti-c or anti-K.\",\"Prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin is used in an unsensitized RhD-negative pregnancy after qualifying sensitizing events to reduce alloimmunization.\"],\"e\":[\"Anti-D immunoglobulin is prevention, not treatment, for RhD alloimmunization.\",\"The key eligible patient is RhD-negative and unsensitized with a qualifying exposure to potentially RhD-positive fetal cells.\",\"Passive anti-D promotes clearance of fetal erythrocytes before maternal B-cell sensitization produces persistent IgG anti-D.\"],\"tk\":\"Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents RhD alloimmunization only before maternal sensitization has occurred.\",\"pr\":\"A positive antibody screen for immune anti-D changes management from prophylaxis to assessment of antibody level and fetal anemia risk.\",\"tp\":\"Check the antibody screen before interpreting whether a recorded anti-D result represents prophylaxis or true alloimmunization.\",\"cn\":\"Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents sensitisation and is given after sensitising events only to non-sensitised RhD-negative women; it does not treat established alloimmunisation.\",\"sl\":\"Anti-D immunoglobulin (Ig) should be given to all non-sensitised RhD-negative women after any presentation with antepartum haemorrhage (APH), independent of routine antenatal prophylaxis; after 20 weeks at least 500 IU followed by a test for fetomaternal haemorrhage (FMH) greater than 4 mL.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F444\"},{\"id\":\"F445\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pathophysiology of Rh disease\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the immunologic basis of Rh hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn?\",\"o\":[\"A maternal IgM response that readily crosses the placenta\",\"Placental insufficiency without an immune response\",\"Maternal IgG antibody formation against a fetal red-cell isoantigen\",\"A fetal T-cell attack on maternal erythrocytes\",\"Maternal complement deficiency causing fetal anemia\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Primary IgM antibodies are large pentamers and do not efficiently cross the placenta; clinically important fetal hemolysis is mediated by IgG.\",\"Placental insufficiency causes uteroplacental hypoperfusion and fetal growth problems, not antibody-mediated extravascular hemolysis.\",\"Maternal exposure to a foreign fetal red-cell isoantigen can generate class-switched IgG antibodies that cross the placenta and hemolyze antigen-positive fetal cells.\",\"The pathogenic immune response is maternal humoral immunity directed at fetal erythrocyte antigens, not fetal T-cell cytotoxicity against maternal cells.\",\"Complement deficiency predisposes to selected infections and immune disorders; Rh disease depends on maternal alloantibody binding to fetal red cells.\"],\"e\":[\"Rh disease is an alloimmune hemolytic anemia caused by maternal antibodies directed against fetal erythrocyte antigens.\",\"After sensitization, maternal IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and binds RhD-positive fetal cells, producing progressive hemolysis and possibly hydrops.\",\"Severe anemia raises fetal cardiac output and can culminate in high-output heart failure, which explains the role of middle cerebral artery (MCA) Doppler surveillance.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal IgG alloantibodies can cross the placenta and destroy antigen-positive fetal erythrocytes.\",\"pr\":\"The fetal middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity rises with significant anemia because blood viscosity falls and cardiac output increases.\",\"tp\":\"In an alloimmunized pregnancy, follow the antibody-specific surveillance pathway rather than giving anti-D as if sensitization had not occurred.\",\"cn\":\"Rh haemolytic disease results from maternal IgG antibodies against a fetal red-cell antigen that cross the placenta and cause haemolysis.\",\"sl\":\"After fetomaternal haemorrhage, maternal B-lymphocytes form antibodies to foreign fetal red cell antigens; on re-exposure a large mass of IgG is produced, readily crosses the placenta, attaches to fetal erythrocytes and causes extravascular haemolysis and anaemia.\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[7,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F445\"},{\"id\":\"F446\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"VTE in pregnancy and puerperium\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about pregnancy-associated venous thromboembolism is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"The postpartum period is the lowest-risk period for pregnancy-associated venous thromboembolism (VTE)\",\"Persistent high suspicion after a negative leg ultrasound warrants repeat imaging\",\"Deep-vein thrombosis can embolize to the pulmonary circulation\",\"Surgery and immobilization increase pregnancy-associated VTE risk\",\"Low-molecular-weight heparin is commonly used for anticoagulation in pregnancy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The postpartum period has a particularly high VTE incidence because hypercoagulability persists while delivery-related vascular injury and immobility add to Virchow triad risk.\",\"When compression ultrasonography is negative but clinical suspicion remains high, Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) advises repeat scanning on days 3 and 7.\",\"A proximal deep-vein thrombosis can dislodge and produce pulmonary embolism, a major direct cause of maternal morbidity and death.\",\"Surgery and prolonged immobility amplify venous stasis and endothelial injury in an already hypercoagulable pregnancy.\",\"Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) has predictable anticoagulant activity and does not cross the placenta, making it a standard agent for pregnancy-associated VTE.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy and the puerperium create a hypercoagulable state, and thrombotic risk does not end at delivery.\",\"Risk is especially important in the postpartum period, when cesarean birth, hemorrhage, infection, immobility, and inherited or acquired thrombophilia may compound baseline risk.\",\"A negative initial compression scan does not completely exclude deep vein thrombosis (DVT) when suspicion is high; serial ultrasound addresses initially nonvisualized or evolving thrombosis.\"],\"tk\":\"The puerperium is a high-risk period for venous thromboembolism, not a protected period.\",\"pr\":\"RCOG recommends repeat compression ultrasound on days 3 and 7 if the first study is negative but DVT suspicion remains high.\",\"tp\":\"Reassess VTE risk after delivery because risk factors can change rapidly with cesarean birth, hemorrhage, infection, or reduced mobility.\",\"cn\":\"The puerperium is the highest-risk period for venous thromboembolism (VTE), about 20-fold, compared with 4-5-fold antenatally.\",\"sl\":\"Pregnant women are 4 to 5 times more likely to experience venous thromboembolism (VTE); risk is further increased in the puerperium to 20 times (highest risk). If compression ultrasound is negative with high clinical suspicion, repeat it on days 3 and 7.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[2,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F446\"},{\"id\":\"F447\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"PALM-COEIN classification\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Using the PALM-COEIN classification (polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified), which cause of abnormal uterine bleeding is nonstructural?\",\"o\":[\"Endometrial polyp\",\"Adenomyosis\",\"Leiomyoma\",\"Ovulatory dysfunction\",\"Endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A uterine polyp is a focal structural lesion included in PALM and is often evaluated with ultrasound or hysteroscopy.\",\"Adenomyosis is a structural uterine disorder in the PALM group and may cause heavy painful menses with a globular tender uterus.\",\"A leiomyoma is a structural myometrial tumor in PALM; submucosal lesions are particularly associated with heavy bleeding.\",\"Ovulatory dysfunction belongs to the COEIN nonstructural group and often produces irregular, unpredictable bleeding from disordered endometrial exposure to sex steroids.\",\"Endometrial hyperplasia or malignancy constitutes the M category of PALM and requires risk-based tissue assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) PALM-COEIN system separates structural from nonstructural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"PALM comprises polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, and malignancy/hyperplasia, whereas ovulatory dysfunction belongs to COEIN.\",\"Anovulation creates prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure followed by unstable endometrial shedding, explaining irregular bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Ovulatory dysfunction is a nonstructural COEIN cause of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"AUB evaluation should still include a pregnancy test and anemia assessment before the classification is finalized.\",\"tp\":\"Use ultrasound when a PALM lesion is suspected and add endometrial sampling when age or risk factors raise concern for hyperplasia or cancer.\",\"cn\":\"In the PALM-COEIN classification (polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified), ovulatory dysfunction is a non-structural cause of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB).\",\"sl\":\"Structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB): polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia; non-structural: coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic and not yet classified.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[46,47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F447\"},{\"id\":\"F448\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding causes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding?\",\"o\":[\"Endometrial carcinoma\",\"Genitourinary or endometrial atrophy\",\"Cervical carcinoma\",\"Endometrial polyp\",\"Submucosal leiomyoma\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometrial carcinoma is an important diagnosis to exclude because postmenopausal bleeding is a common presenting symptom; risk rises with advancing age and unopposed estrogen exposure.\",\"Estrogen deficiency causes endometrial and vaginal atrophy, leaving thin fragile epithelium that can bleed with minimal trauma and accounts for many PMB presentations.\",\"Cervical carcinoma can cause contact or spontaneous bleeding; cervical inspection and screening history help identify cervical pathology.\",\"An endometrial polyp can cause intermittent bleeding and may be detected by transvaginal ultrasound or hysteroscopy.\",\"A submucosal fibroid can distort the cavity and bleed, but fibroids typically become less hormonally active after menopause unless stimulated by exogenous hormones.\"],\"e\":[\"The commonest benign explanation for postmenopausal bleeding is atrophic endometrium or vaginal epithelium due to estrogen deficiency.\",\"Even when atrophy is likely, postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation because endometrial cancer may present with the same symptom.\",\"Loss of estrogen thins the epithelium and reduces vascular support, making tissue friable and prone to microtrauma.\"],\"tk\":\"Atrophy is common in postmenopausal bleeding, but endometrial malignancy must still be excluded.\",\"pr\":\"A thin endometrium on transvaginal ultrasound is reassuring only within an appropriate risk-based PMB pathway; persistent or recurrent bleeding still needs reassessment.\",\"tp\":\"Document cervical findings and medication exposure, then follow the local PMB pathway for ultrasound and endometrial sampling.\",\"cn\":\"Endometrial or vaginal atrophy is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding (PMB), though malignancy must be excluded.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of vaginal bleeding in postmenopausal women: endometrial atrophy 30%, polyps 30%, submucosal fibroids 20%, hyperplasia 8-15%, endometrial carcinoma 8-10%.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[48]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F182\"},{\"id\":\"F449\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An 18-year-old with primary amenorrhea has normal female external genitalia, low body mass index, intensive athletic training, and low-normal follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH). What is the most likely cause?\",\"o\":[\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"Primary ovarian insufficiency\",\"Müllerian agenesis\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Energy deficiency and physiologic stress suppress pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) secretion, producing hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and amenorrhea in susceptible adolescents.\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) usually features hyperandrogenism and chronic ovulatory dysfunction; gonadotropins are not typically globally suppressed by low energy availability.\",\"Primary ovarian insufficiency causes hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with elevated follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) because ovarian estrogen production is reduced.\",\"Müllerian agenesis causes primary amenorrhea with normal ovarian estrogen production and breast development but congenital absence of the uterus and upper vagina.\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity presents in a 46,XY individual with absent uterus, female external genitalia, breast development, and sparse or absent pubic hair.\"],\"e\":[\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea reflects reduced GnRH pulse generation from inadequate energy availability, excessive exercise, psychological stress, or a combination.\",\"The low body mass index (BMI), high exercise load, and low-normal gonadotropins indicate hypogonadotropic hypogonadism rather than primary ovarian failure.\",\"Reduced GnRH lowers LH and FSH stimulation of the ovary, decreasing estradiol production and interrupting endometrial cycling.\"],\"tk\":\"Low energy availability can suppress hypothalamic GnRH and cause hypogonadotropic amenorrhea in adolescents.\",\"pr\":\"Prolonged hypoestrogenism can reduce bone mineral density, so treatment must address nutrition and exercise rather than only menstrual timing.\",\"tp\":\"Screen for disordered eating, excessive exercise, stress, and fracture history while excluding pregnancy and other endocrine causes of amenorrhea.\",\"cn\":\"Low body weight and intense exercise with low or normal gonadotropins cause functional hypothalamic (hypogonadotropic) amenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (low follicle-stimulating hormone, FSH) includes anorexia nervosa and excessive exercise. Hypothalamic amenorrhoea has normal or low FSH and luteinizing hormone (LH) and is caused by extremes of weight loss, stress or exercise.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[10]],[\"L29_0\",[4,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F449\"},{\"id\":\"F450\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding evaluation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 64-year-old presents with a first episode of postmenopausal bleeding. Which statement is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Endometrial cancer risk rises with advancing age, so malignancy must be considered\",\"Endometrial cancer is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding\",\"Every patient with postmenopausal bleeding requires immediate hysterectomy\",\"A normal cervical examination excludes endometrial disease\",\"A single episode of postmenopausal bleeding can be ignored if painless\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The incidence of endometrial carcinoma rises with age, and any new postmenopausal bleeding warrants evaluation for endometrial pathology.\",\"Most postmenopausal bleeding (PMB) is due to benign causes such as atrophy; cancer is less common but clinically important because bleeding is a frequent early symptom.\",\"Initial evaluation is usually diagnostic, using transvaginal ultrasound and/or endometrial sampling according to risk rather than proceeding directly to surgery.\",\"The cervix and endometrium are separate anatomic sites; a normal cervix cannot exclude endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma.\",\"Painless bleeding can be the presenting feature of endometrial malignancy, so absence of pain does not make evaluation unnecessary.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal bleeding is a red-flag symptom because endometrial carcinoma becomes more prevalent with advancing age.\",\"Although atrophy is common, the clinical priority is to exclude endometrial neoplasia rather than assuming a benign cause from a painless single episode.\",\"Endometrial cancer often produces bleeding while still confined to the uterus because tumor tissue disrupts fragile endometrial vessels.\"],\"tk\":\"Any postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation for endometrial pathology even when the episode is painless.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent or recurrent PMB deserves renewed assessment even after a previously reassuring test because new focal pathology can develop.\",\"tp\":\"Record age, obesity, diabetes, tamoxifen or estrogen exposure, and bleeding recurrence when choosing between ultrasound and tissue sampling.\",\"cn\":\"In postmenopausal bleeding, the probability that endometrial cancer is the cause increases with age after menopause.\",\"sl\":\"All postmenopausal women with uterine bleeding must be evaluated for endometrial cancer; 20% will have a malignant genital neoplasm. Probability that the bleeding is caused by endometrial cancer increases with age after menopause.\",\"s\":[[\"L42_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F039\"},{\"id\":\"F451\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Secondary amenorrhea after pregnancy exclusion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After pregnancy has been excluded, which condition is a common endocrine cause of secondary amenorrhea in a reproductive-age patient?\",\"o\":[\"Primary ovarian insufficiency\",\"Hyperprolactinemia\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\",\"Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea\",\"Intrauterine adhesions\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. Primary ovarian insufficiency (amenorrhea with raised follicle-stimulating hormone [FSH] before age 40) is a recognized cause but much less common than polycystic ovary syndrome.\",\"Wrong. Hyperprolactinemia suppresses gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) and causes amenorrhea, but it is less common than polycystic ovary syndrome.\",\"Correct. Once pregnancy is excluded, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is the most common endocrine cause of secondary amenorrhea, through chronic anovulation, often with hyperandrogenism.\",\"Wrong. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea from stress, weight loss or exercise is an important cause, but polycystic ovary syndrome is more common in the general reproductive-age population.\",\"Wrong. Intrauterine adhesions (Asherman syndrome) cause amenorrhea through endometrial scarring, which is an anatomical cause, not an endocrine one.\"],\"e\":[\"Once pregnancy is excluded, PCOS is a common cause of secondary amenorrhea due to chronic ovulatory dysfunction.\",\"The history should look for hyperandrogenism, weight change, galactorrhea, exercise, stress, and uterine procedures because these separate major alternatives.\",\"In PCOS, disordered follicular development prevents regular ovulation, so cyclic progesterone exposure and predictable withdrawal bleeding are absent.\"],\"tk\":\"Pregnancy is excluded first in secondary amenorrhea; PCOS is then a common endocrine cause of chronic anovulation.\",\"pr\":\"Amenorrhea with prolonged anovulation can expose the endometrium to unopposed estrogen, increasing hyperplasia risk.\",\"tp\":\"Order thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and prolactin alongside targeted gonadotropin testing when the clinical phenotype does not clearly establish the cause.\",\"cn\":\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a common endocrine (anovulatory) cause of secondary amenorrhea once pregnancy is excluded.\",\"sl\":\"Secondary amenorrhoea work-up: thyroid function and prolactin (hyperthyroidism and hyperprolactinaemia cause amenorrhoea), progestogen challenge; withdrawal bleeding indicates anovulation such as polycystic ovarian disease (PCOS); hypothalamic amenorrhoea follows weight loss, stress or exercise.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[12,13,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: The key (C, polycystic ovary syndrome, PCOS) is defensible as the commonest anovulatory cause, but hyperprolactinaemia (B) and functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea (D) are also common endocrine causes listed on L29 pages 12 and 16, so the stem does not discriminate.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F451\"},{\"id\":\"F452\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Initial stabilization during seizure\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient is actively having a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. What is the immediate management priority?\",\"o\":[\"Obtain brain computed tomography (CT) before any bedside intervention\",\"Perform an emergency cesarean delivery immediately\",\"Measure serum prolactin as the first action\",\"Protect the airway and stabilize maternal breathing and circulation\",\"Start oral antiepileptic maintenance therapy\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Neuroimaging may be needed for atypical seizures, but maternal stabilization precedes transport for computed tomography (CT) in an actively convulsing patient.\",\"Delivery may be indicated after stabilization in eclampsia, but uncontrolled convulsion first requires resuscitation and seizure treatment.\",\"A serum prolactin level is not a resuscitation test; it does not address immediate hypoxemia or aspiration risk.\",\"Active convulsions threaten airway patency, oxygenation, and aspiration, so immediate care begins with ABC stabilization and safe positioning.\",\"Oral maintenance therapy has a delayed onset and is unsuitable during an active convulsion requiring rapid seizure control.\"],\"e\":[\"An actively seizing pregnant patient requires immediate airway, breathing, and circulation support while injury is prevented.\",\"Maternal hypoxemia can rapidly compromise uteroplacental oxygen delivery, so stabilization comes before imaging or delivery decisions.\",\"If the seizure is due to eclampsia, magnesium sulfate is then used for seizure treatment and prevention while severe hypertension and delivery planning are addressed.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal ABC stabilization is the first priority during an active seizure in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent seizure activity after initial therapy raises concern for status epilepticus, which requires escalation of airway and anticonvulsant management.\",\"tp\":\"Place the patient in a lateral position when feasible, provide oxygen, protect from injury, and obtain help while preparing cause-specific seizure treatment.\",\"cn\":\"First step for a seizing pregnant woman is airway, breathing and circulation (ABC) management with aspiration and injury prevention.\",\"sl\":\"Eclampsia management: appropriate management of ABCs (airway, breathing, circulation) with measures to avoid aspiration, seizure control with 6 g magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) intravenous (IV) bolus, prevent maternal injury and control severe hypertension.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[54]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F452\"},{\"id\":\"F453\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ovarian tumor classification\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about ovarian tumors is correct?\",\"o\":[\"The Risk of Malignancy Index is calculated from cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) alone\",\"Serous, mucinous, endometrioid, and clear-cell carcinomas are epithelial ovarian tumors\",\"Thecoma is the classic ovarian tumor associated with Meigs syndrome\",\"Dysgerminoma is characterized by marked alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) secretion\",\"Germ-cell tumors are the most common ovarian malignancy in older adults\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Risk of malignancy index (RMI) combines cancer antigen 125 (CA-125), ultrasound features, and menopausal status rather than relying on a single serum marker.\",\"These histologies arise from the epithelial ovarian tumor group and comprise major subtypes of epithelial ovarian carcinoma.\",\"Classic Meigs syndrome consists of ovarian fibroma, ascites, and pleural effusion; the association is not defined by thecoma.\",\"Dysgerminoma is more closely associated with lactate dehydrogenase (LDH); markedly elevated alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) suggests a yolk sac tumor or mixed germ-cell component.\",\"In older adults, epithelial malignancies predominate, whereas germ-cell tumors are concentrated in younger patients.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovarian neoplasms are broadly classified as epithelial, germ-cell, or sex-cord stromal tumors.\",\"Serous, mucinous, endometrioid, and clear-cell carcinomas all belong to the epithelial category.\",\"Histologic lineage helps predict age distribution, hormone production, and tumor-marker patterns, which is why LDH, AFP, inhibin, and CA-125 have different roles.\"],\"tk\":\"Serous, mucinous, endometrioid, and clear-cell carcinomas are epithelial ovarian tumors.\",\"pr\":\"A markedly elevated AFP in a young patient with an adnexal mass should raise concern for a yolk sac component rather than pure dysgerminoma.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret tumor markers in context of age, imaging morphology, and suspected histology rather than using a single marker as a diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Serous, mucinous, endometrioid and clear-cell carcinomas are epithelial ovarian tumours, which make up about 90% of ovarian cancers.\",\"sl\":\"Epithelial tumours (90%): serous 75%, endometrioid 10%, clear cell about 7%, mucinous about 5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F033\"},{\"id\":\"F454\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Hegar sign in early pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Softening and compressibility of the lower uterine segment in early pregnancy is known as which sign?\",\"o\":[\"Goodell sign\",\"Chadwick sign\",\"Cullen sign\",\"Piskacek sign\",\"Hegar sign\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The Goodell sign refers to softening of the cervix, not the lower uterine segment.\",\"The Chadwick sign is a bluish-purple discoloration of the cervix and vaginal mucosa from increased vascularity.\",\"The Cullen sign is periumbilical ecchymosis associated with intra-abdominal bleeding, classically pancreatitis or ruptured ectopic pregnancy.\",\"The Piskacek sign describes asymmetric uterine enlargement in early pregnancy from implantation-related growth.\",\"The Hegar sign is softening of the uterine isthmus/lower segment in early pregnancy, making the cervix and uterine body feel unusually separated on bimanual examination.\"],\"e\":[\"Hegar sign is an early pregnancy finding caused by softening of the lower uterine segment.\",\"The original wording describing a “tender uterus” is inaccurate; the defining examination finding is softness and compressibility of the isthmus.\",\"Progesterone, increased vascularity, and remodeling of uterine connective tissue produce early uterine softening before marked abdominal enlargement.\"],\"tk\":\"Hegar sign means softening of the lower uterine segment, not uterine tenderness.\",\"pr\":\"Goodell, Chadwick, and Hegar signs are supportive historical examination findings; pregnancy is confirmed with human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) testing and ultrasound when clinically indicated.\",\"tp\":\"Do not use bimanual signs alone to date or confirm pregnancy when biochemical testing and ultrasound are available.\",\"cn\":\"Hegar's sign is softening and compressibility of the uterine isthmus on bimanual examination at 6-8 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Hegar's sign: softening of the uterine isthmus, resulting in its compressibility on bimanual examination; observed by the 6th to 8th week. Goodell's sign is cervical softening; Chadwick's sign is bluish discolouration.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[44]],[\"L22_0\",[56]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F454\"},{\"id\":\"F455\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Suspicious ovarian cyst morphology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which ultrasound feature is reassuring rather than typical of a complex or suspicious ovarian cyst?\",\"o\":[\"Papillary projections\",\"Solid vascular components\",\"Ascites\",\"Multilocularity with thick septa\",\"Thin-walled unilocular anechoic cyst without a solid component\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Papillary projections represent mural proliferations and increase concern for epithelial neoplasia.\",\"A cyst with solid vascular tissue is morphologically complex and requires risk assessment for malignancy.\",\"Ascites with an adnexal mass may reflect peritoneal involvement or fluid accumulation, increasing concern for malignancy or, less commonly, a benign syndrome such as Meigs.\",\"Multilocularity and thick septations are complex sonographic features that prompt closer assessment than a simple cyst.\",\"A simple unilocular anechoic cyst with a thin wall and no solid element has a low-risk morphology, especially in a premenopausal patient.\"],\"e\":[\"A simple ovarian cyst is typically unilocular, anechoic, thin-walled, and free of solid components.\",\"Papillary tissue, solid vascular areas, thick septa, and ascites are complex or suspicious features that change risk stratification.\",\"Malignant epithelial tumors develop proliferative tissue and abnormal vascularity, producing solid or papillary morphology rather than a purely fluid-filled cavity.\"],\"tk\":\"Simple cyst morphology is reassuring; solid, papillary, thick-septated, or ascitic features increase concern.\",\"pr\":\"Menopausal status changes the pretest probability of malignancy, so the same cyst morphology carries different clinical weight before and after menopause.\",\"tp\":\"Document cyst size, locularity, septa, solid areas, vascularity, and ascites rather than reporting only the diameter.\",\"cn\":\"A thin-walled unilocular anechoic cyst without solid areas is a simple, reassuring cyst; papillary projections, solid areas, septations and ascites are complex.\",\"sl\":\"Complex cysts: ascites, bilateral lesions, septations, solid components/papillary projections, metastases.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F455\"},{\"id\":\"F456\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman 6 weeks after vaginal delivery has urine leakage when coughing and sneezing. What is the most appropriate initial management?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate mid-urethral sling surgery\",\"Antimuscarinic therapy\",\"Supervised pelvic-floor muscle training\",\"Long-term indwelling catheterization\",\"Routine systemic estrogen therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A mid-urethral sling improves continence by reinforcing urethral support; surgery is reserved for persistent bothersome symptoms after conservative treatment.\",\"Antimuscarinics reduce detrusor overactivity and are used for urgency symptoms rather than leakage specifically triggered by cough or exertion.\",\"First-line therapy for stress incontinence includes pelvic-floor muscle training for at least several weeks to improve urethral support and sphincter function.\",\"An indwelling catheter is reserved for selected urinary retention or drainage indications and does not rehabilitate stress incontinence.\",\"Systemic estrogen is not first-line therapy for postpartum stress incontinence; treatment targets pelvic-floor support and modifiable factors.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence is leakage with rises in intra-abdominal pressure, such as coughing or sneezing.\",\"At 6 weeks postpartum, first-line management is pelvic-floor muscle training, because childbirth-related pelvic-floor weakness may improve with rehabilitation and recovery.\",\"Repeated pelvic-floor contraction increases urethral closure support and improves continence without exposing a recently postpartum patient to procedural risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Pelvic-floor muscle training is first-line conservative treatment for postpartum stress urinary incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Urgency without exertional leakage suggests a different bladder phenotype and may respond to bladder training rather than stress-incontinence surgery.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify whether leakage occurs with exertion, urgency, or both before choosing conservative therapy or ordering urodynamic testing.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum stress urinary incontinence (SUI) is managed first with supervised pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT).\",\"sl\":\"SUI conservative management is useful in women who have not completed their family, are breastfeeding or are less than six months postpartum; pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) of at least 3 months, 8 contractions 3 times per day.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[57,59]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F007\"},{\"id\":\"F457\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about antenatal corticosteroids for threatened preterm birth is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"A routine course is recommended at 20 weeks for any patient at risk of preterm birth\",\"They reduce neonatal respiratory distress syndrome\",\"They reduce intraventricular hemorrhage in preterm neonates\",\"They can reduce perinatal mortality when preterm birth is imminent\",\"Betamethasone or dexamethasone are commonly used agents\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Current guidance does not routinely offer antenatal corticosteroids at 20 weeks; benefit is established at later periviable and preterm gestations when birth is likely soon.\",\"Glucocorticoid exposure accelerates type II pneumocyte maturation and surfactant production, reducing neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.\",\"A completed course reduces intraventricular hemorrhage partly through accelerated organ maturation and reduced vulnerability of the preterm germinal matrix.\",\"When used at appropriate gestations before likely preterm birth, corticosteroids reduce neonatal death as well as major respiratory morbidity.\",\"Both betamethasone and dexamethasone cross the placenta efficiently and are standard corticosteroids for fetal maturation.\"],\"e\":[\"Antenatal corticosteroids accelerate fetal lung and multisystem maturation when preterm birth is likely within a short interval.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) offers corticosteroids at 24+0 to 33+6 weeks, discusses them at 23 weeks, and considers them at selected later preterm gestations; routine treatment at 20 weeks is not recommended.\",\"Glucocorticoids induce surfactant proteins and structural lung maturation, reducing RDS, intraventricular haemorrhage (IVH), and neonatal mortality.\"],\"tk\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids are gestation- and risk-dependent; routine administration at 20 weeks is not recommended.\",\"pr\":\"The greatest benefit occurs when birth follows sufficiently soon after treatment, so unnecessary courses should be avoided when preterm delivery is unlikely.\",\"tp\":\"Check gestational age, probability of birth within days, prior steroid exposure, and fetal growth before giving or repeating a course.\",\"cn\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids are offered at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks for threatened preterm birth, not routinely at 20 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Corticosteroids decrease respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), neonatal mortality and intraventricular haemorrhage, and should be offered at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks in suspected or diagnosed preterm labour; betamethasone or dexamethasone, intramuscular, two doses of 12 mg 24 hours apart.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[31,32]],[\"L08_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F266\"},{\"id\":\"F458\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy effects on pre-existing diabetes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following can complicate pre-existing diabetes during pregnancy except which statement?\",\"o\":[\"Easy control\",\"Retinopathy\",\"Nephropathy\",\"Increased insulin requirement\",\"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pregnancy progressively increases insulin resistance, especially in the second and third trimesters, so pre-existing diabetes is often harder rather than easier to control.\",\"Diabetic retinopathy can progress during pregnancy because pre-existing microvascular disease may worsen, particularly with severe baseline retinopathy or rapid glycemic improvement.\",\"Pre-existing diabetic nephropathy can complicate pregnancy and increases the risks of proteinuria, hypertension, and preeclampsia.\",\"Placental hormones such as human placental lactogen increase insulin resistance, often raising insulin requirements as pregnancy advances.\",\"Pregnancy increases susceptibility to DKA at lower glucose concentrations, particularly during infection, vomiting, or insulin omission.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy is a physiologic state of increasing insulin resistance, so pre-existing diabetes is not typically easier to control.\",\"Retinopathy and nephropathy may worsen or require closer monitoring, and insulin requirements often rise in later gestation.\",\"Placental diabetogenic hormones antagonize insulin action, while accelerated starvation and reduced buffering make ketoacidosis possible at lower glucose levels than outside pregnancy.\"],\"tk\":\"Pre-existing diabetes usually becomes more metabolically demanding during pregnancy rather than easier to control.\",\"pr\":\"Preconception retinal and renal assessment helps identify patients at greatest risk of microvascular deterioration during pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Review insulin needs frequently and give sick-day instructions because vomiting or infection can precipitate DKA rapidly in pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Pregnancy worsens pre-existing diabetes control: insulin requirement rises, with hypoglycaemia, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) and worsening retinopathy.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal complications of pre-existing diabetes mellitus (DM): increased insulin requirement, hypoglycaemia, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA, more with type 1), deterioration in diabetic retinopathy, increased pre-eclampsia, infection and caesarean section rate.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F458\"},{\"id\":\"F459\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Vacuum-assisted delivery contraindications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is a contraindication to vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery?\",\"o\":[\"Fully dilated cervix\",\"Ruptured membranes\",\"Engaged vertex with known position\",\"Face presentation\",\"Adequate maternal pelvis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Full cervical dilatation is a prerequisite for operative vaginal delivery and prevents cervical entrapment or laceration during traction.\",\"Operative vaginal delivery requires ruptured membranes, which provide direct access to the presenting fetal scalp or head for correct instrument placement.\",\"A known fetal head position and engaged head are prerequisites that allow correct instrument placement and safe traction.\",\"Vacuum cups require application to the fetal scalp over the flexion point; a face presentation exposes facial structures and cannot provide safe cup placement.\",\"Assessment that the pelvis is clinically adequate reduces the risk of attempting operative vaginal birth in cephalopelvic disproportion.\"],\"e\":[\"Vacuum extraction requires a vertex presentation, fully dilated cervix, ruptured membranes, engaged head, known position, and no suspected disproportion.\",\"In face presentation, the cup cannot be safely applied to the scalp flexion point and may injure facial structures.\",\"Vacuum traction works by transmitting force through the scalp to promote descent and flexion; this mechanism depends on correct cup placement on the vertex.\"],\"tk\":\"Face presentation is a contraindication to vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery.\",\"pr\":\"A failed vacuum attempt should not be followed by repeated uncontrolled traction because sequential instrument use increases neonatal trauma.\",\"tp\":\"Before applying an instrument, state the station, position, cervical dilatation, membrane status, analgesia, bladder status, and backup plan.\",\"cn\":\"Face presentation is a contraindication to vacuum-assisted delivery, which requires an engaged vertex with known position.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F459\"},{\"id\":\"F460\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Gestational diabetes risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"At booking, which history is a recognized National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) risk factor that warrants testing for gestational diabetes?\",\"o\":[\"Age younger than 20 years\",\"Gestational diabetes in a previous pregnancy\",\"Previous infant weighing 3.0 kg at term\",\"No first-degree relative with diabetes\",\"Booking body mass index (BMI) of 22 kg/m²\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Young age alone is not a National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) screening criterion; major risk factors include previous GDM, obesity, family history, prior macrosomia, and high-prevalence ethnicity.\",\"Prior gestational diabetes confers a high recurrence risk and is a specific NICE indication for early testing plus repeat testing if the first result is normal.\",\"A prior infant of 4.5 kg or more, not an uncomplicated 3.0-kg birth, is the macrosomia threshold used by NICE as a risk factor.\",\"Absence of a first-degree family history lowers one recognized risk marker; NICE considers first-degree diabetes history a positive criterion.\",\"NICE uses BMI above 30 kg/m² as a screening risk factor; a BMI of 22 kg/m² does not meet that threshold.\"],\"e\":[\"NICE uses defined clinical risk factors to identify patients who should receive diagnostic testing for gestational diabetes.\",\"A history of gestational diabetes in a previous pregnancy is particularly important because recurrence is common and prompts testing early in pregnancy, with repeat OGTT at 24–28 weeks if needed.\",\"Prior GDM reflects persistent susceptibility to insulin resistance and beta-cell insufficiency, which re-emerges as placental hormones increase insulin resistance.\"],\"tk\":\"Previous gestational diabetes is a major risk factor for recurrent GDM and warrants guideline-directed testing.\",\"pr\":\"NICE also recognizes BMI >30 kg/m², previous ≥4.5-kg infant, first-degree family history, and high-prevalence family origin as GDM risk factors.\",\"tp\":\"Do not substitute nonspecific historical events for the guideline list; document each recognized risk factor at booking and order the appropriate OGTT pathway.\",\"cn\":\"Previous gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is a recognised risk factor; offer early self-monitoring or an early oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT).\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors for gestational diabetes: body mass index (BMI) above 30, previous macrosomic baby 4.5 kg or more, previous gestational diabetes, first-degree relative with diabetes, high-prevalence ethnicity. After previous gestational diabetes offer early self-monitoring or a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) soon after booking, repeated at 24-28 weeks if normal.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[27,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F460\"},{\"id\":\"F461\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":0,\"L\":7,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Consent and pelvic examination\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Before a gynecologic examination, which action is inappropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Introduce yourself and confirm the patient's identity\",\"Explain the purpose and steps of the examination\",\"Offer an appropriate chaperone\",\"Maintain privacy while the patient changes\",\"Meet the patient only after she has already undressed and changed into an examination gown\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Clear clinician identification and patient identification establish professional communication and reduce wrong-patient errors.\",\"Valid consent requires an understandable explanation of the purpose, expected steps, and potential discomfort of the examination.\",\"An intimate examination should include an offer of a trained chaperone according to local policy and patient preference.\",\"Providing privacy and dignity during changing is a core component of respectful intimate examination practice.\",\"Initial communication should occur while the patient is appropriately clothed and comfortable; consent and explanation should precede unnecessary exposure or undressing.\"],\"e\":[\"Intimate examination begins with communication, consent, dignity, and privacy, not with physical positioning.\",\"The patient should ordinarily meet the clinician and receive an explanation before undressing, with a chaperone offered and time for questions.\",\"Respectful preparation improves informed consent and reduces loss of autonomy or unnecessary exposure during pelvic examination.\"],\"tk\":\"Gynecologic examination requires informed consent, privacy, and appropriate chaperone practice before exposure.\",\"pr\":\"Consent remains ongoing during an intimate examination; the patient can ask to pause or stop at any time.\",\"tp\":\"Explain each step immediately before performing it and keep the patient covered except for the area that must be examined.\",\"cn\":\"Before intimate examination, introduce yourself, explain, obtain verbal consent, offer a chaperone and ensure privacy before the patient undresses.\",\"sl\":\"Always explain the need and nature of the examination, obtain informed verbal consent, be accompanied by a female chaperone, examine in a private setting and keep the patient covered with only relevant parts exposed.\",\"s\":[[\"L07_0\",[40]],[\"L00_0\",[3]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F461\"},{\"id\":\"F462\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":5,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Congenital rubella\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is classically associated with congenital rubella syndrome?\",\"o\":[\"Conductive hearing loss as the characteristic deficit\",\"Neural tube defect as the defining anomaly\",\"Sensorineural hearing loss\",\"Pyloric stenosis\",\"Isolated macrosomia\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Conductive loss results from external or middle-ear transmission problems; rubella embryopathy primarily damages the sensorineural auditory apparatus.\",\"Neural tube defects arise from failed neural tube closure and are more strongly linked to folate deficiency and selected teratogens than rubella.\",\"Congenital rubella commonly causes sensorineural deafness, reflecting viral injury to the developing inner ear and auditory pathway.\",\"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is a gastric outlet disorder with projectile nonbilious vomiting and pyloric muscle hypertrophy, rather than a hallmark of the infection syndrome described.\",\"Macrosomia is associated with maternal hyperglycemia and fetal hyperinsulinemia; congenital infection more often causes growth restriction.\"],\"e\":[\"Congenital rubella syndrome classically includes sensorineural hearing loss, ocular disease such as cataracts, and congenital cardiac lesions.\",\"The source's “conductive hearing loss” wording is incorrect because rubella primarily injures the inner ear, producing a sensorineural deficit.\",\"Maternal viremia can cross the placenta during organogenesis and disrupt cell division and vascular development, causing multisystem congenital anomalies.\"],\"tk\":\"Congenital rubella classically causes sensorineural, not conductive, hearing loss.\",\"pr\":\"The fetal risk from maternal rubella infection is greatest with early gestational exposure, when organogenesis is active.\",\"tp\":\"Verify maternal rubella immunity preconception or antenatally and give postpartum MMR to nonimmune patients when appropriate because the live vaccine is avoided during pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Congenital rubella syndrome classically causes sensorineural deafness, cataracts and heart defects such as patent ductus arteriosus (PDA).\",\"sl\":\"Congenital rubella syndrome: heart defects (patent ductus arteriosus [PDA], pulmonary stenosis), eye defects (cataracts, microphthalmos, retinopathy), microcephaly and sensorineural deafness, hepatosplenomegaly, blueberry muffin rash.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F462\"},{\"id\":\"F463\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":25,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about antiphospholipid syndrome is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"It can cause venous or arterial thrombosis\",\"It is associated with recurrent pregnancy morbidity\",\"Laboratory criteria include lupus anticoagulant or anticardiolipin antibodies\",\"It is an inherited thrombophilia caused by a single germline mutation\",\"Diagnosis requires compatible clinical and laboratory findings\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"APS promotes arterial and venous thrombosis through antibody-mediated activation of endothelium, platelets, and complement.\",\"Placental thrombosis and inflammation contribute to recurrent fetal loss, placental insufficiency, and preeclampsia in obstetric APS.\",\"Persistent lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, or anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies are accepted laboratory components of APS classification.\",\"APS is an acquired autoimmune thrombophilia, defined by clinical events plus persistent antiphospholipid antibodies, rather than a single inherited mutation.\",\"APS is not diagnosed from an antibody test alone; accepted frameworks require clinical thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity plus persistent laboratory positivity.\"],\"e\":[\"Antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired autoimmune prothrombotic disorder, not an inherited thrombophilia.\",\"Obstetric manifestations include recurrent pregnancy loss, fetal growth restriction, placental insufficiency, and hypertensive disease, while nonobstetric manifestations include venous or arterial thrombosis.\",\"Antiphospholipid antibodies promote thrombosis and complement activation at the maternal-placental interface, impairing placental function.\"],\"tk\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired autoimmune thrombophilia defined by clinical events plus persistent antiphospholipid antibodies.\",\"pr\":\"Transient antibody positivity during infection is insufficient; classification requires persistent positivity on repeat testing separated in time.\",\"tp\":\"When APS is suspected after thrombosis or recurrent pregnancy morbidity, document the exact antibody tests and timing rather than labeling a single positive result as definitive.\",\"cn\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) is an acquired autoimmune thrombophilia, not an inherited single-gene disorder.\",\"sl\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome and lupus are listed as autoimmune risk factors (for stillbirth and pre-eclampsia); tests are lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I (anti-β2GP1).\",\"s\":[[\"L25_0\",[17,43]],[\"L01_0\",[32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F463\"},{\"id\":\"F464\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Follicular and luteal phases\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the normal menstrual cycle is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"The luteal phase is relatively constant at about 14 days\",\"The follicular phase is always exactly 14 days long\",\"The follicular phase varies more than the luteal phase\",\"Ovulation generally occurs about 14 days before the next menstrual period\",\"Progesterone rises after ovulation\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"After ovulation, the corpus luteum has a relatively fixed lifespan, making the luteal phase approximately 14 days in many cycles.\",\"The follicular phase is the variable portion of the menstrual cycle; its duration changes with follicular recruitment and timing of ovulation.\",\"Cycle-length variation is driven mainly by the follicular phase, which accounts for most intercycle timing variability, whereas luteal duration is comparatively stable.\",\"Because luteal duration is relatively stable, ovulation is estimated at roughly 14 days before the next menses, not always on cycle day 14.\",\"The corpus luteum secretes progesterone after ovulation, converting proliferative endometrium to a secretory pattern.\"],\"e\":[\"The follicular phase is variable, whereas the luteal phase is relatively constant at roughly 14 days.\",\"Therefore ovulation is better estimated as occurring about 14 days before the next period than as universally occurring on day 14.\",\"Follicular recruitment and estradiol production determine when the luteinising hormone (LH) surge occurs, while the postovulatory corpus luteum has a more predictable lifespan.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal cycle-length variation mainly reflects the follicular phase; it is not always exactly 14 days.\",\"pr\":\"A mid-luteal progesterone sample should be timed about 7 days before the expected next period, not automatically on day 21 for every cycle length.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about usual cycle length before timing ovulation-related investigations or fertility counseling.\",\"cn\":\"Cycle-length variation comes from the follicular phase; the luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days, the follicular phase is not.\",\"sl\":\"The estrogen (proliferative) phase is variable while the progesterone phase is fixed at 14 days; menstruation appears exactly two weeks after ovulation. Cycle length varies because of follicular phase length.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[6,11]],[\"L33_1\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F101\"},{\"id\":\"F465\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Fetal station and pelvic landmarks\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about fetal station and the maternal pelvis is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Engagement means the presenting part has reached the ischial spines\",\"Station +5 is at the ischial spines\",\"Station 0 is at the level of the maternal ischial spines\",\"The second stage ends with delivery of the placenta\",\"The obstetric conjugate is measured directly during a routine vaginal examination\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Engagement occurs when the biparietal diameter passes the pelvic inlet, which does not require the head to have reached station zero.\",\"The ischial spines are station 0; +5 represents a presenting part near the introitus/crowning.\",\"The ischial spines define station zero; negative stations lie above them and positive stations indicate descent below this midpelvic landmark.\",\"The second stage ends with birth of the fetus; placental delivery occurs during the third stage.\",\"The diagonal conjugate can be estimated clinically; the obstetric conjugate is usually inferred rather than directly measured by routine examination.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal station describes descent relative to the maternal ischial spines, which are defined as station 0.\",\"Negative values indicate the presenting part is above the spines, whereas positive values indicate progressive descent toward the outlet.\",\"The ischial spines are palpable and lie at the midpelvis, so they provide a reproducible clinical reference point for descent.\"],\"tk\":\"Station 0 corresponds to the level of the ischial spines.\",\"pr\":\"Engagement and station are related but not identical: an engaged head has passed the pelvic inlet with its biparietal diameter.\",\"tp\":\"Record both station and fetal head position before operative vaginal delivery because instrument choice depends on accurate descent and rotation assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Station 0 means the leading bony part of the fetal head is at the level of the maternal ischial spines.\",\"sl\":\"Station is the level of the denominator of the presenting part above or below the plane of the ischial spines; the ischial spines are palpable vaginally on the mid-pelvis wall and used to assess descent. Engagement is when the widest diameter has passed through the pelvic inlet.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[3,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F049\"},{\"id\":\"F466\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Variable fetal heart decelerations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about fetal assessment is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Crown-rump length is highly accurate for first-trimester dating\",\"The routine anomaly scan is performed in the mid-trimester\",\"A modified biophysical profile combines fetal heart-rate testing with amniotic-fluid assessment\",\"Early decelerations are associated with fetal head compression\",\"Variable decelerations are caused by fetal head compression\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"First-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate routine ultrasound measurement for gestational dating.\",\"Detailed structural fetal screening is performed in the mid-trimester, commonly around 18–22 weeks depending on the screening program.\",\"The modified biophysical profile (BPP) uses a non-stress test (NST)/cardiotocography (CTG) component plus assessment of amniotic fluid volume to screen short- and longer-term fetal well-being.\",\"Early decelerations mirror contractions and arise from a vagally mediated response to fetal head compression.\",\"Variable decelerations are most commonly caused by umbilical cord compression; head compression classically produces early decelerations.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal heart-rate deceleration morphology reflects different physiologic stresses.\",\"Variable decelerations are abrupt and typically result from umbilical cord compression, whereas early decelerations track contractions and reflect head compression.\",\"Cord compression transiently alters fetal arterial pressure and vagal tone, producing the characteristic abrupt fall and recovery in heart rate.\"],\"tk\":\"Variable decelerations are primarily associated with umbilical cord compression, not fetal head compression.\",\"pr\":\"Recurrent variable decelerations require assessment of their depth, duration, recovery, baseline variability, and the overall CTG context rather than interpretation in isolation.\",\"tp\":\"When variable decelerations persist, check maternal position, contraction pattern, cord-related risk, and the full tracing before escalating management.\",\"cn\":\"Variable decelerations are caused by umbilical cord compression, not fetal head compression, which causes early decelerations.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal distress in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM): variable deceleration reflecting umbilical cord compression caused by oligohydramnios.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F162\"},{\"id\":\"F467\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about pelvic organ prolapse is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Nulliparity is a major established risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse\",\"A vaginal bulge or pressure is a common symptom\",\"Parity and vaginal delivery increase risk\",\"A pessary can be used when surgery is undesirable or unsuitable\",\"Chronic raised intra-abdominal pressure can contribute\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The strongest acquired risks include parity and vaginal childbirth; nulliparity lacks the childbirth-related levator and connective-tissue injury that increases prolapse risk.\",\"Pelvic organ prolapse commonly causes a sense of vaginal bulging, heaviness, or pressure, especially when standing or straining.\",\"Pregnancy and vaginal birth can injure levator ani muscles, fascia, and pudendal innervation, producing long-term pelvic support weakness.\",\"A vaginal pessary provides mechanical vaginal support and is useful for patients who prefer to avoid surgery or are poor surgical candidates.\",\"Obesity, chronic cough, and constipation repeatedly increase intra-abdominal pressure, producing chronic pelvic-floor loading that can worsen prolapse.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic organ prolapse reflects failure of pelvic-floor muscular and connective-tissue support.\",\"The major clinical risks include parity, vaginal childbirth, age, obesity, and chronic increases in intra-abdominal pressure rather than nulliparity.\",\"Damage to levator muscles and endopelvic fascia reduces resistance to downward pressure, allowing pelvic viscera to descend toward the vaginal canal.\"],\"tk\":\"Parity and vaginal delivery are major risk factors for pelvic organ prolapse; nulliparity is not a major acquired risk factor.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms correlate imperfectly with anatomic stage; the most specific complaint is often a visible or palpable vaginal bulge.\",\"tp\":\"Offer pelvic-floor therapy and pessary care when appropriate, and base surgery on symptoms, compartment defects, sexual goals, and medical fitness.\",\"cn\":\"Parity, not nulliparity, is the strongest risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse (POP).\",\"sl\":\"Parity is the strongest risk factor; heavy lifting, obesity and chronic pulmonary disease raise abdominal pressure. Symptoms: bulge, heaviness or dragging. Management options include physiotherapy or pessary and surgery.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[8,11,18,47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F467\"},{\"id\":\"F468\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Prostaglandins in obstetrics\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about prostaglandins used in obstetrics and gynecology is false?\",\"o\":[\"Misoprostol can be used for medical management of early pregnancy loss\",\"Carboprost should generally be avoided in patients with asthma\",\"Mifepristone followed by misoprostol is effective for medical abortion\",\"Misoprostol is contraindicated solely because a patient has bronchial asthma\",\"Misoprostol can be used for cervical ripening in selected induction protocols\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Misoprostol promotes cervical ripening and uterine contraction and is used in evidence-based regimens for medical management of miscarriage.\",\"Carboprost is a PGF2α analogue that can provoke bronchospasm, so asthma is a major caution or contraindication.\",\"Mifepristone blocks progesterone receptors, and subsequent misoprostol stimulates uterine contractions, making the combination highly effective.\",\"Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analogue and does not share the clinically important bronchoconstrictor effect of carboprost; asthma alone is not its classic contraindication.\",\"Misoprostol can support cervical ripening because prostaglandin signaling promotes myometrial activity and cervical remodeling in selected induction protocols.\"],\"e\":[\"Misoprostol is a PGE1 analogue, whereas carboprost is a PGF2α analogue with a clinically important bronchoconstrictor effect.\",\"Asthma is therefore a major concern with carboprost, not a blanket contraindication to misoprostol.\",\"Prostaglandin receptor activity differs between agents, so drug selection depends on the desired uterotonic or cervical-ripening effect and patient comorbidities.\"],\"tk\":\"Asthma is classically a contraindication to carboprost, not to misoprostol.\",\"pr\":\"Misoprostol has multiple obstetric uses, but the dose and route vary substantially between miscarriage, induction, cervical preparation, and postpartum hemorrhage protocols.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing a prostaglandin, check prior uterine surgery, gestational context, asthma history, and the exact protocol for dose and route.\",\"cn\":\"Carboprost (prostaglandin F2-alpha) is avoided in asthma because of bronchospasm; misoprostol (prostaglandin E1) is used for miscarriage, abortion and induction.\",\"sl\":\"Medical management of miscarriage uses misoprostol; contraindications listed: mitral stenosis, hypertension, haemoglobinopathy, anticoagulation and asthma. Prostaglandin F2-alpha (carboprost) should be avoided in asthmatics (bronchospasm).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[15]],[\"L12_0\",[9]],[\"L09_0\",[15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: Key D is correct by international guidance: asthma is a contraindication to carboprost, not to misoprostol. However, Dr Firas's early pregnancy slide 15 lists asthma among misoprostol contraindications, so by the slides D would read as true; answer D but be aware of this slide.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F468\"},{\"id\":\"F469\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"High-risk HPV and cervical cancer\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about human papillomavirus (HPV) and cervical cancer is correct?\",\"o\":[\"HPV 6 and HPV 11 are the principal oncogenic types causing cervical carcinoma\",\"HPV vaccination treats established invasive cervical cancer\",\"HPV 16 and HPV 18 are high-risk oncogenic types strongly associated with cervical cancer\",\"Cervical cancer is most common before age 20\",\"A negative visual cervical examination excludes high-grade intraepithelial disease\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"HPV 6 and 11 are low-risk types associated mainly with genital warts rather than invasive cervical carcinoma.\",\"Prophylactic HPV vaccines prevent new infection with vaccine-covered types; they are not therapeutic cancer treatments for established invasive disease.\",\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV 16 or 18 accounts for a large proportion of cervical cancers through viral E6 and E7 oncoprotein activity.\",\"Invasive cervical cancer is rare in adolescents because progression from persistent HPV infection to malignancy usually takes years.\",\"High-grade cervical lesions may be microscopic or endocervical, so screening and colposcopic pathways cannot be replaced by visual inspection alone.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV, especially types 16 and 18, is the central causal pathway for most cervical cancers.\",\"Low-risk HPV 6 and 11 are primarily linked to anogenital warts, not the oncogenic phenotype tested here.\",\"Viral E6 and E7 proteins disrupt p53 and retinoblastoma tumor-suppressor pathways, allowing genomic instability and progression from dysplasia to carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"HPV 16 and 18 are major high-risk oncogenic types associated with cervical cancer.\",\"pr\":\"HPV vaccination is most effective before exposure but can still provide future type-specific protection according to age and program eligibility.\",\"tp\":\"Use vaccination and guideline-based screening together; vaccination does not remove the need for screening in eligible patients.\",\"cn\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) 16 and 18 are high-risk oncogenic types strongly linked to cervical cancer.\",\"sl\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is the primary factor: HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 45; 50% are caused by HPV 16 and 18. Cervical cancer rarely occurs in women younger than 20.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F469\"},{\"id\":\"F470\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Premature ovarian insufficiency management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about premature ovarian insufficiency is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Hormone replacement is usually continued until around the natural age of menopause if not contraindicated\",\"Premature ovarian insufficiency is managed by immediate surgery in all patients\",\"A combined hormonal contraceptive can be an alternative when contraception is desired\",\"Untreated premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) increases the risk of low bone density\",\"Conventional hormone replacement therapy (HRT) does not provide reliable contraception\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Sex-steroid replacement is generally continued until the average natural menopause age to reduce symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health.\",\"POI is primarily a hormone-deficiency disorder managed with sex-steroid replacement and cause-specific care; routine immediate surgery has no general therapeutic role.\",\"A combined hormonal contraceptive can provide estrogen replacement plus contraception in suitable patients without contraindications.\",\"Chronic estrogen deficiency accelerates bone resorption, increasing the risk of osteopenia, osteoporosis, and fracture.\",\"Standard menopausal HRT is not contraceptive; intermittent spontaneous ovulation can still occur in POI.\"],\"e\":[\"Premature ovarian insufficiency causes estrogen deficiency before age 40 and is usually managed with sex-steroid replacement, not surgery.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends HRT or a combined hormonal contraceptive unless contraindicated, generally continued until at least the age of natural menopause.\",\"Estrogen replacement limits vasomotor symptoms and reduces adverse effects of prolonged hypoestrogenism on bone and cardiovascular health.\"],\"tk\":\"Premature ovarian insufficiency is treated medically with sex-steroid replacement; routine immediate surgery is inappropriate.\",\"pr\":\"Spontaneous ovulation can occasionally occur in POI, so patients who do not desire pregnancy still need effective contraception.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss fertility goals, bone health, cardiovascular risk, and whether HRT or combined contraception better fits the patient's needs.\",\"cn\":\"Premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) is treated medically with hormone replacement until about the natural age of menopause, not surgery.\",\"sl\":\"All patients with premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) should have hormone replacement therapy (HRT) or combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP) until at least age 51; untreated POI increases osteoporosis risk. HRT is not contraception.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_1\",[5,7,13]],[\"L34_0\",[57]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F470\"},{\"id\":\"F471\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal circulation oxygen streaming\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about normal fetal circulation is correct?\",\"o\":[\"The umbilical arteries carry the most oxygenated fetal blood\",\"The ductus arteriosus connects the left atrium directly to the aorta\",\"The right atrium contains uniformly the most oxygenated blood in the fetus\",\"Highly oxygenated umbilical venous blood is preferentially streamed through the foramen ovale toward the left atrium\",\"The ductus venosus shunts blood from the pulmonary artery to the aorta\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The umbilical arteries carry relatively deoxygenated fetal blood from the fetus to the placenta for gas exchange.\",\"The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance fetal pulmonary circulation.\",\"The right atrium receives mixed venous streams; preferential streaming directs the most oxygenated inferior vena cava (IVC) blood across the foramen ovale, so it is not uniformly the most oxygenated chamber.\",\"Oxygen-rich blood from the umbilical vein passes through the ductus venosus and inferior vena cava, then is preferentially directed across the foramen ovale into the left atrium.\",\"The ductus venosus creates a hepatic bypass pathway, directing oxygen-rich umbilical venous blood toward the inferior vena cava.\"],\"e\":[\"The placenta is the organ of fetal gas exchange, not the fetal lungs.\",\"Oxygen-rich blood returns through the umbilical vein, traverses the ductus venosus, and is preferentially streamed across the foramen ovale into the left atrium to supply the heart and brain.\",\"Parallel fetal shunts—the ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus—direct flow around the liver and high-resistance pulmonary circulation.\"],\"tk\":\"The most oxygenated fetal blood is preferentially directed across the foramen ovale toward the left heart.\",\"pr\":\"After birth, falling pulmonary vascular resistance and rising left-atrial pressure promote functional closure of the foramen ovale.\",\"tp\":\"When interpreting congenital heart physiology, track where each fetal shunt connects rather than memorizing chamber oxygenation in isolation.\",\"cn\":\"Oxygenated umbilical venous blood passes the ductus venosus and is streamed through the foramen ovale to the left atrium.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F471\"},{\"id\":\"F472\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"wateen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Ursodeoxycholic acid reliably prevents stillbirth\",\"Serum creatinine normally rises substantially in healthy pregnancy\",\"A primary inflammatory skin eruption is required for diagnosis\",\"Persistent hypernatremia is the characteristic biochemical finding\",\"It typically causes pruritus without a primary rash and elevated serum bile acids\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ursodeoxycholic acid may improve symptoms and selected outcomes, but Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) states it does not prevent stillbirth; fetal risk is linked mainly to bile-acid severity.\",\"Glomerular filtration increases in pregnancy, so serum creatinine normally falls relative to nonpregnant values.\",\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) produces itch without a primary rash; excoriations may appear secondarily from scratching.\",\"ICP is diagnosed through bile-acid elevation and liver-test abnormalities, not a characteristic hypernatremic pattern.\",\"This cholestatic liver disorder of pregnancy classically causes pruritus without a primary rash together with elevated serum bile acids and sometimes transaminitis.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents with pruritus without a primary rash and elevated serum bile acids.\",\"Fetal risk rises with higher bile-acid concentrations, while ursodeoxycholic acid may reduce symptoms or selected outcomes but does not reliably prevent stillbirth.\",\"Impaired hepatobiliary transport causes maternal accumulation of bile acids, which explains both pruritus and the laboratory abnormality used for diagnosis.\"],\"tk\":\"ICP is defined by pregnancy-associated pruritus without a primary rash plus elevated bile acids after alternative causes are excluded.\",\"pr\":\"RCOG emphasizes that only severe ICP clearly carries a higher stillbirth risk, so bile-acid severity influences delivery planning.\",\"tp\":\"Repeat bile acids and liver tests when symptoms persist, review alternative liver disease, and plan timing of birth using bile-acid level plus obstetric risk factors.\",\"cn\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents with pruritus without a rash (palms and soles) and raised bile acids and liver enzymes.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis: pruritus without skin rash other than excoriations (especially palms and soles), worse at night, with raised liver function tests (LFTs) and bile acids. Ursodeoxycholic acid reduces LFTs and pruritus but there is no evidence for reduction in fetal risk.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[17,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F472\"},{\"id\":\"F473\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":25,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum anti-D prophylaxis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"For an unsensitized RhD-negative woman who delivers an RhD-positive infant, postpartum anti-D immunoglobulin should ideally be administered within what interval?\",\"o\":[\"Within 72 hours of delivery\",\"Within 24 hours only\",\"Within 48 hours only\",\"At 7 days postpartum\",\"At 6 weeks postpartum\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Postpartum anti-D immunoglobulin is most effective when given within 72 hours after delivery of an RhD-positive infant to reduce maternal alloimmunization.\",\"A 24-hour limit is unnecessarily restrictive; prophylaxis remains indicated beyond the first day when the mother is unsensitized and the infant is RhD-positive.\",\"A 48-hour limit is also too narrow because standard postpartum prophylaxis is recommended through the 72-hour window.\",\"Waiting a full week risks avoidable sensitization because fetal erythrocytes may already have triggered a maternal immune response.\",\"A 6-week delay is far beyond the routine postpartum prophylaxis interval and would miss the period of greatest preventive benefit.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal exposure to RhD-positive fetal erythrocytes can trigger formation of anti-D antibodies that endanger future pregnancies.\",\"Giving anti-D immunoglobulin within 72 hours after birth suppresses active maternal sensitization when the newborn is RhD-positive and the mother is not already sensitized.\",\"Passive anti-D promotes clearance of fetal RhD-positive cells before the maternal immune system develops durable anti-D memory.\"],\"tk\":\"Postpartum anti-D prophylaxis should be given within 72 hours after delivery of an RhD-positive infant to an unsensitized RhD-negative mother.\",\"pr\":\"The postpartum dose may need adjustment after a large fetomaternal hemorrhage, assessed with an appropriate quantification test.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm the mother's antibody status and the newborn's rhesus D (RhD) type before discharge so indicated prophylaxis is not omitted.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum anti-D immunoglobulin for a non-sensitised RhD-negative woman with an RhD-positive baby is given within 72 hours of delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Anti-D: RhD-negative mother, fetus RhD-positive or unknown; give within 72 h of the sensitising event; Kleihauer test before and after birth guides the dose.\",\"s\":[[\"L25_0\",[52]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F473\"},{\"id\":\"F474\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Chlamydia diagnostic testing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which test is currently preferred for diagnosing genital Chlamydia trachomatis infection?\",\"o\":[\"Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)\",\"Culture on susceptible cell lines\",\"Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT)\",\"Gram stain\",\"Serologic antibody testing\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antigen immunoassays have lower sensitivity than modern molecular assays and are no longer preferred for routine genital chlamydia diagnosis.\",\"Cell culture is highly specific and remains useful in selected medicolegal or special settings, but it is less sensitive and less practical for routine urogenital testing.\",\"Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) provide the highest routine diagnostic sensitivity and are the recommended tests for genital C. trachomatis infection.\",\"Gram stain cannot reliably visualize intracellular chlamydial organisms and therefore cannot establish the diagnosis.\",\"Serology reflects systemic antibody responses and lacks sufficient precision for diagnosing an active uncomplicated genital infection.\"],\"e\":[\"Modern diagnosis of genital chlamydia relies on nucleic acid amplification testing because it is highly sensitive and specific.\",\"For routine genital detection, NAAT is preferred to culture or antigen testing under current Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) guidance.\",\"Molecular amplification detects pathogen nucleic acid directly, allowing accurate diagnosis from vaginal swabs or first-catch urine without requiring viable organisms.\"],\"tk\":\"Use NAAT as the preferred routine diagnostic test for genital chlamydial infection.\",\"pr\":\"For women, a self- or clinician-collected vaginal swab is an optimal specimen for chlamydia NAAT.\",\"tp\":\"Test at the anatomic sites of sexual exposure; urine testing alone can miss some extragenital infections.\",\"cn\":\"Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is the preferred test for genital Chlamydia trachomatis infection.\",\"sl\":\"The current standard of care for all cases is nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT): vulvovaginal swabs are the specimen of choice in women, then endocervical swabs, first-catch urine, urethral swabs.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F394\"},{\"id\":\"F475\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse symptoms\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is NOT a typical symptom of pelvic organ prolapse?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal lump\",\"Backache\",\"Reducible mass after defecation\",\"Urgency\",\"Complete bowel obstruction\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A vaginal bulge or lump is the characteristic patient-reported symptom of pelvic organ prolapse, often worsening with standing or straining.\",\"Low back or pelvic pressure can accompany prolapse because pelvic support defects produce traction and heaviness symptoms.\",\"Posterior compartment prolapse may cause a bulge that becomes more evident with defecation and may require manual reduction or splinting.\",\"Urinary urgency can coexist with anterior or apical prolapse because altered bladder outlet and support may produce lower urinary tract symptoms.\",\"Complete bowel obstruction is not a usual manifestation of pelvic organ prolapse; bowel symptoms are more commonly incomplete emptying, splinting, or obstructed defecation.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic organ prolapse commonly causes a vaginal bulge, pressure, or heaviness and may produce urinary or defecatory dysfunction.\",\"A reducible vaginal mass, backache, and urgency can occur with support defects, whereas complete mechanical bowel obstruction is not a typical presentation.\",\"Descent of the anterior, apical, or posterior vaginal compartments alters pelvic support and organ emptying without usually creating a fixed intestinal blockage.\"],\"tk\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse most characteristically causes vaginal bulge and pressure symptoms, sometimes with urinary or defecatory dysfunction.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms correlate imperfectly with anatomic stage; the sensation of a bulge has the strongest relationship with prolapse extending to or beyond the hymen.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about splinting, incomplete bladder emptying, and whether symptoms worsen late in the day or with prolonged standing.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) causes bulge, backache, urinary and defecatory symptoms but not complete bowel obstruction.\",\"sl\":\"Symptoms: bulge, heaviness or dragging; backache; need to push the prolapse back after defecation. Urinary: frequency and urgency, stress incontinence, voiding dysfunction. Rectocele: obstructed defecation, incomplete emptying, constipation.\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[18,19,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F475\"},{\"id\":\"F476\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cycle-adjusted pregnancy dating\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman reports a last menstrual period (LMP) of March 3, 2024 and regular 24-day cycles. After adjusting the standard 280-day estimate for her shorter cycle, what is the estimated due date?\",\"o\":[\"December 10, 2024\",\"December 4, 2024\",\"November 29, 2024\",\"December 17, 2024\",\"January 7, 2025\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"December 10 reflects a simplified calendar application of Naegele's rule without accounting accurately for the shorter 24-day cycle.\",\"A 24-day cycle is 4 days shorter than 28 days, so the 280-day LMP estimate is reduced by 4 days, giving December 4, 2024.\",\"November 29 subtracts substantially more than the 4-day cycle-length adjustment and would underestimate gestational duration.\",\"December 17 shifts the due date later despite the shorter cycle, which implies later rather than earlier ovulation.\",\"January 7 is too late for an LMP in early March and would correspond to a gestation well beyond the conventional 40-week estimate.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy dating from a reliable LMP uses an estimate of 280 days from the first day of the LMP for a 28-day cycle.\",\"With a 24-day cycle, expected ovulation occurs about 4 days earlier, so the date is adjusted 4 days earlier to December 4, 2024.\",\"Cycle-length adjustment changes the assumed timing of ovulation; a shorter cycle implies earlier ovulation and an earlier estimated date of delivery (EDD).\"],\"tk\":\"Adjust an LMP-based EDD for cycle length when cycles are consistently shorter or longer than 28 days.\",\"pr\":\"First-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate ultrasound method for establishing or confirming gestational age.\",\"tp\":\"Document whether the LMP is certain and whether cycles are regular before relying on menstrual dating.\",\"cn\":\"Due date is 280 days after the last menstrual period (LMP) for 28-day cycles, moved earlier by the days cycles are shorter.\",\"sl\":\"LMP (last menstrual period) to EDD (estimated date of delivery) is 280 days / 40 weeks / 9 months and 7 days; Naegele's rule: add 7 days, subtract 3 months, add 1 year.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F476\"},{\"id\":\"F477\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Chronic pelvic pain differential\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is NOT a typical cause of chronic pelvic pain lasting 6 months or longer?\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic inflammatory disease\",\"Psychological and trauma-related factors\",\"Irritable bowel syndrome\",\"Ectopic pregnancy\",\"Bladder pain syndrome\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Prior pelvic inflammatory disease can lead to adhesions, hydrosalpinx, and persistent pelvic pain after the acute infection has resolved.\",\"Psychological distress and a history of trauma can coexist with chronic pelvic pain and may amplify pain processing without implying that symptoms are psychogenic.\",\"Irritable bowel syndrome is a frequent gastrointestinal contributor to chronic pelvic pain, particularly when pain is associated with altered bowel habits.\",\"An ectopic pregnancy usually presents as an acute or subacute pregnancy-related emergency rather than a persistent pain syndrome lasting at least 6 months.\",\"Bladder pain syndrome produces chronic suprapubic pain related to bladder filling and is an important urologic differential diagnosis.\"],\"e\":[\"Chronic pelvic pain is typically defined as pelvic pain persisting for at least 6 months and often has gynecologic, gastrointestinal, urologic, and psychosocial contributors.\",\"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) sequelae, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and bladder pain syndrome can cause persistent symptoms, whereas ectopic pregnancy is usually an acute pregnancy complication.\",\"Chronic pain frequently reflects overlapping peripheral pathology and central pain sensitization, which is why multidisciplinary assessment can be necessary.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate chronic pelvic pain disorders from acute pregnancy emergencies such as ectopic pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Endometriosis and IBS commonly coexist, so a single positive diagnosis should not end evaluation when symptoms span more than one organ system.\",\"tp\":\"Always obtain a pregnancy test in reproductive-age patients with new pelvic pain, even when chronic pain is already documented.\",\"cn\":\"Chronic pelvic pain arises from pelvic inflammatory disease, irritable bowel syndrome, bladder pain syndrome or psychosocial factors; ectopic pregnancy is acute.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F477\"},{\"id\":\"F478\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Imaging for structural AUB\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"When a structural PALM cause of abnormal uterine bleeding is suspected, what is the usual first-line pelvic imaging study?\",\"o\":[\"Transvaginal ultrasound, with transabdominal imaging when needed\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\",\"Diagnostic hysteroscopy as the initial test in every patient\",\"Empiric hormonal therapy without evaluation\",\"Endometrial biopsy as the primary imaging test\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Correct. Transvaginal ultrasound, with transabdominal views when needed, is the first-line imaging study for suspected polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma or malignancy and hyperplasia (PALM) causes of abnormal uterine bleeding.\",\"Wrong. Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is reserved for cases where ultrasound is inconclusive or detailed mapping is needed, for example before surgery.\",\"Wrong. Diagnostic hysteroscopy is useful for focal intracavitary lesions but is not the initial test in every patient.\",\"Wrong. Empiric hormonal therapy without evaluation is not an imaging study and risks missing structural disease or malignancy.\",\"Wrong. Endometrial biopsy is a histological sampling procedure for hyperplasia or cancer, not an imaging test.\"],\"e\":[\"The PALM causes of abnormal uterine bleeding are polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, and malignancy/hyperplasia.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound is generally the initial imaging study because it evaluates the endometrium, myometrium, adnexa, and uterine contour efficiently.\",\"Hysteroscopy or MRI is added selectively when ultrasound suggests a focal cavity lesion or when anatomic definition remains inadequate.\"],\"tk\":\"Use transvaginal ultrasound as first-line imaging when structural AUB is suspected.\",\"pr\":\"Saline infusion sonography can improve detection of intracavitary polyps and submucosal fibroids when standard ultrasound is equivocal.\",\"tp\":\"Choose endometrial sampling based on age and malignancy risk, not merely because bleeding is heavy.\",\"cn\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) is the first-line imaging for suspected structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB).\",\"sl\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound scan (TVS USS) is good to identify fibroids, adenomyosis and polyps and to measure endometrial thickness.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[56]],[\"L33_1\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F478\"},{\"id\":\"F479\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cephalopelvic disproportion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following can support a diagnosis of cephalopelvic disproportion during labor EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Slow cervical dilatation despite adequate contractions\",\"Severe caput and moulding\",\"A well-descended fetal head with continued normal progress\",\"Arrest of fetal descent\",\"Persistent lack of progress despite adequate uterine activity\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Slow dilation despite adequate uterine activity raises concern for mechanical dystocia after power abnormalities have been excluded.\",\"Marked caput and moulding can develop when the fetal head repeatedly meets resistance at the maternal pelvis.\",\"A well-descended head with ongoing normal progress indicates effective passage through the pelvis and argues against clinically significant disproportion.\",\"Arrest of descent during the second stage is a classic manifestation of a mismatch between the presenting part and the birth canal.\",\"Persistent labor arrest despite adequate contractions suggests an obstructive component rather than simply insufficient uterine power.\"],\"e\":[\"Cephalopelvic disproportion is suspected when labor fails to progress despite adequate contractions and no correctable malposition or power problem explains the arrest.\",\"Severe caput, moulding, failure of descent, and persistent labor arrest strengthen the concern; normal descent and continued progress do not.\",\"Mechanical mismatch prevents the presenting diameter from traversing the pelvis, leading to increasing fetal-head deformation without effective descent.\"],\"tk\":\"Suspect cephalopelvic disproportion when adequate contractions fail to produce cervical change or fetal descent.\",\"pr\":\"The diagnosis is clinical and dynamic; antenatal pelvimetry poorly predicts who will achieve vaginal birth.\",\"tp\":\"Before diagnosing CPD, reassess fetal position, station, bladder distension, contraction adequacy, and whether active-phase or second-stage criteria for arrest are actually met.\",\"cn\":\"Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD) is suggested by poor progress despite adequate contractions, arrest of descent and severe caput and moulding.\",\"sl\":\"Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD) is anatomical disproportion between the fetal head and maternal pelvis and cannot be diagnosed definitively except after a failed trial of labour. Moulding (overriding sutures) and caput result from pressure in a tight birth canal.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[9,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F479\"},{\"id\":\"F480\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Cesarean uterine incisions\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about uterine incisions at cesarean delivery is correct?\",\"o\":[\"A lower-segment tran";